it could be done at home.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:31.260
But three days strength training,
Monday, Wednesday, Friday.

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:33.360
Conditioning, Tuesday,
Thursday, you know, two days.

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:36.720
It's a pretty easy roundabout
way to split that up,

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.760
of course, depending upon training goals.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:40.650
And as you said, the
aesthetic goals like that

00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:42.150
will shift dramatically.

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:44.646
But if you want to see
the benefits of both,

00:10:44.646 --> 00:10:48.120
that's probably the effect
of dose for strength training

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:49.860
and the effect of dose for conditioning

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:51.030
at the bare minimum level.

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:54.030
Again, being a much better
performer conditioning wise,

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:55.380
you're going to want to do more than that.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:58.170
- And in terms of the
duration of those workouts,

00:10:58.170 --> 00:10:59.340
what's your suggestion?

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:01.440
I've been weight training
for about 30 years,

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:06.390
running for about 30 years,
and mainly for health.

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:10.500
And have found that if
I work hard in the gym

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:14.010
or at resistance training for
more than 60 minutes or so,

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:16.350
it's very hard for me to
recover, I start getting cold,

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:18.450
I start getting weaker
from workout to workout,

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:20.280
but amazingly, at least to me,

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:23.550
if I keep those workouts to
about 10 minutes of warmup

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:28.050
and 55 minutes or so of really hard work

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:29.199
for resistance training,

00:11:29.199 --> 00:11:31.170
and I keep the cardiovascular work

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:34.887
to about 30 or 45 minutes, I feel great.

00:11:34.887 --> 00:11:37.080
And I seem to make some progress,

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:39.870
at least someplace in the
workout from workout to workout.

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:42.180
- Yeah, I mean, those are good numbers,

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:43.800
'cause those are kind of
numbers that we usually preach.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:46.710
We try to keep our workouts to
an hour or less if possible.

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:49.170
Now, depending upon the
split that you're following,

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:50.970
if you're on a total body split,

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:53.070
there's just going to be
more that has to be done

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:54.210
in a given amount of time.

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:57.420
And again if you're training
primarily for strength,

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:58.946
that could prolong the workout,

00:11:58.946 --> 00:12:00.810
'cause of the longer rest
time is in between sets.

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:03.780
But in general, when you're
not focused on that one aspect,

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:05.130
but the overall health picture,

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:08.850
then you can get the job
done in under an hour.

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:10.897
And again, I always say, on top of

00:12:10.897 --> 00:12:12.930
"If you want to look like an
athlete train, like an athlete,"

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:14.667
is, "You could either train
longer or you could train hard,

00:12:14.667 --> 00:12:16.050
but you can't do both."

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:18.840
And I really believe
that the focus for me,

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:19.999
I have a busy life,

00:12:19.999 --> 00:12:21.840
I have a lot of other things
that I do believe it or not.

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:25.020
And it's like, I want to go
hard and I want to go get out.

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:27.567
And I find that my body
also responds to that,

00:12:27.567 --> 00:12:29.610
and I think a lot of guys'
body respond to that.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:32.178
And particularly as
you start to get older,

00:12:32.178 --> 00:12:35.404
I think it's the length of the workout

00:12:35.404 --> 00:12:37.890
that actually causes more problems

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:39.960
than the intensity of what you're doing.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.420
Particularly if you're warmed
up properly like you said.

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:44.370
I've found personally that my warmup

00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:46.830
has had to become more of an
integral part of my workout

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:48.330
than it ever has before.

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:51.060
I could get in the gym when I was 20,

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:53.779
and I'm going right over, I'm
doing the one set, two sets,

00:12:53.779 --> 00:12:55.189
I'm ready to go.

00:12:55.189 --> 00:12:57.150
You know, and I never do
another workout warmup set

00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:59.268
for any of the other exercise
I do the rest of the day.

00:12:59.268 --> 00:13:02.070
That's not true anymore.

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:03.930
And I found that as long as I'm willing

00:13:03.930 --> 00:13:05.929
to sort of give myself a
little bit of a warmup,

00:13:05.929 --> 00:13:08.400
the intensity is not what bothers me.

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:10.647
I'm very much in control
of the weights that I use

00:13:10.647 --> 00:13:12.270
and it doesn't bother me.

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:14.268
But if I start to go pretty long,

00:13:14.268 --> 00:13:16.650
I start to feel achy or
I start to have problems.

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:18.300
So again, depending upon age,

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:20.640
that also plays a factor in the length.

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:23.580
But again, I think everybody can achieve,

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:25.260
on a standard program,

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:28.080
can achieve the results that
they want within an hour.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.090
- In terms of splits,
you mentioned splits.

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:32.940
And so for those who aren't
familiar with this term splits,

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:35.460
it's really which body
parts are you training on?

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:36.630
Which days?

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:39.360
Seems like almost
everybody follows a weekly

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:40.320
workout schedule.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:42.840
Although the body of course
doesn't care about the week.

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:44.730
There's no reason thing
that once every seven days

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:46.830
or twice every seven days
makes sense physiologically,

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:48.003
it's just the body doesn't work that.

00:13:48.003 --> 00:13:49.953
But, that's the way life is structured.

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:54.750
I've seen you discuss three days a week,

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:57.030
whole body workouts.

00:13:57.030 --> 00:13:59.978
I've heard of splits
like a pushing one day,

00:13:59.978 --> 00:14:03.690
pulling another day, legs
another day, a day off, repeat.

00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:05.790
I mean, there's so many
variations on this.

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:08.040
What are some general themes
that we can throw out there?

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:11.790
And in order to avoid the
huge matrix of possibilities,

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:13.410
you have some wonderful
content that points those.

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:14.250
And we will cap-

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:17.370
In our caption show notes,
we'll link out to some of those

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:19.440
that different ways to design splits.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:21.660
But in terms of giving people a logic

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:24.438
of how to think about
splitting up body parts,

00:14:24.438 --> 00:14:26.970
what's governing the split?

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:30.060
What are the rules and the
logic that dictate a split?

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:32.970
- For me, the first rule
is will you stick to it?

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:34.230
Like if you, 'cause there are split,

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:36.960
I don't particularly
like full body splits.

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:38.257
And I was actually talking to Jesse

00:14:38.257 --> 00:14:39.090
about that the other day like,

00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:42.390
I don't necessarily like to
have to train everything.

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:45.540
Now of course the volumes will
come down per muscle group.

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:48.057
But if you don't like to do that,

00:14:48.057 --> 00:14:50.280
and you actually don't look
forward to your workout

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:52.140
because you're dreading
having to do everything

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:53.940
and feeling maybe too fatigued

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:55.320
by the time your workout's over,

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:57.720
or the fact that those generally
do take a little bit longer

00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:59.130
and don't fit into your schedule.

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:01.440
I don't care how effective the split is,

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.330
a split not done is not effective.

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:04.950
So you need to find one that fits.

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:07.470
So maybe you go into
an alternative option,

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:09.780
like a push-pull-legs, like you mentioned.

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:13.860
And that could be done either
one cycle through the week,

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:15.330
on a Monday, Wednesday, Friday split,

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:17.190
or it could be twice in a week.

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:19.004
So you're actually training six times,

00:15:19.004 --> 00:15:22.380
where you repeat it,
pull-push-legs, pull-push-legs,

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:24.810
or however you want to do it

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:26.970
with either a day off in
between the three days,

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:29.250
or at the end of the six days.

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:31.350
And again, that actually
impacts your schedule.

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:33.180
I've broken that down before where it's,

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:35.460
if you put it in between the three days,

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:37.530
it's good because you're giving
yourself an extra rest day

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:40.440
in between, but it starts
to shift that day off

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:41.850
every week as we wrap around.

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:43.020
So for those guys that we're choosing

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:45.530
that seven day schedule out
of convenience in our heads,

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:47.460
it starts to mess with that off day.

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:49.170
So others like to just
keep it predictably,

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:51.813
let's say on a Sunday, and
train six days in a row.

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:56.925
But that's a better way
to maybe group similar

00:15:56.925 --> 00:16:00.000
muscle actions together, which I think,

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.530
I definitely prefer that,

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:05.010
because if I'm going to be
training pulling movements,

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:07.140
at least there there's
a synergy between them.

00:16:07.140 --> 00:16:11.299
And I feel like I'm looking
to achieve one goal that day.

00:16:11.299 --> 00:16:13.980
And then, I mean, quite honestly,

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:16.080
you can go back to the bro-split days,

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:18.330
and those still work effectively.

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:20.520
There's a reason why
they worked in the past.

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:23.700
I think that science shows
that there's smarter ways

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:24.840
to do them these days.

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:27.810
Like you can come back
and hit a related muscle,

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:30.450
so you could do, let's
say biceps on one day

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:32.910
and then come back two
days later and do back,

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:36.750
realizing again, synergy
between the exercises there,

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:38.820
your biceps are going to
get re-stimulated again.

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:41.040
So you could figure out
ways to make that work,

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:43.110
but the thing that I
think is effective there,

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:44.220
is that tends to be one of the ones

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:45.690
that people like the most.

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:48.481
Because they can go in, they
get their pump, they feel good.

00:16:48.481 --> 00:16:51.810
It's pretty solely focused
on one muscle group.

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:53.580
- Is that the definition of a bro-split?

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:55.350
One...
- One muscle group a day?

00:16:55.350 --> 00:16:57.300
- Yeah, I see, so it's
very much geared towards

00:16:57.300 --> 00:16:59.627
strength and aesthetics
really maximizing...

00:16:59.627 --> 00:17:03.060
- Probably more aesthetics
than strength, yeah.

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:06.000
You're just...
- Hence the bro, the bro name.

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:08.010
- But again, like, you know,
in here I am a science guy

00:17:08.010 --> 00:17:10.560
and I could appreciate the
benefits of a bro-split.

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:13.261
Especially 'cause again, like to what end?

00:17:13.261 --> 00:17:16.680
Who's goal are we trying to achieve here?

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:18.330
Those are ours.

00:17:18.330 --> 00:17:22.200
Like if I'm applying my
standards and my goals

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:24.570
or even like athletic ideals,

00:17:24.570 --> 00:17:26.370
but they just want to get in shape,

00:17:26.370 --> 00:17:30.540
then it's perfectly fine to do
a bro split in that instance,

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:32.310
if you're sticking to it again

00:17:32.310 --> 00:17:34.590
and you're seeing the results
that you want to see from it.

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:38.670
But they're able to really
keep their focus on one muscle,

00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:40.051
they get to focus on.

00:17:40.051 --> 00:17:42.151
Look, a lot of times people struggle with

00:17:42.151 --> 00:17:44.640
the way of an exercise feels

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:46.140
until their second or third set.

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:48.150
Like they don't have that
proper percept of ability

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:49.800
to kind of lock in on an exercise.

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:54.480
So spending a few, not only
sets in the same exercise,

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:56.490
but then doing another exercise
for the same muscle group

00:17:56.490 --> 00:17:57.870
helps them to dial in a little bit better

00:17:57.870 --> 00:17:59.400
and get more out of their training.

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:01.320
- Yeah, that raised a really interesting,

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:02.430
I think important question.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:04.710
Early on when I started
resistance training,

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:06.900
which was when I was 16 in high school,

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:10.680
I got in touch with and I was
learning from Mike Menzer.

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:13.620
- Me too, that's crazy.

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:15.120
- And Mike was very helpful.

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:15.953
Very, very helpful.

00:18:15.953 --> 00:18:16.786
We got to be friendly...

00:18:16.786 --> 00:18:17.760
- So, I just read his book,

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:19.770
I didn't get a chance to be
him, so I'm jealous, right?

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:21.873
- Well, back then no internet,

00:18:22.739 --> 00:18:26.430
I paid by Western Union type
thing to send him some money

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:27.263
- From the back of the magazine.

00:18:27.263 --> 00:18:29.010
- And then he got on the phone with me

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:30.240
and my mother at the time was like,

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:32.490
why is this grown man calling the house?

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:34.590
And he gave me a very
straightforward split,

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:36.360
which was shoulders, arms one day,

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:39.180
he had me taking two days
off and then training legs

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:41.730
and then two days off and then
chest and back, et cetera.

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:43.470
- And that's a variation
of a bro-split too.

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:45.360
Where you're sort of
breaking them down that way,

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:47.387
chest them back or chest
them bis, you know?

00:18:47.387 --> 00:18:49.110
- And it worked very well for me,

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:51.120
I probably would've,
because of my age I think,

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:54.240
and because I was untrained,
I think, largely untrained,

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:57.060
I think it would've grown
on many different programs,

00:18:57.060 --> 00:18:58.440
but it worked very well for me.

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:01.500
I eventually just made that
in every other day thing.

00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:04.080
Shoulders and arms day
off, legs day or two off,

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:06.115
'cause if you hit legs
right, at least for me,

00:19:06.115 --> 00:19:08.003
I'm not training the next day.

00:19:08.003 --> 00:19:10.800
And then I'm not doing
much of anything athletic

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:13.260
the next day, and chest,
back and repeat and so on.

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:15.300
And the reason I found that helpful

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:18.655
is I almost always
recovered between workouts.

00:19:18.655 --> 00:19:22.320
The six day a week
program of push-pull-legs,

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:25.270
push-pull-legs, to me seems excruciating

00:19:25.270 --> 00:19:27.150
from two standpoints.

00:19:27.150 --> 00:19:30.030
One is, at least with
my recovery abilities,

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:31.380
or lack of recovery abilities,

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:33.660
I can't imagine coming back feeling fresh.

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:34.920
And the other one is,

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:37.320
if I'm in the gym more
than four days a week,

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:39.270
I really start to fatigue it

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:41.580
about the whole psychological
experience of it.

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:43.980
Whereas if I'm in there
three or four days a week.

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:46.560
In other words, if I put a day
off in between each workout,

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:48.120
I really want to be there.

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:50.735
And I get in there with a lot of fire.

00:19:50.735 --> 00:19:52.950
And I'm also doing other
things on the off days.

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:55.230
So I think that, I love that you mentioned

00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:56.460
the split that you'll stick to.

00:19:56.460 --> 00:19:59.220
And that you can bring the intensity to,

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:00.920
because I think that
that's really important.

00:20:00.920 --> 00:20:03.420
I sometimes hear about two a day training.

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:05.460
I've done two a day training
twice in my lifetime,

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.625
both times I got sick two days later.

00:20:07.625 --> 00:20:10.560
That's correlation, not causation.

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:14.340
But is there ever an
instance where two a day

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:16.110
weight training makes sense

00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:21.110
for the non-drug assisted,
typical recovery ability person?

00:20:21.510 --> 00:20:24.720
- Actually, I think it makes
sense in some scenarios,

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:26.280
but it doesn't make sense practically

00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:27.420
for a lot of people's schedules.

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:29.610
So like if you could break down,

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:31.210
let's say you were going to do even

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:35.220
some version of a total body session,

00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:38.400
or maybe like you're going to
do an upper lower split, right?

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:42.540
You could do an upper workout
and do the anterior chain

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:45.630
or the pushing portion
of that in one session

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:48.750
and then come back and do
the pulling session later on

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:51.150
at night, if you had the opportunity to.

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:52.370
The thing that you benefit from there

00:20:52.370 --> 00:20:54.060
is the freshness of focus.

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:57.870
Again, like something
in my head is sacrificed

00:20:57.870 --> 00:20:59.970
by the time you get
towards the latter half

00:20:59.970 --> 00:21:01.160
of whatever workout you're in.

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:02.910
To the same point you made before like,

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:06.360
when you start to approach
that 50 minutes an hour mark,

00:21:06.360 --> 00:21:08.490
you are either losing
focus, you're losing energy,

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:11.400
you're losing contract viability,
you're losing something.

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:14.220
And if you're relegating whatever it is,

00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:16.710
the pulling portion of that
to the end of that workout,

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:18.150
something suffers so that,

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:19.950
and if they realize that's happening,

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:21.090
then maybe you switch them up

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:22.170
the next time you do the workout

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:24.750
where the pulling portion of
the upper workout goes first,

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:26.100
and then the pushing goes later,

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:29.580
so you're at least not
just continuing that cycle,

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:30.840
but at the same time,

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:32.363
if you were able to kind of split them up,

00:21:32.363 --> 00:21:34.890
you get a chance to kind of take a break,

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:36.870
you could have that
freshness of focus again,

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:38.670
and you could actually
get a better effort in,

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:40.770
'cause again, I think
effort drives the results.

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:42.660
So if the effort is not compromised,

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:44.040
then you should be able to do that.

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:47.268
But systemically is that a problem?

00:21:47.268 --> 00:21:48.603
And I think that it is a
problem for a lot of people.

00:21:48.603 --> 00:21:50.148
It's just hard to,

00:21:50.148 --> 00:21:53.070
it's hard to rev the engine
up a lot of times in the day.

00:21:53.070 --> 00:21:54.510
You warm that thing up once,

00:21:54.510 --> 00:21:56.550
it's like that car in the
winter, you get it going once,

00:21:56.550 --> 00:21:57.383
you're lucky, okay,

00:21:57.383 --> 00:21:58.770
now you got to drive
it the rest of the day.

00:21:58.770 --> 00:22:01.560
But you put it in the garage

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:04.110
and try to start it the
next day, it's a problem.

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:06.390
So, you know, young people
can get away with it

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:08.910
a lot more than older people could.

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:10.830
- Well, I've never had a
strong recovery quotient,

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:12.904
but if I stick to this
one day off in between,

00:22:12.904 --> 00:22:15.540
every once in a while, two
days in a row training,

00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:16.740
maybe because I have to travel

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:18.270
and I want to make sure
I get all the workouts

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:20.310
and kind of thing, I seem to be okay.

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:21.960
I like your example of warming up the car,

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:24.359
spoken like a true east coaster,

00:22:24.359 --> 00:22:26.250
or those of us are on the west coast,

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:27.150
I took a moment there,

00:22:27.150 --> 00:22:31.530
but we folks from the East
Coast and the Midwest, get it,

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:33.990
and certainly from Europe.

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:38.842
In terms of the mixing up
of cardiovascular training

00:22:38.842 --> 00:22:43.705
and resistance training,
same day, different day,

00:22:43.705 --> 00:22:47.310
which one should come first,
which one should come second?

00:22:47.310 --> 00:22:49.740
If one main goals, again,

00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:51.780
everyone listening has
different goals are,

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:53.160
most people would like to either maintain

00:22:53.160 --> 00:22:54.570
or gain some muscle.

00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:56.240
I don't know many people
that want to lose muscle.

00:22:56.240 --> 00:22:57.720
To maintain or gains some muscle,

00:22:57.720 --> 00:23:00.390
usually in specific
locations on their body,

00:23:00.390 --> 00:23:03.030
most people would like to be
a bit leaner or a lot leaner.

00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:04.740
There are a few people out there that are

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:06.180
either naturally lean or don't want,

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:07.500
or actually just want to gain weight.

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:09.210
But assuming that people
want to get leaner,

00:23:09.210 --> 00:23:10.740
put on some muscle, maintain muscle,

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:13.950
and want to have a healthy
heart and a healthy brain,

00:23:13.950 --> 00:23:16.611
which basically requires a
healthy cardiovascular system,

00:23:16.611 --> 00:23:20.040
how would you incorporate
cardiovascular work

00:23:20.040 --> 00:23:22.893
into the overall weekly regimen?

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:26.340
- So again, I think that the bare minimum

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:28.830
is probably twice a week
in terms of cardiovascular.

00:23:28.830 --> 00:23:30.330
If you want to have some semblance

00:23:30.330 --> 00:23:31.500
of cardiovascular conditioning.

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:33.960
But I think most people
who actually need it more

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:36.060
or want to pursue it more than that

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:37.830
are going to need more time to do that.

00:23:37.830 --> 00:23:41.910
So at some point it can't just
be relegated to a day off,

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:44.820
or a day off from the
weight training workouts.

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:48.300
So at some point it has
to occur on the same day.

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:50.333
And in that case, I just like to put it,

00:23:50.333 --> 00:23:52.980
if that is, you're not your primary goal,

00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:56.040
but you're looking more for
the, just the overall picture,

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:57.000
the aesthetics you mentioned,

00:23:57.000 --> 00:23:58.680
putting muscle on in certain areas,

00:23:58.680 --> 00:23:59.867
then I would put it at
the end of the workout.

00:23:59.867 --> 00:24:02.730
'Cause you don't want
it in any way compromise

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:04.020
the weight training work out.

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:06.300
And as we've sort of referenced
a couple times already,

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:08.700
the intensity of those
workouts is important.

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:11.220
And we know there's a strength component

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:14.004
to those workouts also that is going to

00:24:14.004 --> 00:24:17.070
be a helpful stimulus for growth.

00:24:17.070 --> 00:24:19.470
So the conditioning, the cardio,

00:24:19.470 --> 00:24:22.380
that stuff done prior to any training,

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:23.550
strength training, workout,

00:24:23.550 --> 00:24:25.650
is likely going to impair your ability

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:27.150
to perform at your best.

00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:29.310
So unless it's just done
for a quick little warmup

00:24:29.310 --> 00:24:30.143
in the beginning,

00:24:30.143 --> 00:24:31.980
but then it's not sustained
long enough really to be

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:34.110
a benefit for cardiovascular conditioning.

00:24:34.110 --> 00:24:37.391
So I just like to put that at the end,

00:24:37.391 --> 00:24:42.390
realizing that even if
my effort level is lower,

00:24:42.390 --> 00:24:43.890
my output is lower,

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:47.850
if it's still placing a
demand on my cardiac output

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:51.030
to get that conditioning
effect because I'm fatigued,

00:24:51.030 --> 00:24:52.770
it still has a demand
on my cardiac output,

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:54.150
so it's still achieving its goal,

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:56.150
but it didn't interfere with my main goal

00:24:56.150 --> 00:24:58.800
of being able to increase
my performance in the gym.

00:24:58.800 --> 00:24:59.633
- Got it.

00:24:59.633 --> 00:25:02.250
And in terms of the form
of cardiovascular training,

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:07.250
I've seen you do a number of,
I have to say very impressive

00:25:07.320 --> 00:25:09.210
high intensity interval type work.

00:25:09.210 --> 00:25:10.710
So burpee type work,

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:14.130
or pushups with crunches mixed into them,

00:25:14.130 --> 00:25:15.810
anyway, people can see your videos,

00:25:15.810 --> 00:25:17.760
I didn't describe those in the best way,

00:25:17.760 --> 00:25:20.970
but rather than on the treadmill

00:25:20.970 --> 00:25:22.980
or out jogging for 30-45 minutes,

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:25.407
is that because you
prefer higher intensity,

00:25:25.407 --> 00:25:28.260
higher heart rate type training,

00:25:28.260 --> 00:25:30.116
or is it because you
live in cold Connecticut,

00:25:30.116 --> 00:25:33.300
and you don't want to be
out jogging on the roads

00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:35.160
in the middle of the winter?

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:36.300
- I think all the above,

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:38.490
I mean, those are factors
from a personal level,

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:41.909
but I think that if you are-

00:25:41.909 --> 00:25:46.620
If we could blend function
across these realms

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:49.080
and not have such a delineation between,

00:25:49.080 --> 00:25:51.480
this is my way training,
and this is my conditioning,

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:54.300
but figure out a way
to blend them together,

00:25:54.300 --> 00:25:57.420
I always think that you've got
a better opportunity to get

00:25:57.420 --> 00:25:59.640
that more well rounded result.

00:25:59.640 --> 00:26:03.480
And I like to kind of mix up
that straight conditioning work

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:06.180
and also some of the footwork drills.

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:08.070
We have some high expectations for guys

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:09.330
that come into our programs,

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:11.640
like to just do some footwork drills.

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:12.960
- Like ladders.
- Like ladders,

00:26:12.960 --> 00:26:14.400
or line drills or something.

00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:15.233
And you know what happens?

00:26:15.233 --> 00:26:16.980
People become intrigued and interested.

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:20.400
Like I never, I haven't
tried this since high school.

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:23.220
And they become interested
in just the challenge of it,

00:26:23.220 --> 00:26:26.400
and then as we become almost
distracted by the challenge,

00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:28.860
we're now like finding
ourselves conditioning.

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:31.350
And I always think
that's an important part,

00:26:31.350 --> 00:26:33.300
that sometimes you got to draw people in

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:36.840
to show them what they
might be interested in.

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:39.120
And from the output or the effect of it,

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:41.670
I just think that when
you're able to blend some,

00:26:41.670 --> 00:26:44.280
still maintain some of
that strength training

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:46.050
into the exercise, as you mentioned,

00:26:46.050 --> 00:26:48.300
let's say I'm doing some kind
of a push up or a burpee,

00:26:48.300 --> 00:26:51.990
there is an anaerobic component to that

00:26:51.990 --> 00:26:53.580
that is going to be helpful,

00:26:53.580 --> 00:26:56.823
that then rather than just
walking or just jogging.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:00.570
Not to say that that
isn't an effective means

00:27:00.570 --> 00:27:03.354
for strict cardiac conditioning,

00:27:03.354 --> 00:27:06.870
it's one of the ways that
we've had for centuries,

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:07.710
how to do it.

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:10.170
But I just think that if we can blend it,

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:12.480
then it becomes maybe a
little bit more interesting

00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:14.700
and you get some of
those crossover benefits,

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:16.290
and it doesn't become so segmented

00:27:16.290 --> 00:27:17.970
in terms of what we're trying to do.

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:20.700
- I love the idea of bringing
some mental challenge

00:27:20.700 --> 00:27:23.520
and some desire to improve
a skill while conditioning.

00:27:23.520 --> 00:27:25.320
That's not something that
I've thought of before,

00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:26.910
and it's simply 'cause I've overlooked it,

00:27:26.910 --> 00:27:29.850
but it makes sense because, my
sister who's reasonably fit,

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:31.809
although I'm always trying
to get her to do a bit more,

00:27:31.809 --> 00:27:34.410
she always asks me, what should I take?

00:27:34.410 --> 00:27:36.420
And I'm a, I don't
believe her in supplements

00:27:36.420 --> 00:27:38.160
are for certain people
in certain instances,

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:39.990
but I keep telling her, you know, it's,

00:27:39.990 --> 00:27:42.090
the behaviors are going to end,

00:27:42.090 --> 00:27:44.520
nutrition are going to
have the greatest outsize

00:27:44.520 --> 00:27:45.750
positive effect.

00:27:45.750 --> 00:27:49.545
And she loves things like
dance classes and things,

00:27:49.545 --> 00:27:52.110
or kickboxing, these
kinds of things which,

00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:54.660
so it makes sense that
if you can hook somebody

00:27:54.660 --> 00:27:56.820
on the conditioning
aspect or the skill aspect

00:27:56.820 --> 00:27:58.950
and kind of trick them
into doing more cardio,

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:00.630
so to speak that's terrific.

00:28:00.630 --> 00:28:03.240
Also the neuroscientist
in me just has to say,

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:06.480
forgive me, that anytime you're engaging

00:28:06.480 --> 00:28:09.300
the two sets of motor neurons,
the ones in your brain,

00:28:09.300 --> 00:28:11.070
the upper motor neurons and
the ones in your spinal cord,

00:28:11.070 --> 00:28:12.750
anytime you're engaging
those upper motor neurons,

00:28:12.750 --> 00:28:15.593
which are for deliberate
well controlled action,

00:28:15.593 --> 00:28:17.910
you're doing a great thing for your brain

00:28:17.910 --> 00:28:19.110
in terms of brain longevity.

00:28:19.110 --> 00:28:22.890
So, now I need to incorporate
some actual skills

00:28:22.890 --> 00:28:23.763
into my training.

00:28:25.135 --> 00:28:27.240
Going back to weight training a bit,

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:30.810
one of the most important
things I learned from you,

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:33.745
so over the years was that,

00:28:33.745 --> 00:28:38.100
when training to increase
muscle size, to really think,

00:28:38.100 --> 00:28:39.780
not so much about moving weights,

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:41.460
but more about challenging muscles.

00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:42.293
- Yeah.

00:28:42.293 --> 00:28:45.300
- I also heard this from
my friend, Ben Pakulski,

00:28:45.300 --> 00:28:46.920
who's a very well accomplished,

00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:47.880
He was a bodybuilder,

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:50.370
now he's into other aspects
of fitness, teaches fitness,

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:52.440
but don't move weights challenge muscles,

00:28:52.440 --> 00:28:53.850
unless you're trying to power lift

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:57.390
or something of that sort, which
I'm not, immensely helpful.

00:28:57.390 --> 00:29:00.118
But the other thing that
I learned from you that,

00:29:00.118 --> 00:29:03.539
combined with that was this idea that

00:29:03.539 --> 00:29:06.930
certain muscles will grow
better and get stronger

00:29:06.930 --> 00:29:09.840
much more easily, maybe
even will recover better

00:29:09.840 --> 00:29:12.540
because of our ability to
contract them really hard.

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:14.670
And this what I call the Cavaliere test,

00:29:14.670 --> 00:29:18.638
which is at least if I could paraphrase,

00:29:18.638 --> 00:29:23.588
so for instance, if I can, it's
always the bicep, isn't it?

00:29:23.588 --> 00:29:25.290
Let's use the calf or the bicep.

00:29:25.290 --> 00:29:28.290
If you can flex your
bicep to the point where

00:29:28.290 --> 00:29:29.670
it hurts a little bit,

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:31.938
like it almost feels
like a cramp or a cramp,

00:29:31.938 --> 00:29:33.060
where you can flex your
calf to the point where

00:29:33.060 --> 00:29:34.320
it really cramps up a little bit,

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:36.030
almost feels like it's nodding up,

00:29:36.030 --> 00:29:37.530
that's a pretty good indication

00:29:37.530 --> 00:29:39.000
that you're going to be able to

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:41.458
stimulate that muscle well under load

00:29:41.458 --> 00:29:43.830
if you're doing the movement properly.

00:29:43.830 --> 00:29:46.470
And that's the feeling to
actually aim for each repetition,

00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:48.477
maybe even throughout the repetition.

00:29:48.477 --> 00:29:52.680
For me, this completely
transformed my results.

00:29:52.680 --> 00:29:54.570
And this was, I think
maybe five/six years ago

00:29:54.570 --> 00:29:55.920
that I first heard this from you,

00:29:55.920 --> 00:29:58.560
body parts that for me, lagged behind,

00:29:58.560 --> 00:30:01.050
that I thought maybe genetically
weren't going to work for me,

00:30:01.050 --> 00:30:04.200
immediately just started growing, right?

00:30:04.200 --> 00:30:06.120
And I was getting stronger and stronger,

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:08.400
and I thought this is
really something so much so

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:10.797
that I've dedicated a
portion of my research

00:30:10.797 --> 00:30:13.650
along with, in collaboration
with another group

00:30:13.650 --> 00:30:14.937
to try and understand

00:30:14.937 --> 00:30:18.060
what's happening in these upper
motor neurons in the brain

00:30:18.060 --> 00:30:20.250
that can engage the muscles even more.

00:30:20.250 --> 00:30:22.710
And that it's not just
about progressive overload

00:30:22.710 --> 00:30:24.417
or putting a pump into the muscle.

00:30:24.417 --> 00:30:28.110
That it's really this
mind muscle connection

00:30:28.110 --> 00:30:30.690
is a real thing when it
comes to predicting results

00:30:30.690 --> 00:30:32.430
and that you can get better at it.

00:30:32.430 --> 00:30:34.527
So forgive me if we're paraphrasing

00:30:34.527 --> 00:30:36.360
your incredible content around this,

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:38.700
it made a tremendous difference for me

00:30:38.700 --> 00:30:41.010
and a number of other people
that have passed that along to.

00:30:41.010 --> 00:30:44.103
But what can you, first of all,
how did you arrive at that?

00:30:45.180 --> 00:30:46.830
Because we hear about the
mind-muscle connection,

00:30:46.830 --> 00:30:48.600
but I really heard it first from you.

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:50.572
How did you arrive at
this kind of cramp test,

00:30:50.572 --> 00:30:52.282
the Cavaliere test as I'll call it?

00:30:52.282 --> 00:30:53.760
It's always weird when people name things

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:55.020
after themselves in science,

00:30:55.020 --> 00:30:56.880
but other scientists can name things.

00:30:56.880 --> 00:30:58.890
So there is now officially
the Cavaliere test,

00:30:58.890 --> 00:31:01.170
is whether or not you can cramp the muscle

00:31:01.170 --> 00:31:03.230
in the absence of load, just flexing it

00:31:03.230 --> 00:31:04.710
to the point where it hurts a little bit.

00:31:04.710 --> 00:31:06.870
That would be a good indication

00:31:06.870 --> 00:31:08.970
that you could grow that muscle well.

00:31:08.970 --> 00:31:10.920
How did you come up with this?

00:31:10.920 --> 00:31:15.000
- I mean, honestly, it's
something that made sense to me,

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:19.230
because during my workouts,
even as a young kid

00:31:19.230 --> 00:31:21.540
just starting out, I always wanted to know

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:22.590
what is it working?

00:31:22.590 --> 00:31:24.630
A lot of people ask that
question more so than you think,

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:26.310
like, what is this supposed to work?

00:31:26.310 --> 00:31:28.770
And a lot, I don't know
if you've ever noticed,

00:31:28.770 --> 00:31:30.450
but like when people ask that question,

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:32.280
if they're being trained by a trainer,

00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:34.530
and the trainer's saying,
well, just do this,

00:31:34.530 --> 00:31:37.080
do this exercise and they'll
show you how to do it.

00:31:37.080 --> 00:31:39.300
But then they'll say, but
what is it supposed to work?

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:40.890
Where am I supposed to feel this, right?

00:31:40.890 --> 00:31:43.620
People, did they just
inherently ask that question?

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:45.120
A lot of people will.

00:31:45.120 --> 00:31:47.460
I was one of those that did
that, and I asked that question.

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:48.510
Not because I knew what I was doing,

00:31:48.510 --> 00:31:49.650
but just because I don't know,

00:31:49.650 --> 00:31:52.852
I wanted to know what was
supposed to be doing the work.

00:31:52.852 --> 00:31:55.290
Once you do that, and you
start to seek that out,

00:31:55.290 --> 00:31:56.520
and say, okay, well,

00:31:56.520 --> 00:31:58.836
the bicep is what's supposed
to be doing the work,

00:31:58.836 --> 00:32:01.770
then I want to make sure
the bicep's doing the work.

00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:05.070
So then I would just sort of
really like tweak the movement

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:07.770
to make it do more work or
feel more uncomfortable,

00:32:07.770 --> 00:32:09.090
or get it stronger contraction,

00:32:09.090 --> 00:32:10.716
knowing if that's supposed to do the job,

00:32:10.716 --> 00:32:13.350
it wasn't until PT
school that I'm learning,

00:32:13.350 --> 00:32:15.150
oh, well, the flexion of the elbow

00:32:15.150 --> 00:32:16.500
is the brachial and the bias

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:18.716
and the bias' responsible for supination.

00:32:18.716 --> 00:32:20.490
You started to, I learned
other components of it,

00:32:20.490 --> 00:32:23.400
but all I wanted to know was
to bring my arm up in a curl,

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:25.050
what is supposed to do the job?

00:32:25.050 --> 00:32:30.050
So I would seek out ways
to make that happen better.

00:32:30.237 --> 00:32:31.770
And when I was able to do that,

00:32:31.770 --> 00:32:33.780
I could feel the stronger contraction.

00:32:33.780 --> 00:32:35.520
And I just, I don't know what it, I just,

00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:37.080
I was no visionary, I just felt like

00:32:37.080 --> 00:32:39.300
I knew that that was
going to be better for me

00:32:39.300 --> 00:32:40.890
if the muscle I was trying to grow

00:32:40.890 --> 00:32:43.290
was being stressed more effectively.

00:32:43.290 --> 00:32:47.190
So when I was attempting to do this

00:32:47.190 --> 00:32:50.190
across different exercises,
I would notice that

00:32:50.190 --> 00:32:54.570
what I could do potentially
on a curl where my arm is up,

00:32:54.570 --> 00:32:56.974
where you asking me to flex
my bicep that position,

00:32:56.974 --> 00:33:00.900
I couldn't do if I was
doing a concentration curl,

00:33:00.900 --> 00:33:03.780
or I couldn't carry over to a cable curl,

00:33:03.780 --> 00:33:06.240
and that shouldn't really change,

00:33:06.240 --> 00:33:08.520
'cause the function is
still largely the same,

00:33:08.520 --> 00:33:11.310
there's still elbow flexion,
there's still supination,

00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:13.350
why am I not able to do it there?

00:33:13.350 --> 00:33:15.511
And that's when it sort of
clued into me that like,

00:33:15.511 --> 00:33:18.180
your mind-muscle connection on

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:20.580
not just your mind with one muscle,

00:33:20.580 --> 00:33:22.710
but on every exercise matters,

00:33:22.710 --> 00:33:25.200
and it varies from exercise to exercise.

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:28.500
And even if you don't gain
muscle size from doing that,

00:33:28.500 --> 00:33:30.360
although I think it's very hard not to,

00:33:30.360 --> 00:33:32.025
especially if you're
not used to doing that,

00:33:32.025 --> 00:33:34.966
there's a term I like to call muscularity,

00:33:34.966 --> 00:33:36.780
which is a difference, right?

00:33:36.780 --> 00:33:39.360
It's the level of sort of
resting tone in the muscle.

00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:41.430
That improves dramatically.

00:33:41.430 --> 00:33:43.636
If you can learn how to just start to

00:33:43.636 --> 00:33:46.185
engage that muscle better,

00:33:46.185 --> 00:33:49.620
the muscularity, the
resting tone of that muscle

00:33:49.620 --> 00:33:54.620
is harder, it's more at
attention, it's more alive.

00:33:55.710 --> 00:33:59.040
And it's all driven from
being able to connect better

00:33:59.040 --> 00:34:02.070
neurologically with the muscle
that you're trying to train.

00:34:02.070 --> 00:34:04.533
I've talked about a lot in efficiency

00:34:04.533 --> 00:34:07.500
is really what you're
trying to seek in movements

00:34:07.500 --> 00:34:09.900
when you're trying to create hypertrophy.

00:34:09.900 --> 00:34:11.010
When strength is your goal,

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:13.050
efficiency of the movement
is what you're looking for.

00:34:13.050 --> 00:34:15.450
You're looking to have
muscles tied together

00:34:15.450 --> 00:34:17.040
and work well efficiently,

00:34:17.040 --> 00:34:18.810
the chest, the shoulders, the triceps,

00:34:18.810 --> 00:34:21.240
to get a bar off of your
chest or in a bench press.

00:34:21.240 --> 00:34:24.446
You're not looking to
make it a very inefficient

00:34:24.446 --> 00:34:26.460
leverages for your chest,

00:34:26.460 --> 00:34:28.530
to try to grow your
chest in a bench press,

00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:31.170
you're trying to let the
whole package come together

00:34:31.170 --> 00:34:32.340
for a greater output.

00:34:32.340 --> 00:34:35.070
But when you're trying to go
and create muscle hypertrophy

00:34:35.070 --> 00:34:37.200
or even this muscularity
that I talk about,

00:34:37.200 --> 00:34:39.747
you need to seek ways to make
it feel more uncomfortable.

00:34:39.747 --> 00:34:41.970
If you don't feel the discomfort,

00:34:41.970 --> 00:34:43.290
then you're doing something wrong.

00:34:43.290 --> 00:34:46.350
And I struggle to this day
on certain muscle groups

00:34:46.350 --> 00:34:48.960
to still do that, even knowing
what I'm trying to work

00:34:48.960 --> 00:34:51.780
and knowing what the goal of
everything I'm preaching here.

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:53.217
It's very difficult for some muscles

00:34:53.217 --> 00:34:55.560
and for certain people to
do this on certain muscles.

00:34:55.560 --> 00:34:58.860
But as you mentioned, practice does help.

00:34:58.860 --> 00:35:01.680
And the more you become consistent

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:03.450
and deliberate with what
you're trying to do,

00:35:03.450 --> 00:35:05.546
the more of a result you actually get.

00:35:05.546 --> 00:35:08.790
- It's couple of really
couple of points I'd like

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:11.073
to delve into further.

00:35:12.060 --> 00:35:14.520
First of all, my hunch was always that

00:35:14.520 --> 00:35:18.000
the muscle groups that grew most easily

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:21.270
and that I could contract
hardest without any-

00:35:21.270 --> 00:35:23.966
The first time I did the
Cavaliere test, got 10 out 10.

00:35:23.966 --> 00:35:25.290
If we give it a 10 out of 10 scale.

00:35:25.290 --> 00:35:27.210
It could just like cinch,
isolate those muscles,

00:35:27.210 --> 00:35:28.860
cinch them, grow them easily.

00:35:28.860 --> 00:35:30.180
I mean, there's certain body parts,

00:35:30.180 --> 00:35:31.013
I don't want to say which ones,

00:35:31.013 --> 00:35:33.510
'cause it doesn't really
matter, that I always felt like

00:35:33.510 --> 00:35:35.220
if I just did pushups, they would grow,

00:35:35.220 --> 00:35:36.783
and these muscles are far
away from any of the muscles

00:35:36.783 --> 00:35:38.490
that are supposed to
be involved in pushups,

00:35:38.490 --> 00:35:41.220
even though I like to think
I'm doing pushups correctly.

00:35:41.220 --> 00:35:43.144
You'll tell me if I'm not.

00:35:43.144 --> 00:35:46.170
But some of that I think is genetic,

00:35:46.170 --> 00:35:48.840
and some of that has to do
with the sports that I played

00:35:48.840 --> 00:35:49.800
when I was younger.

00:35:49.800 --> 00:35:52.080
So I swam, I played soccer, I skateboard.

00:35:52.080 --> 00:35:53.130
And then later I boxed.

00:35:53.130 --> 00:35:55.829
And so the muscles
involved in those sports

00:35:55.829 --> 00:35:58.110
were always very easy to engage

00:35:58.110 --> 00:35:59.744
later when I went into the gym.

00:35:59.744 --> 00:36:03.690
So I guess perhaps a call to parents,

00:36:03.690 --> 00:36:05.805
having kids do a lot of dynamic activity,

00:36:05.805 --> 00:36:08.310
seems like it might be a good idea.

00:36:08.310 --> 00:36:11.490
The other thing is this
issue of muscularity.

00:36:11.490 --> 00:36:12.900
I am so glad you brought that up.

00:36:12.900 --> 00:36:16.500
There are, I have to imagine
a large number of listeners

00:36:16.500 --> 00:36:18.030
who don't want to get bigger.

00:36:18.030 --> 00:36:20.580
They don't want to take
up a larger clothing size.

00:36:20.580 --> 00:36:23.400
They don't want to take up more space.

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.200
In fact, some of them would
like to take up less space,

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:28.470
but they want that quality
that you're describing,

00:36:28.470 --> 00:36:31.770
which is that, oftentimes
you hear it more in the,

00:36:31.770 --> 00:36:34.413
here I'm stereotyping a
bit, but with kindness.

00:36:34.413 --> 00:36:37.470
You hear from women who
haven't weight trained,

00:36:37.470 --> 00:36:39.030
they say, I don't want to get big, often.

00:36:39.030 --> 00:36:39.863
Sometimes they do,

00:36:39.863 --> 00:36:41.850
but most women that
I've helped weight train

00:36:41.850 --> 00:36:42.810
or talked to about weight train say,

00:36:42.810 --> 00:36:44.460
I don't want to get big,
I want to get toned.

00:36:44.460 --> 00:36:46.110
And I think what they're referring to

00:36:46.110 --> 00:36:48.061
is this quality of muscularity.

00:36:48.061 --> 00:36:50.910
- A hundred percent,
- This idea that at resting

00:36:50.910 --> 00:36:53.100
or at close to rest, or
anytime someone reaches out

00:36:53.100 --> 00:36:54.120
and grabs a glass,

00:36:54.120 --> 00:36:56.250
that the muscles almost look
like they're kind of twitching

00:36:56.250 --> 00:36:57.390
underneath the skin.

00:36:57.390 --> 00:37:00.633
And yet it's Saran wrap skin
anatomy chart type skin.

00:37:02.130 --> 00:37:05.928
So this thing of
muscularity, or resting tone,

00:37:05.928 --> 00:37:07.650
has a physiological basis,

00:37:07.650 --> 00:37:09.720
I think it's how readily the nerves

00:37:09.720 --> 00:37:11.250
are communicating with the muscles.

00:37:11.250 --> 00:37:14.790
And you're saying that by
learning to engage the muscles

00:37:14.790 --> 00:37:18.270
more actively, the resting tone
or muscularity can improve.

00:37:18.270 --> 00:37:20.460
Have you seen that both in men and women?

00:37:20.460 --> 00:37:21.293
- Yeah, oh yeah.

00:37:21.293 --> 00:37:25.380
- And do you think this is
something that takes upkeep,

00:37:25.380 --> 00:37:28.260
maintenance, or that once
you develop that in a muscle,

00:37:28.260 --> 00:37:29.773
you can just kind of let it coast.

00:37:29.773 --> 00:37:32.820
- I think like everything
at key, it requires upkeep,

00:37:32.820 --> 00:37:35.550
you know, user lose it,
I do believe firmly,

00:37:35.550 --> 00:37:38.970
but like I think that the
development of the connections

00:37:38.970 --> 00:37:41.160
is going to be harder than the
maintenance of the connection.

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:44.412
As I said, I still struggle
to this day for myself with

00:37:44.412 --> 00:37:47.310
unnamed muscle groups, they also,-

00:37:47.310 --> 00:37:51.840
But there's just certain areas

00:37:51.840 --> 00:37:54.690
that are harder for your
brain for whatever reason

00:37:54.690 --> 00:37:57.870
to just develop that connection
at that type of level

00:37:57.870 --> 00:38:00.690
to create that extra strong contraction.

00:38:00.690 --> 00:38:04.440
But I think that with
proper dedication and focus,

00:38:04.440 --> 00:38:06.180
and then I'll go right out and say,

00:38:06.180 --> 00:38:07.650
calves is one of the areas that

00:38:07.650 --> 00:38:09.870
I don't necessarily have
a great connection with,

00:38:09.870 --> 00:38:12.360
and I also obviously must not care so much

00:38:12.360 --> 00:38:14.070
'cause I don't put in the time and effort

00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:17.547
to create that connection as I could.

00:38:17.547 --> 00:38:20.460
So I think what might happen is,

00:38:20.460 --> 00:38:21.900
you know yeah, there
could be a struggle there,

00:38:21.900 --> 00:38:23.951
but then with struggle comes disinterest,

00:38:23.951 --> 00:38:26.340
'cause you're like, well
screw it, I'm a calf knot

00:38:26.340 --> 00:38:27.660
and I'm not going to do anything about it.

00:38:27.660 --> 00:38:31.620
So I think if you put
the required effort in,

00:38:31.620 --> 00:38:33.777
and the time and repetitions
that you will develop that,

00:38:33.777 --> 00:38:36.180
and once you do develop it,
it's going to stick around

00:38:36.180 --> 00:38:37.470
a lot longer than it would

00:38:37.470 --> 00:38:39.726
had you not invested
any time into it at all,

00:38:39.726 --> 00:38:41.880
not requiring as much of that.

00:38:41.880 --> 00:38:44.556
But I mean, I don't
know, like you mentioned,

00:38:44.556 --> 00:38:46.716
now when you train, it's like,

00:38:46.716 --> 00:38:49.740
you're just, this is just
part of how you train now,

00:38:49.740 --> 00:38:50.790
like you're going hard,

00:38:50.790 --> 00:38:53.460
you're trying to really
forcefully contract.

00:38:53.460 --> 00:38:55.320
You're not just moving the weight,

00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:57.090
I say from point A to point B,

00:38:57.090 --> 00:38:59.460
but you're like trying
to contract the weight

00:38:59.460 --> 00:39:00.735
through that range.

00:39:00.735 --> 00:39:04.350
That is a mindset that I try to put into

00:39:04.350 --> 00:39:05.490
what everything I'm doing,

00:39:05.490 --> 00:39:08.280
unless of course I'm focused
on a strength exercise

00:39:08.280 --> 00:39:11.160
where I'm just trying
to lift a greater amount

00:39:11.160 --> 00:39:12.900
and use all the muscles together.

00:39:12.900 --> 00:39:15.720
But when the goal is
inefficiency for hypertrophy,

00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:19.740
I am really trying to
create that contraction,

00:39:19.740 --> 00:39:21.420
and it's just part of my training.

00:39:21.420 --> 00:39:24.930
So I guess that for consistency's sake,

00:39:24.930 --> 00:39:26.580
as long as I'm training is happening.

00:39:26.580 --> 00:39:29.580
If I get away from training
that it's not happening at all,

00:39:29.580 --> 00:39:30.480
but you know, even there,

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:33.690
I probably another embarrassing
admission probably,

00:39:33.690 --> 00:39:35.520
will mindfully do it throughout the day,

00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:38.310
even with no weight in my
hand, in certain muscle groups,

00:39:38.310 --> 00:39:40.980
whether it be my abs or my arm
or my shoulders or something,

00:39:40.980 --> 00:39:43.020
I'm doing something just to
sort of engage the muscles.

00:39:43.020 --> 00:39:46.650
And I do think that some of
that sort of inane practice

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:48.900
actually helps by the time
you go back into the gym.

00:39:48.900 --> 00:39:52.230
You just kind of keep
that connection going.

00:39:52.230 --> 00:39:55.290
- Well, it certainly obeys all
the rules of neuroplasticity.

00:39:55.290 --> 00:39:57.900
You know the fire together
wired together mantra,

00:39:57.900 --> 00:40:00.780
which is the words of my
colleague, Carla Shatz,

00:40:00.780 --> 00:40:03.450
hold true for all aspects
of neural function,

00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:05.100
including nerve to muscle.

00:40:05.100 --> 00:40:06.690
So these flexing throughout the day

00:40:06.690 --> 00:40:10.590
or the deliberate isolation
of contracting a muscle

00:40:10.590 --> 00:40:12.750
throughout the day is without question

00:40:12.750 --> 00:40:14.040
engaging neuroplasticity.

00:40:14.040 --> 00:40:15.870
And if you were to do
fewer those repetitions,

00:40:15.870 --> 00:40:17.400
you're going to get less engagement

00:40:17.400 --> 00:40:18.840
of the nerve to muscle connection.

00:40:18.840 --> 00:40:22.830
I can say this with a smile
and with confidence because,

00:40:22.830 --> 00:40:24.900
one of the first things all
neuroscience students learn

00:40:24.900 --> 00:40:26.490
is about the neuromuscular junction,

00:40:26.490 --> 00:40:29.430
'cause it's a really
simple example of where

00:40:29.430 --> 00:40:30.780
the more times the nerve fires

00:40:30.780 --> 00:40:32.250
and gets the muscle to contract,

00:40:32.250 --> 00:40:33.570
the stronger that connection get,

00:40:33.570 --> 00:40:35.400
receptors are brought
there, et cetera, et cetera,

00:40:35.400 --> 00:40:37.560
there's a whole bunch of
mechanisms for a topic

00:40:37.560 --> 00:40:39.150
of another podcast.

00:40:39.150 --> 00:40:42.790
But basically that
practice throughout the day

00:40:43.650 --> 00:40:45.650
makes total sense and works.

00:40:45.650 --> 00:40:47.690
- Yeah, and there's no, believe me,

00:40:47.690 --> 00:40:48.720
there's no science behind that

00:40:48.720 --> 00:40:50.460
in terms of the application of it.

00:40:50.460 --> 00:40:51.660
You do it when,

00:40:51.660 --> 00:40:54.300
you catch yourself doing
it from time to time.

00:40:54.300 --> 00:40:56.910
But it is definitely
something that's easily done

00:40:56.910 --> 00:40:59.220
discreetly and you wind up doing.

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:03.025
I actually, I think in a recent
video when I did talk about

00:41:03.025 --> 00:41:07.350
growing your arms by just
improving the connection.

00:41:07.350 --> 00:41:10.230
Not that that connection
itself is applying any load

00:41:10.230 --> 00:41:12.540
or resistance that's significant

00:41:12.540 --> 00:41:13.950
to create overload for growth,

00:41:13.950 --> 00:41:15.830
but it's the development
of that connection

00:41:15.830 --> 00:41:18.090
that I then take back with me into the gym

00:41:18.090 --> 00:41:19.440
at a more effective level

00:41:19.440 --> 00:41:21.120
that takes every exercise I do there

00:41:21.120 --> 00:41:22.140
and makes it more effective.

00:41:22.140 --> 00:41:24.890
- That's like sharpening
the blade, so to speak.

00:41:24.890 --> 00:41:27.545
Yeah, certainly obeys the laws

00:41:27.545 --> 00:41:29.673
of nerve to muscle physiology.

00:41:30.900 --> 00:41:33.138
Wanted to just touch
on a couple of things.

00:41:33.138 --> 00:41:36.330
If the goal is to challenge muscles,

00:41:36.330 --> 00:41:39.450
and one is dividing their
body into, let's say,

00:41:39.450 --> 00:41:42.783
a three or four day a week
split or so, or maybe up to six,

00:41:44.190 --> 00:41:46.980
how do you know when a muscle is ready

00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:48.361
to be challenged again?

00:41:48.361 --> 00:41:50.730
Again, I've heard, okay, every 48 hours is

00:41:50.730 --> 00:41:53.400
protein synthesis increases
and then we'll get into this

00:41:53.400 --> 00:41:54.750
and then it drops off.

00:41:54.750 --> 00:41:59.066
But frankly, if I train my legs hard,

00:41:59.066 --> 00:42:01.710
I can get stronger from
workout to workout,

00:42:01.710 --> 00:42:04.680
or at least better in some
way, workout to workout,

00:42:04.680 --> 00:42:06.300
leg workout to leg workout,

00:42:06.300 --> 00:42:09.510
training them once every
five to eight days.

00:42:09.510 --> 00:42:12.930
If I train them more often, I get worse.

00:42:12.930 --> 00:42:17.220
So whatever that 48 hour
to 72 hour thing is,

00:42:17.220 --> 00:42:18.960
somehow my legs don't obey that,

00:42:18.960 --> 00:42:21.420
or maybe something else is wrong with me,

00:42:21.420 --> 00:42:23.640
but I'm sure there are many
things else wrong with me,

00:42:23.640 --> 00:42:27.930
but how do you assess
recovery at the local level,

00:42:27.930 --> 00:42:29.490
meaning at the level of the muscles,

00:42:29.490 --> 00:42:30.990
so we'll talk about soreness,

00:42:30.990 --> 00:42:33.960
and getting better, stronger,
more repetitions, et cetera?

00:42:33.960 --> 00:42:35.340
And then at the systemic level,

00:42:35.340 --> 00:42:36.870
the level of the nervous system.

00:42:36.870 --> 00:42:40.020
And I'd love for you to
tell us about the tool that,

00:42:40.020 --> 00:42:40.980
again, I learned from you,

00:42:40.980 --> 00:42:43.230
which is actually using a physical scale,

00:42:43.230 --> 00:42:45.570
because it turns out that,

00:42:45.570 --> 00:42:47.580
will let you tell what the tool is,

00:42:47.580 --> 00:42:50.640
but that tool is also actively being used

00:42:50.640 --> 00:42:54.360
for assessing cognitive decline,
and cognitive maintenance,

00:42:54.360 --> 00:42:55.380
and cognitive function

00:42:55.380 --> 00:42:57.360
in people with Alzheimer's and dementia.

00:42:57.360 --> 00:42:59.220
- That makes total sense.

00:42:59.220 --> 00:43:00.183
Makes total sense.

00:43:01.590 --> 00:43:06.235
So regarding the first
part of the question,

00:43:06.235 --> 00:43:09.600
how would you kind of dictate
when a muscle's recovered?

00:43:09.600 --> 00:43:14.490
So I do think that what you're
experiencing is totally real,

00:43:14.490 --> 00:43:17.220
that different muscles
recover at different rates.

00:43:17.220 --> 00:43:20.283
And I've always been so
fascinated by this concept.

00:43:21.120 --> 00:43:23.970
I've talked about internally
with my team, but like,

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:28.680
I feel like what we really need
the holy grail to training,

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:30.900
is going to be when we're
able to crack the code

00:43:30.900 --> 00:43:34.830
on an individual basis,
when a muscle is recovered,

00:43:34.830 --> 00:43:37.560
and that is going to dictate
its training schedule.

00:43:37.560 --> 00:43:40.950
And the fact that you might have a bicep

00:43:40.950 --> 00:43:44.430
that could be trained
via a pulling workout,

00:43:44.430 --> 00:43:45.780
a regular bicep dedicated workout,

00:43:45.780 --> 00:43:46.800
forget to split at the moment.

00:43:46.800 --> 00:43:48.809
You may have a bicep
that's able to be trained,

00:43:48.809 --> 00:43:51.843
that can be trained again the next day.

00:43:52.920 --> 00:43:53.753
And then the next day,

00:43:53.753 --> 00:43:55.230
and then maybe you need
a day off after that.

00:43:55.230 --> 00:43:59.160
But like, that can vary from
person to person for sure,

00:43:59.160 --> 00:44:01.800
and it can vary from muscle
to muscle in that person

00:44:01.800 --> 00:44:04.260
over the course of time as you mentioned,

00:44:04.260 --> 00:44:06.510
'cause the systemic
recovery is going to impact

00:44:06.510 --> 00:44:07.680
all those muscles anyway,

00:44:07.680 --> 00:44:09.674
but let's say you're
systemically recovering,

00:44:09.674 --> 00:44:14.310
every muscle itself is going
to have a recovery rate.

00:44:14.310 --> 00:44:16.560
And I think what's fascinating is that,

00:44:16.560 --> 00:44:19.350
when you talked about before
we like to train in this week,

00:44:19.350 --> 00:44:22.200
or we have, like the way
our mind looks at training,

00:44:22.200 --> 00:44:24.690
well, if that was the
case with the biceps,

00:44:24.690 --> 00:44:28.740
that bicep is a slave to the
rest of your training split.

00:44:28.740 --> 00:44:31.380
Where it's like, well, why
does it have to be also

00:44:31.380 --> 00:44:34.470
at the end of every
eighth day or whatever,

00:44:34.470 --> 00:44:36.720
when it might respond better to something

00:44:36.720 --> 00:44:37.740
much more frequently?

00:44:37.740 --> 00:44:40.980
And your legs are also
being thrown into that mix.

00:44:40.980 --> 00:44:42.690
There's a Mike Menzer
concept where he's like,

00:44:42.690 --> 00:44:45.990
train one set and be done for 14 days.

00:44:45.990 --> 00:44:50.970
I mean, there's such variability
between muscle groups

00:44:50.970 --> 00:44:53.574
and you're linking them all together.

00:44:53.574 --> 00:44:58.574
I think that coming back
and using muscle soreness

00:44:59.250 --> 00:45:03.630
as a guideline for that, is
one of the only tools we have

00:45:03.630 --> 00:45:05.250
in terms of the local level.

00:45:05.250 --> 00:45:07.860
You know we don't really
have, being able to measure,

00:45:07.860 --> 00:45:11.550
let's say CPK levels inside
of a muscle would be amazing,

00:45:11.550 --> 00:45:16.110
at a local level to see how
recovered that muscle is,

00:45:16.110 --> 00:45:19.020
but that becomes fairly invasive,
at least to my knowledge,

00:45:19.020 --> 00:45:20.040
it becomes fairly invasive.

00:45:20.040 --> 00:45:21.990
So what are our tools?

00:45:21.990 --> 00:45:23.910
I mean, I think that at the basic level,

00:45:23.910 --> 00:45:25.290
that's the one that most
people can relate to

00:45:25.290 --> 00:45:27.780
and easily identify, and
then use that as a guideline.

00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:29.619
And if you're training
when you're really sore,

00:45:29.619 --> 00:45:32.250
it's probably not a great idea.

00:45:32.250 --> 00:45:33.660
And it's probably a good indication

00:45:33.660 --> 00:45:35.040
that that muscle's not recovered,

00:45:35.040 --> 00:45:37.260
but at least hearing what
you and I are saying here

00:45:37.260 --> 00:45:38.610
might be a comfort to the person to say,

00:45:38.610 --> 00:45:41.040
yeah, it is possible
that it's not recovered.

00:45:41.040 --> 00:45:43.097
Just because 48 hours
is the recommendation.

00:45:43.097 --> 00:45:46.020
And just because research points
to muscle protein synthesis

00:45:46.020 --> 00:45:47.970
needing a restimulation, well, maybe not,

00:45:47.970 --> 00:45:50.136
maybe you're not necessarily there yet.

00:45:50.136 --> 00:45:52.842
For that muscle that you're not there yet.

00:45:52.842 --> 00:45:54.630
So it's all really interesting stuff,

00:45:54.630 --> 00:45:58.677
but as far as the systemic recovery,

00:45:58.677 --> 00:46:01.080
I think there's a lot of
ways people talk about

00:46:01.080 --> 00:46:03.267
resting heart rate
measured in the morning,

00:46:03.267 --> 00:46:07.320
all different kinds of core temperature

00:46:07.320 --> 00:46:09.270
and things like that
that might become altered

00:46:09.270 --> 00:46:12.420
in a state of non recovery.

00:46:12.420 --> 00:46:16.920
But grip strength is very
much tied to performance

00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:18.150
and recovery.

00:46:18.150 --> 00:46:19.530
And when I was at the Mets,

00:46:19.530 --> 00:46:22.170
we used to actually take
grip strength measurements

00:46:22.170 --> 00:46:24.300
as a baseline in spring
training all the time.

00:46:24.300 --> 00:46:25.650
Now, obviously as a baseball player,

00:46:25.650 --> 00:46:26.970
you're gripping a bat, you're pitcher,

00:46:26.970 --> 00:46:28.290
you're gripping a ball like-

00:46:28.290 --> 00:46:29.790
Having good grip strength is important,

00:46:29.790 --> 00:46:32.040
so if you've noticed somebody
had a very weak grip,

00:46:32.040 --> 00:46:33.450
it's just a good focal point

00:46:33.450 --> 00:46:35.520
of a specialized training
component for the...

00:46:35.520 --> 00:46:37.050
- Would you use this
every day with those guys?

00:46:37.050 --> 00:46:38.550
- No, we would do, in spring training,

00:46:38.550 --> 00:46:40.879
we do sort of a baseline
entry level measurement.

00:46:40.879 --> 00:46:43.200
And then we would measure
it throughout the season,

00:46:43.200 --> 00:46:45.150
maybe once every two weeks or three weeks.

00:46:45.150 --> 00:46:50.040
And the idea there was
to manage the recover,

00:46:50.040 --> 00:46:51.273
measure the recovery.

00:46:52.197 --> 00:46:57.060
But I just gave it away, to
determine overall recovery,

00:46:57.060 --> 00:46:59.070
your grip strength is
pretty highly correlated.

00:46:59.070 --> 00:47:01.980
So we have found that
with one of those scales,

00:47:01.980 --> 00:47:03.690
those old fashioned bathroom scales

00:47:03.690 --> 00:47:05.850
at like the bathroom and
beyond, or whatever you can get,

00:47:05.850 --> 00:47:07.770
which by the way, almost impossible,

00:47:07.770 --> 00:47:09.840
I believe Jesse and I were
searching for the last scale

00:47:09.840 --> 00:47:10.950
to put in that video.

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:12.210
And we almost couldn't find one,

00:47:12.210 --> 00:47:14.970
'cause everything is like
digital and everything.

00:47:14.970 --> 00:47:17.460
I'm looking at the old
fashioned dial controls.

00:47:17.460 --> 00:47:19.530
- It's like old Macintosh computers.

00:47:19.530 --> 00:47:22.470
There's a huge market
for them and old phones.

00:47:22.470 --> 00:47:23.790
Kids keep your phones now.

00:47:23.790 --> 00:47:26.370
In 30 years, the lame phone now

00:47:26.370 --> 00:47:28.273
will be worth a lot of money.
- It'll be worth a lot.

00:47:28.273 --> 00:47:33.273
So, I wound up finding
one, and it's a great tool

00:47:34.170 --> 00:47:37.693
for just squeezing the
scale with your hands

00:47:37.693 --> 00:47:40.083
and seeing what type of
output you could get.

00:47:40.981 --> 00:47:43.560
And I think we all can relate to this

00:47:43.560 --> 00:47:45.300
when you just visualize,

00:47:45.300 --> 00:47:47.550
imagine the last time you
were sick, or that when you,

00:47:47.550 --> 00:47:49.590
or just try this, the next time
you wake up in the morning,

00:47:49.590 --> 00:47:51.930
when you first wake up in the
morning, you're still groggy.

00:47:51.930 --> 00:47:53.160
Try to squeeze your hand,

00:47:53.160 --> 00:47:54.930
try to make a fist as hard as you can.

00:47:54.930 --> 00:47:56.790
You're going to sit
there angry at your fist

00:47:56.790 --> 00:47:59.670
because it won't contract
as hard as you know it can.

00:47:59.670 --> 00:48:02.971
You don't have the ability
to just create the output.

00:48:02.971 --> 00:48:06.510
And that is because in that
state, you're still sleepy,

00:48:06.510 --> 00:48:09.721
you're still fatigued,
you're not even awake

00:48:09.721 --> 00:48:12.840
the whole level at this point.

00:48:12.840 --> 00:48:17.840
Well, that is still an actual
phenomenon that happens that,

00:48:19.290 --> 00:48:22.320
a lack of recovery, or
lack of wakefulness,

00:48:22.320 --> 00:48:23.430
or whatever you want to say

00:48:23.430 --> 00:48:25.470
is going to lead to a
decreased output there.

00:48:25.470 --> 00:48:28.260
So when you start to measure
that on a daily basis,

00:48:28.260 --> 00:48:29.880
you can get a pretty good
sense of where you're at.

00:48:29.880 --> 00:48:32.070
And I think when people
start to see a drop off

00:48:32.070 --> 00:48:36.423
of 10% or so, or even
greater of their grip output,

00:48:37.260 --> 00:48:39.270
you really should skip the gym that day.

00:48:39.270 --> 00:48:40.971
Because I don't think there's
much you're going to do there

00:48:40.971 --> 00:48:43.380
that's going to be that beneficial,

00:48:43.380 --> 00:48:46.890
even if it is the day to train
legs or whatever day it is.

00:48:46.890 --> 00:48:49.740
- I love this tool, it's
simple, it's low cost,

00:48:49.740 --> 00:48:51.270
if you can find such a scale.

00:48:51.270 --> 00:48:53.460
I guess you could also find
one of those grippers that,

00:48:53.460 --> 00:48:55.745
and you could do this in a
very non quantitative way,

00:48:55.745 --> 00:48:58.980
but better would be a scale
where you could actually measure

00:48:58.980 --> 00:49:02.375
how hard you can squeeze this
thing at a given time of day.

00:49:02.375 --> 00:49:05.130
It draws to mind, just a
little neuroscience factor,

00:49:05.130 --> 00:49:07.560
in the world of circadian neurobiology,

00:49:07.560 --> 00:49:10.140
one of the consistent findings is that

00:49:10.140 --> 00:49:12.210
in the middle of your nighttime,

00:49:12.210 --> 00:49:14.690
they'll wake people up and
they'll say, do this test.

00:49:14.690 --> 00:49:17.010
In the laboratory they
use a different apparatus,

00:49:17.010 --> 00:49:18.630
but it's essentially the same thing.

00:49:18.630 --> 00:49:19.463
And in the middle of the night

00:49:19.463 --> 00:49:21.060
grip strength is very, very low.

00:49:21.060 --> 00:49:23.280
And mid-morning, grip strength is high,

00:49:23.280 --> 00:49:25.740
and as the body temperature
goes up into the afternoon,

00:49:25.740 --> 00:49:26.573
grip strength goes higher
and higher and higher,

00:49:26.573 --> 00:49:27.960
and then it drops off.

00:49:27.960 --> 00:49:29.910
There's this circadian
rhythm and grip temperature.

00:49:29.910 --> 00:49:30.810
So you probably want to do this

00:49:30.810 --> 00:49:33.030
at more or less the same time each day

00:49:33.030 --> 00:49:33.863
if you're going to use it.

00:49:33.863 --> 00:49:36.840
But it I think it's
brilliant in its simplicity

00:49:36.840 --> 00:49:39.060
and it's directness to
these upper motor neurons,

00:49:39.060 --> 00:49:41.250
'cause that's really what
it's assessing your ability.

00:49:41.250 --> 00:49:43.680
Again, it's about the ability
to contract the muscles hard.

00:49:43.680 --> 00:49:44.513
If you can't do that,

00:49:44.513 --> 00:49:45.880
you're not going to get
an effective workout.

00:49:45.880 --> 00:49:46.800
- Yeah, and they also, I mean,

00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:48.900
there certainly are more
sophisticated tools too.

00:49:48.900 --> 00:49:53.130
As PT, we have hand grip dynamometers,

00:49:53.130 --> 00:49:55.587
we can measure one side at a time too.

00:49:55.587 --> 00:49:58.650
I'm not really, I'm getting
a little bit blinded

00:49:58.650 --> 00:50:00.840
by the fact that both hands
are squeezing into that scale

00:50:00.840 --> 00:50:03.030
and I don't get really
a left right comparison.

00:50:03.030 --> 00:50:03.900
But even at that level,

00:50:03.900 --> 00:50:05.250
that could give you a
little bit more detail,

00:50:05.250 --> 00:50:06.180
but that comes with a cost,

00:50:06.180 --> 00:50:07.590
those are pretty expensive devices.

00:50:07.590 --> 00:50:09.337
But if it's, listen,
if you were an athlete,

00:50:09.337 --> 00:50:12.862
the $200, $300 it cost
to have one of those

00:50:12.862 --> 00:50:15.652
would be well worth the added investment.

00:50:15.652 --> 00:50:17.970
- And I'm sure some of our
listeners will want one too,

00:50:17.970 --> 00:50:20.256
'cause there are a lot
of tech geeks out there,

00:50:20.256 --> 00:50:24.480
not tech industry geeks, but
people who like tech gear.

00:50:24.480 --> 00:50:25.320
What's it called again?

00:50:25.320 --> 00:50:26.790
- It's a hand grip dynamometer.

00:50:26.790 --> 00:50:29.523
- Hand grip dynamometer.
- Dynamometer.

00:50:29.523 --> 00:50:32.250
Said by Jeff with a
great East Coast accent,

00:50:32.250 --> 00:50:36.090
and by me in a terrible
botched at West Coast version.

00:50:36.090 --> 00:50:39.390
Thank you, well, I'll put
that in the show notes also.

00:50:39.390 --> 00:50:42.990
Now I think recovery is key,
we always hear about sleep.

00:50:42.990 --> 00:50:45.480
You grow when you sleep and
incidentally your brain,

00:50:45.480 --> 00:50:48.270
you stimulate learning when
you're awake obviously,

00:50:48.270 --> 00:50:51.240
but the reordering of neural
connections happens in sleep.

00:50:51.240 --> 00:50:53.640
This is why sleep is
the way to get smarter,

00:50:53.640 --> 00:50:55.530
provided you're also
doing the learning part.

00:50:55.530 --> 00:50:56.700
The sleep's the way to get stronger

00:50:56.700 --> 00:50:58.560
provided you're also
doing the training part.

00:50:58.560 --> 00:50:59.790
You've had some really,

00:50:59.790 --> 00:51:01.740
you've put out interesting
content over the years

00:51:01.740 --> 00:51:04.380
in terms of even sleep position.

00:51:04.380 --> 00:51:08.340
One of the major changes that
I made to my sleep behavior

00:51:08.340 --> 00:51:11.670
is to not have the sheets
tucked in at the end of the bed.

00:51:11.670 --> 00:51:12.503
And I'll tell you,

00:51:12.503 --> 00:51:14.130
this had a profound
impact on several things.

00:51:14.130 --> 00:51:14.963
First of all,

00:51:14.963 --> 00:51:16.620
my feet have always been
the bane of my existence,

00:51:16.620 --> 00:51:18.307
broke them a bunch skateboarding.

00:51:18.307 --> 00:51:21.390
And I noticed when I'd
run, I'd get shin splints.

00:51:21.390 --> 00:51:23.890
And then I started to
notice that my feet sort of,

00:51:24.750 --> 00:51:26.622
you're the PT, they were kind of floppy,

00:51:26.622 --> 00:51:30.312
as if I was pointing my toes
slightly all the time at rest.

00:51:30.312 --> 00:51:34.530
And I realized that based on
listening to you previously,

00:51:34.530 --> 00:51:36.985
that my sheets were wrapped
tight, not hotel tight.

00:51:36.985 --> 00:51:38.100
- Right.

00:51:38.100 --> 00:51:39.271
- What their thing in the hotels.

00:51:39.271 --> 00:51:43.680
And I started releasing the
sheets at the end of the bed,

00:51:43.680 --> 00:51:45.957
and I also started doing
some tibialis work.

00:51:45.957 --> 00:51:49.920
Front of Shin's work
essentially, changed everything.

00:51:49.920 --> 00:51:52.890
My back pain from running
my shin splints disappeared,

00:51:52.890 --> 00:51:53.910
my posture improved,

00:51:53.910 --> 00:51:55.777
although my audience will tell me

00:51:55.777 --> 00:51:56.610
that it still needs improvement.

00:51:56.610 --> 00:51:58.500
They're always five or 10 people that-

00:51:58.500 --> 00:52:01.560
I've actually had chairs
sent to our mailing address.

00:52:01.560 --> 00:52:02.580
Very nice chairs, right?

00:52:02.580 --> 00:52:04.473
So I'm trying there.

00:52:05.400 --> 00:52:07.035
But this is fascinating, right?

00:52:07.035 --> 00:52:09.750
The position that one sleeps in,

00:52:09.750 --> 00:52:12.120
I fortunately have never
had any shoulder issues

00:52:12.120 --> 00:52:14.070
knock on wood, but maybe you could just

00:52:14.070 --> 00:52:17.880
talk to us a little bit about
sleep and sleep position

00:52:17.880 --> 00:52:19.817
for sake of waking position and movement.

00:52:19.817 --> 00:52:22.140
'Cause this, I think is a very unique

00:52:22.140 --> 00:52:24.762
and very powerful way
to think about sleep.

00:52:24.762 --> 00:52:27.450
This podcast has done
a lot of episodes about

00:52:27.450 --> 00:52:28.710
keeping the room cool,

00:52:28.710 --> 00:52:29.970
getting sunlight in your eyes, et cetera,

00:52:29.970 --> 00:52:31.620
how to get into sleep.

00:52:31.620 --> 00:52:34.470
But you've talked about physically,

00:52:34.470 --> 00:52:36.540
what positions might
be better to sleep in.

00:52:36.540 --> 00:52:39.450
So please, please enrich us.

00:52:39.450 --> 00:52:41.040
- I mean, first of all,

00:52:41.040 --> 00:52:44.678
some people's opinions of
that type of content is that,

00:52:44.678 --> 00:52:47.550
you sleep in the position
that's most comfortable

00:52:47.550 --> 00:52:49.260
so you ensure that you're sleeping.

00:52:49.260 --> 00:52:52.110
Oh great, I understand
that we all want to sleep,

00:52:52.110 --> 00:52:53.460
that's the goal when we
put our head on the pillow,

00:52:53.460 --> 00:52:55.140
is to actually fall asleep
and wake up in the morning

00:52:55.140 --> 00:52:56.910
and not know what the hell
happened, unless you had a dream.

00:52:56.910 --> 00:52:59.040
But you know beyond that,

00:52:59.040 --> 00:53:02.490
there are certainly physical
components to sleep.

00:53:02.490 --> 00:53:05.493
That is why a lot of times
people will wake up and say like

00:53:05.493 --> 00:53:08.730
that you can incur pretty
serious injuries in sleep.

00:53:08.730 --> 00:53:10.650
People will wake up and
have like a shoulder

00:53:10.650 --> 00:53:12.300
that did not bother them at all,

00:53:12.300 --> 00:53:15.750
be humming the next day
or even for weeks after,

00:53:15.750 --> 00:53:18.030
because of the one sleep
position they put themselves in,

00:53:18.030 --> 00:53:19.530
in a prolonged way.

00:53:19.530 --> 00:53:20.820
And they happened to have a deep sleep,

00:53:20.820 --> 00:53:22.556
even through the discomfort.

00:53:22.556 --> 00:53:25.830
That can do actually some damage.

00:53:25.830 --> 00:53:28.710
So it's understandable
that the body can incur

00:53:28.710 --> 00:53:31.710
some strain and stress if you're
sleeping in the wrong way.

00:53:31.710 --> 00:53:33.930
One of the things I say
right off the bat is,

00:53:33.930 --> 00:53:34.830
sleeping on your stomach

00:53:34.830 --> 00:53:36.810
just doesn't really have many benefits.

00:53:36.810 --> 00:53:40.830
You're putting yourself
into a position that is,

00:53:40.830 --> 00:53:45.150
depending upon the
orientation of your mattress

00:53:45.150 --> 00:53:46.470
or how many pillows you're using,

00:53:46.470 --> 00:53:48.660
but you're basically
putting yourselves into

00:53:48.660 --> 00:53:51.720
excessive extension of the lumbar spine,

00:53:51.720 --> 00:53:53.760
which for most people, isn't very good,

00:53:53.760 --> 00:53:57.721
if you're a disc patient, I
guess that might be helpful,

00:53:57.721 --> 00:53:59.370
for relocating the disc.

00:53:59.370 --> 00:54:00.795
But I mean, for the most part,

00:54:00.795 --> 00:54:03.540
your hands are then
usually not at your sides,

00:54:03.540 --> 00:54:04.890
but they're up under your arms,

00:54:04.890 --> 00:54:07.110
so you've got them into
sort of internal rotation up

00:54:07.110 --> 00:54:08.520
over elevation in your head.

00:54:08.520 --> 00:54:10.200
It's just not a great position.

00:54:10.200 --> 00:54:12.210
You also have to crank your
neck for one side or the other

00:54:12.210 --> 00:54:14.430
in order to breathe, or you're
going to your face down,

00:54:14.430 --> 00:54:15.540
straight into the pillow.

00:54:15.540 --> 00:54:17.730
So I would skip that one.

00:54:17.730 --> 00:54:20.520
And there's some people that
are total belly sleepers.

00:54:20.520 --> 00:54:22.050
And I would just say, listen,

00:54:22.050 --> 00:54:25.080
I don't think that is the most helpful,

00:54:25.080 --> 00:54:26.550
long term way for you to sleep.

00:54:26.550 --> 00:54:28.388
Try to adopt a different position.

00:54:28.388 --> 00:54:32.460
Sleeping on your side oftentimes is,

00:54:32.460 --> 00:54:34.380
is also brought along with that,

00:54:34.380 --> 00:54:36.960
the legs knees coming
up towards the chest,

00:54:36.960 --> 00:54:38.070
prolonged hip flexion.

00:54:38.070 --> 00:54:40.246
Listen, we're doing enough
of that during the day.

00:54:40.246 --> 00:54:41.820
- What we're doing right now.

00:54:41.820 --> 00:54:43.200
- We don't need to do another 10 hours

00:54:43.200 --> 00:54:45.150
or eight hours or something
at night like that.

00:54:45.150 --> 00:54:49.339
And it just is reinforcing,
and as we said too,

00:54:49.339 --> 00:54:51.540
let's say you trained that day,

00:54:51.540 --> 00:54:54.194
you're just reinforcing
muscle shortening overnight.

00:54:54.194 --> 00:54:57.330
Where the body is healing
and trying to create

00:54:57.330 --> 00:54:59.984
some changes in your body.

00:54:59.984 --> 00:55:02.460
One of the reasons why
I recommend stretching

00:55:02.460 --> 00:55:04.694
or static stretching
prior to going to bed,

00:55:04.694 --> 00:55:06.360
a lot of people don't really
want to do it at that point,

00:55:06.360 --> 00:55:08.610
'cause it could take 10
minutes, 5-10 minutes,

00:55:08.610 --> 00:55:10.230
depending upon how many
muscles you have to stretch.

00:55:10.230 --> 00:55:13.230
But it's good to sort of try to establish

00:55:13.230 --> 00:55:15.240
just longer length temporarily

00:55:15.240 --> 00:55:18.210
prior to going into a state where
you're going to be not moving

00:55:18.210 --> 00:55:22.530
and recovering and creating
new changes in the muscle.

00:55:22.530 --> 00:55:27.530
So, that kind of, I don't
say it doesn't rule out

00:55:27.540 --> 00:55:28.650
the side sleeper.

00:55:28.650 --> 00:55:30.360
The side sleeper could
be very, very helpful

00:55:30.360 --> 00:55:33.990
for somebody that has
apnea or other conditions.

00:55:33.990 --> 00:55:36.720
So again, it's not an
all or nothing approach,

00:55:36.720 --> 00:55:40.237
but it's something that you
need to pay attention to.

00:55:40.237 --> 00:55:41.910
When you are on your back,

00:55:41.910 --> 00:55:42.957
like you were talking about

00:55:42.957 --> 00:55:47.460
and your feet are wedged
underneath a tight sheets

00:55:47.460 --> 00:55:48.300
at the end of the bed.

00:55:48.300 --> 00:55:51.630
And most of us, unless we
consciously are pulling them up,

00:55:51.630 --> 00:55:53.670
don't prefer our beds to
have really loose sheets

00:55:53.670 --> 00:55:54.503
at the end of the bed.

00:55:54.503 --> 00:55:56.040
- It's hard to make
the bed in the morning.

00:55:56.040 --> 00:55:59.370
- So it's like you you're going
to want to have them tight.

00:55:59.370 --> 00:56:01.740
Well, I'm saying as you experienced,

00:56:01.740 --> 00:56:06.740
you're going to have these
prolonged plant reflection,

00:56:07.170 --> 00:56:12.170
that's going to likely lead
to shorter calves over time

00:56:12.267 --> 00:56:14.850
because you're lacking all that length

00:56:14.850 --> 00:56:16.980
for that long period of
time that you could have

00:56:16.980 --> 00:56:18.690
if you just loosened up the sheets

00:56:18.690 --> 00:56:21.150
and allowed your feet to
just hang out where they are.

00:56:21.150 --> 00:56:23.160
Now, the resting position of the ankle

00:56:23.160 --> 00:56:24.330
is not endorse reflection,

00:56:24.330 --> 00:56:26.040
it's going to be still
in some plain reflection,

00:56:26.040 --> 00:56:27.540
but not being driven down

00:56:27.540 --> 00:56:29.070
and pulled down into that position.

00:56:29.070 --> 00:56:32.070
And I think what happens
actually is people who get

00:56:32.070 --> 00:56:34.710
uncomfortable that way,
even in their sleep

00:56:34.710 --> 00:56:36.090
will shift away from that

00:56:36.090 --> 00:56:37.890
by turning either onto
their side of their stomach.

00:56:37.890 --> 00:56:41.160
So there's definitely an
impact of the body position

00:56:41.160 --> 00:56:43.620
and sleep in figuring out the best way

00:56:43.620 --> 00:56:46.620
that you can still sleep of
course, and get your rest,

00:56:46.620 --> 00:56:49.380
but have a mindful eye towards
what it's doing to your body

00:56:49.380 --> 00:56:53.460
and choose the one that's
least abrasive to your body

00:56:53.460 --> 00:56:55.050
is the way you should go.

00:56:55.050 --> 00:56:57.800
- That's terrific, and again,
it's really helped me and

00:56:58.913 --> 00:57:01.830
I'm a big believer based on good science

00:57:01.830 --> 00:57:04.770
out of Stanford and elsewhere that

00:57:04.770 --> 00:57:06.810
as much as we can be
nasal breathers in sleep,

00:57:06.810 --> 00:57:07.643
we probably should be.

00:57:07.643 --> 00:57:10.680
I don't know if you've
done any content yet about

00:57:10.680 --> 00:57:12.390
taping the mouth shut
with some medical tape,

00:57:12.390 --> 00:57:15.330
but you know the benefits
of nasal breathing in sleep

00:57:15.330 --> 00:57:16.163
are pretty tremendous,

00:57:16.163 --> 00:57:18.120
but it takes a little bit of
training for people to do,

00:57:18.120 --> 00:57:19.320
and the training is very simple.

00:57:19.320 --> 00:57:21.060
It's a little piece of medical tape.

00:57:21.060 --> 00:57:24.150
So again, a topic for another time.

00:57:24.150 --> 00:57:25.380
I'm glad you mentioned stretching,

00:57:25.380 --> 00:57:26.940
I was going to ask about
stretching a little bit later,

00:57:26.940 --> 00:57:28.473
but let's talk about stretching.

00:57:28.473 --> 00:57:30.780
When's the best time to stretch

00:57:30.780 --> 00:57:33.390
for particular types of results?

00:57:33.390 --> 00:57:34.230
And maybe you could define

00:57:34.230 --> 00:57:35.781
some of the different types of stretching.

00:57:35.781 --> 00:57:37.980
So you just mentioned a little bit of,

00:57:37.980 --> 00:57:40.985
what you call it, light stretching or?

00:57:40.985 --> 00:57:43.860
I'm completely naive here on stretching.

00:57:43.860 --> 00:57:45.330
So let me just say, I
can think of stretching

00:57:45.330 --> 00:57:49.590
where I hold the stretch and
really try and "lengthen"

00:57:49.590 --> 00:57:52.350
in air quotes folks, I don't
want the PTs jumping all over.

00:57:52.350 --> 00:57:53.183
I don't know what it is,

00:57:53.183 --> 00:57:55.530
but nutrition and the
PTs online are really,

00:57:55.530 --> 00:57:57.240
they've got pitchforks in both hands.

00:57:57.240 --> 00:57:58.700
Academics...

00:57:58.700 --> 00:58:00.441
- That's a recent evolution, I think.

00:58:00.441 --> 00:58:02.241
And not the nutrition as much,

00:58:02.241 --> 00:58:04.050
but the PTs have become a
little bit angry these days.

00:58:04.050 --> 00:58:06.660
- I see, well, I always say
with feelings of powerlessness

00:58:06.660 --> 00:58:07.940
comes aggression.

00:58:07.940 --> 00:58:09.570
Remember that folks.

00:58:09.570 --> 00:58:14.316
So in any case, they're
stretching where I'm

00:58:14.316 --> 00:58:19.316
trying to consciously
"lengthen," again in air quotes,

00:58:19.470 --> 00:58:22.260
the muscle I'm not yanking on the limb

00:58:22.260 --> 00:58:23.370
or bobbing up and down.

00:58:23.370 --> 00:58:24.960
Maybe you could define the
different types of stretching

00:58:24.960 --> 00:58:27.330
for people, maybe give
us some rough guidelines

00:58:27.330 --> 00:58:28.980
about what or not to do if cold or warm,

00:58:28.980 --> 00:58:31.110
before training, after
training, et cetera.

00:58:31.110 --> 00:58:32.880
- So, yeah, there's obviously

00:58:32.880 --> 00:58:34.440
there's a lot of different
types of stretching

00:58:34.440 --> 00:58:36.840
that could get even to P and F stretching

00:58:36.840 --> 00:58:39.990
and things that are a
little bit more niche.

00:58:39.990 --> 00:58:42.900
But in general, the two
basic forms of stretching

00:58:42.900 --> 00:58:44.910
are active stretching
and passive stretching,

00:58:44.910 --> 00:58:48.486
and your dynamic work.

00:58:48.486 --> 00:58:50.676
And your passive stretching is done

00:58:50.676 --> 00:58:53.946
with the goal of trying
to create an increase

00:58:53.946 --> 00:58:57.270
in the flexibility of the muscle.

00:58:57.270 --> 00:58:59.370
So whether you're actually
increasing the length

00:58:59.370 --> 00:59:01.590
of that muscle, more
so what you're doing is

00:59:01.590 --> 00:59:04.500
increasing the- or decreasing
the resistance of that muscle

00:59:04.500 --> 00:59:07.920
to want to stay at a certain
level of flexibility.

00:59:07.920 --> 00:59:10.620
So when we can sort of take the breaks off

00:59:10.620 --> 00:59:14.340
and allow that muscle to
allow us more range of motion,

00:59:14.340 --> 00:59:16.260
we're inherently increasing flexibility

00:59:16.260 --> 00:59:18.180
without necessarily having
to increase the length

00:59:18.180 --> 00:59:19.053
of that muscle.

00:59:20.400 --> 00:59:23.910
That is usually done at a time
far away from your workout,

00:59:23.910 --> 00:59:25.650
because they have shown where

00:59:25.650 --> 00:59:28.740
this type of stretching
done prior to an activity,

00:59:28.740 --> 00:59:33.690
and it could be like a
structured activity like lifting,

00:59:33.690 --> 00:59:35.760
or it could be a little
bit less structured,

00:59:35.760 --> 00:59:39.390
like competing in a sport
in a spontaneous type way,

00:59:39.390 --> 00:59:44.340
that there is a period of
recalibration that is needed

00:59:44.340 --> 00:59:45.660
after doing this,

00:59:45.660 --> 00:59:47.447
because you're disrupting the
length tension relationship

00:59:47.447 --> 00:59:51.810
of the muscle that causes
you to not necessarily

00:59:51.810 --> 00:59:54.600
be able to rely on these,
I've talked about before,

00:59:54.600 --> 00:59:57.240
stored motor engrams in
your mind in terms of,

00:59:57.240 --> 01:00:00.004
this is the pattern for how
I swing a golf club, say.

01:00:00.004 --> 01:00:03.900
And now introducing a
little bit of flexibility,

01:00:03.900 --> 01:00:05.430
or added flexibility, or range,

01:00:05.430 --> 01:00:07.410
because of the stretching I did before,

01:00:07.410 --> 01:00:11.538
it takes maybe a whole or two
or three to match up again.

01:00:11.538 --> 01:00:13.860
Oh, this is what he's trying to do,

01:00:13.860 --> 01:00:15.900
that golf swinging thing
that I remembered again,

01:00:15.900 --> 01:00:18.330
like it's not remembering
that every component,

01:00:18.330 --> 01:00:21.477
like I have to bend my
right wrist back 10 degrees,

01:00:21.477 --> 01:00:23.340
and then I have to bend my
elbow and I have to break,

01:00:23.340 --> 01:00:26.010
like your body stores these
patterns for motor efficiency.

01:00:26.010 --> 01:00:29.580
And when I have to start
matching up that stored pattern

01:00:29.580 --> 01:00:32.367
with what's feeling new
because of the increased range,

01:00:32.367 --> 01:00:33.870
I can impair performance.

01:00:33.870 --> 01:00:36.240
And again, it could happen
even in a gym workout

01:00:36.240 --> 01:00:37.260
where you're talking about your first,

01:00:37.260 --> 01:00:38.681
second set, third set,

01:00:38.681 --> 01:00:41.280
where maybe the
repercussions aren't as big,

01:00:41.280 --> 01:00:43.140
'cause I'll just do a few extra sets.

01:00:43.140 --> 01:00:45.270
But in performance, if you screw
up your first three rounds,

01:00:45.270 --> 01:00:46.740
you're playing on a PJ tour and you shoot

01:00:46.740 --> 01:00:49.770
your six over after three, you're done.

01:00:49.770 --> 01:00:51.920
So I think it matters there.

01:00:51.920 --> 01:00:56.160
As far as the dynamic, so we
relegate that as I mentioned,

01:00:56.160 --> 01:00:57.120
sort of towards the end of the day

01:00:57.120 --> 01:00:59.310
when it's not going to impact performance,

01:00:59.310 --> 01:01:03.171
but even maybe have the
additional benefit of creating

01:01:03.171 --> 01:01:06.900
the feeling of length or
the increase or decrease

01:01:06.900 --> 01:01:08.646
in resistance to this length

01:01:08.646 --> 01:01:11.400
at a time when I know
my body is going to try

01:01:11.400 --> 01:01:13.410
to tend to heal and heal shorter,

01:01:13.410 --> 01:01:14.940
never longer, but heal shorter.

01:01:14.940 --> 01:01:17.880
So if I can introduce a little
bit of that extra length

01:01:17.880 --> 01:01:20.100
or decreased resistance to that length,

01:01:20.100 --> 01:01:21.270
it's a better time to do it.

01:01:21.270 --> 01:01:24.680
So I think it promotes a better recovery.

01:01:24.680 --> 01:01:26.250
If I want to...

01:01:26.250 --> 01:01:28.728
- Sorry to interrupt, but so
stretching later in the day,

01:01:28.728 --> 01:01:33.090
because I'm intrigued by
this concept of heal shorter.

01:01:33.090 --> 01:01:34.860
So part of the healing
and recovery process

01:01:34.860 --> 01:01:36.030
means the shortening of the muscles.

01:01:36.030 --> 01:01:36.863
This is the tensing up in sleep.

01:01:36.863 --> 01:01:38.610
- Yes, yeah.
- Could you elaborate

01:01:38.610 --> 01:01:40.530
just a bit on that and then
sorry to break your flow,

01:01:40.530 --> 01:01:41.363
but then to continue...

01:01:41.363 --> 01:01:44.070
- No, just basically,
what's been shown is that,

01:01:44.070 --> 01:01:48.330
when the repair process,
muscular repair from,

01:01:48.330 --> 01:01:50.160
let's say strength
training during the day,

01:01:50.160 --> 01:01:52.470
the repair process usually
results in a muscle

01:01:52.470 --> 01:01:56.190
that is slightly shorter rather
than increased in length.

01:01:56.190 --> 01:01:59.490
It's just, muscles prefer to sort of

01:01:59.490 --> 01:02:03.540
ratchet their way down
into that contraction,

01:02:03.540 --> 01:02:07.170
and then maintain that more comfortable

01:02:07.170 --> 01:02:08.400
length tension relationship.

01:02:08.400 --> 01:02:09.990
So when you're sleeping,

01:02:09.990 --> 01:02:12.510
it tends to air on the side of shorter

01:02:12.510 --> 01:02:15.090
rather than longer, when ideally
we don't really want that.

01:02:15.090 --> 01:02:18.000
We want to maintain as much of that length

01:02:18.000 --> 01:02:21.390
because with more length, we
actually have more leverage.

01:02:21.390 --> 01:02:23.280
That muscle has more leverage to contract.

01:02:23.280 --> 01:02:24.840
If it was all the way contracted,

01:02:24.840 --> 01:02:27.000
you really can't obviously
we know generate much force

01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:29.640
in a muscle that's already
maximally contracted.

01:02:29.640 --> 01:02:33.900
So I think we want to do something
that we, whatever we can,

01:02:33.900 --> 01:02:35.730
whatever little weapons
we have in our arsenal

01:02:35.730 --> 01:02:39.570
that could allow us to
do this prior to sleep.

01:02:39.570 --> 01:02:42.420
And again, it's just making
it a conscious choice

01:02:42.420 --> 01:02:43.410
to do it at a time of the day

01:02:43.410 --> 01:02:45.160
that makes a little bit more sense.

01:02:46.020 --> 01:02:48.780
Dynamic stretching is really
not done for that purpose

01:02:48.780 --> 01:02:53.780
of trying to create any
type of feeling of act

01:02:54.030 --> 01:02:56.400
or increasing the potential length

01:02:56.400 --> 01:02:57.450
as you said of the muscle,

01:02:57.450 --> 01:03:00.120
but more so the readiness
of the muscle to perform.

01:03:00.120 --> 01:03:04.400
And increasing exploring the
ends of that range of motion

01:03:04.400 --> 01:03:07.620
in a more dynamic way so
you're not hanging out there

01:03:07.620 --> 01:03:09.387
in disrupting that length
tension relationship

01:03:09.387 --> 01:03:12.840
but just sort of touching
the ends of those barriers

01:03:12.840 --> 01:03:16.380
so that when you feel movement
again, it feels looser,

01:03:16.380 --> 01:03:17.430
it feels more ready.

01:03:17.430 --> 01:03:20.880
And obviously at the same
time warming up blood flow,

01:03:20.880 --> 01:03:23.610
all the benefits we get from
just warming up in general.

01:03:23.610 --> 01:03:25.620
So like that's the series

01:03:25.620 --> 01:03:27.524
you've probably seen a
bunch of times but like

01:03:27.524 --> 01:03:32.524
leg swings and butt kicks
and lunge, walking lunges

01:03:33.090 --> 01:03:34.890
and all types of-
- Toe touches.

01:03:34.890 --> 01:03:36.840
- Toe touches, all those kind of drills,

01:03:36.840 --> 01:03:37.980
those active stretching drills

01:03:37.980 --> 01:03:41.010
or lunging with rotations
for the upper body

01:03:41.010 --> 01:03:43.200
to try to get some of the
thoracic spine involved too.

01:03:43.200 --> 01:03:46.110
Those are the drills that
people will do prior to training

01:03:46.110 --> 01:03:47.670
that are both excitatory

01:03:47.670 --> 01:03:49.410
in terms of just the nervous system

01:03:49.410 --> 01:03:52.590
but also helpful for just
the general warm up the body

01:03:52.590 --> 01:03:53.640
'cause the blood flow.

01:03:53.640 --> 01:03:55.440
But from a muscle readiness standpoint

01:03:55.440 --> 01:03:58.470
not impairing the performance

01:03:58.470 --> 01:04:01.440
while at the same time
exploring the increased ranges

01:04:01.440 --> 01:04:04.170
'cause as you know the
first toe touch you do

01:04:04.170 --> 01:04:05.547
is not as high as the
last toe touch you do.

01:04:05.547 --> 01:04:07.819
- For me it doesn't even include the toe.

01:04:07.819 --> 01:04:10.710
- The shin touch.
- The touch attempt.

01:04:10.710 --> 01:04:12.780
- So like those are going to improve

01:04:12.780 --> 01:04:14.430
with each subsequent rep

01:04:14.430 --> 01:04:16.560
and I think that's what people actually,

01:04:16.560 --> 01:04:18.000
when you can see those,

01:04:18.000 --> 01:04:21.630
those actual changes from
rep one to rep seven,

01:04:21.630 --> 01:04:22.500
you just feel ready,

01:04:22.500 --> 01:04:25.230
you feel more alert and
ready to go in your workout.

01:04:25.230 --> 01:04:29.137
So the dynamic type of stretching
and I mentioned earlier on

01:04:29.137 --> 01:04:33.210
what I've had to do to sort
of increase my warm up focus.

01:04:33.210 --> 01:04:35.496
I think that's more of what
I try to do these days.

01:04:35.496 --> 01:04:38.313
I try to be a little bit
more alert to the fact that,

01:04:39.241 --> 01:04:40.650
my body's not ready.

01:04:40.650 --> 01:04:42.720
When I was working with
Antonio Brown I remember like

01:04:42.720 --> 01:04:46.995
he would spend 20 minutes, 30
minutes on all dynamic work.

01:04:46.995 --> 01:04:49.620
And I've never seen
anybody spend that long

01:04:49.620 --> 01:04:50.670
on their dynamic work.

01:04:50.670 --> 01:04:54.660
But like he said, he just
didn't feel right and ready

01:04:54.660 --> 01:04:56.970
to go unless he did a lot of that.

01:04:56.970 --> 01:05:00.210
And I mean his dynamic stretching routine

01:05:00.210 --> 01:05:02.555
would be a workout for most everybody.

01:05:02.555 --> 01:05:04.620
And it's crazy how much he did.

01:05:04.620 --> 01:05:06.180
- These pro athletes are amazing.

01:05:06.180 --> 01:05:08.970
And you've had the great
fortune of working with

01:05:08.970 --> 01:05:11.253
and improving their abilities.

01:05:12.180 --> 01:05:13.950
But I can only imagine

01:05:13.950 --> 01:05:16.710
'cause I also imagine he is
pretty strong in the gym also.

01:05:16.710 --> 01:05:19.140
- I mean, it always amazes me

01:05:19.140 --> 01:05:20.520
the guys that make it to that level

01:05:20.520 --> 01:05:22.627
no matter what sport they do.

01:05:22.627 --> 01:05:24.420
They're so gifted in everything.

01:05:24.420 --> 01:05:27.330
Like David Wright used to
make me laugh all the time

01:05:27.330 --> 01:05:30.183
with the Mets because no matter what I,

01:05:31.020 --> 01:05:33.030
Ping pong, like anything

01:05:33.030 --> 01:05:34.500
because of his hand-eye coordination.

01:05:34.500 --> 01:05:37.140
Like anything great at.

01:05:37.140 --> 01:05:37.973
Jump rope.

01:05:37.973 --> 01:05:39.395
I remember he hadn't
done a lot of jump rope

01:05:39.395 --> 01:05:43.230
and I think jump rope's
one of the best things

01:05:43.230 --> 01:05:44.910
you could do from a
conditioning standpoint.

01:05:44.910 --> 01:05:47.556
It's actually fairly
interesting, it's not just,

01:05:47.556 --> 01:05:51.090
it's not too harsh on the joints

01:05:51.090 --> 01:05:54.450
even though it's a ballistic
move and he wasn't-

01:05:54.450 --> 01:05:56.250
I have to admit, if he listens to this,

01:05:56.250 --> 01:05:57.083
he's going to want to kill me,

01:05:57.083 --> 01:05:59.460
but I was better at him at jump roping.

01:05:59.460 --> 01:06:00.508
One of the only things I could do.

01:06:00.508 --> 01:06:02.910
And then I gave him about five days

01:06:02.910 --> 01:06:04.710
and he completely blew me out of the water

01:06:04.710 --> 01:06:06.900
to the point where I could
never keep up with him anymore.

01:06:06.900 --> 01:06:08.280
He made it look effortless.

01:06:08.280 --> 01:06:11.160
It's like that's where the
athlete in someone comes out.

01:06:11.160 --> 01:06:13.350
No matter what they pick
up, they're good at it.

01:06:13.350 --> 01:06:15.630
And I think that when you
see guys like this in the gym

01:06:15.630 --> 01:06:17.700
like their strength levels
tend to be pretty damn good

01:06:17.700 --> 01:06:21.000
and their abilities, their
coordination, their everything

01:06:21.000 --> 01:06:22.800
just tends to sort of
be good at that level.

01:06:22.800 --> 01:06:24.390
And it sort of amazes me.

01:06:24.390 --> 01:06:28.288
Why those guys can go pick up
a golf club and go shoot 72

01:06:28.288 --> 01:06:30.923
and having never really played.

01:06:30.923 --> 01:06:33.960
They're just naturally
good at whatever they do.

01:06:33.960 --> 01:06:34.800
- Yeah, I have a couple,

01:06:34.800 --> 01:06:37.500
I'm smiling 'cause I have a
couple really close friends

01:06:37.500 --> 01:06:38.820
who did a number of years

01:06:38.820 --> 01:06:40.800
some several decades in the SEAL teams.

01:06:40.800 --> 01:06:43.350
And I don't know that their
skill level at everything

01:06:43.350 --> 01:06:45.570
is so high as you're
describing for athletes

01:06:45.570 --> 01:06:48.510
but their level of
competitiveness is beyond.

01:06:48.510 --> 01:06:49.590
I ocean swam with one,

01:06:49.590 --> 01:06:52.380
there's no chance that I'm
going to right out swim Pat

01:06:52.380 --> 01:06:53.490
ever, ever.

01:06:53.490 --> 01:06:55.020
He actually goes back and forth sometimes

01:06:55.020 --> 01:06:57.510
just to check up on
me, which I appreciate.

01:06:57.510 --> 01:06:58.950
Thank you Pat.

01:06:58.950 --> 01:07:00.120
I haven't drowned yet.

01:07:00.120 --> 01:07:03.570
But in addition to that,
we could play horseshoes

01:07:03.570 --> 01:07:05.670
and it's like this switch
that just flips on,

01:07:05.670 --> 01:07:07.412
like he's going to murder me.

01:07:07.412 --> 01:07:08.640
Of course he's a very nice guy, right?

01:07:08.640 --> 01:07:09.840
In general, they tend to be very nice.

01:07:09.840 --> 01:07:12.540
But the level of competitiveness
is kind of unreal.

01:07:12.540 --> 01:07:13.440
They're selected for it.

01:07:13.440 --> 01:07:14.430
- They're trying to beat themselves

01:07:14.430 --> 01:07:15.330
they're not even trying to beat you.

01:07:15.330 --> 01:07:16.836
- That's right, I'm not
even in the competition.

01:07:16.836 --> 01:07:18.990
- You're not even there.
- Yeah, exactly.

01:07:18.990 --> 01:07:19.823
Thank you.

01:07:20.784 --> 01:07:24.060
Now I won't feel so bad or worse.

01:07:24.060 --> 01:07:25.440
It's true, it's a remarkable thing.

01:07:25.440 --> 01:07:26.730
I'm glad you mentioned jump roping.

01:07:26.730 --> 01:07:29.876
I used to skip rope for warmup for boxing,

01:07:29.876 --> 01:07:33.356
like three minute rounds
or something like that.

01:07:33.356 --> 01:07:36.066
But I'm glad you brought it up because

01:07:36.066 --> 01:07:38.466
skipping rope is something that

01:07:38.466 --> 01:07:40.650
obviously has a cardiovascular component,

01:07:40.650 --> 01:07:42.360
there's the conditioning
component, there's timing

01:07:42.360 --> 01:07:44.190
and it is kind of interesting, right?

01:07:44.190 --> 01:07:45.901
It's frustrating when you don't get it

01:07:45.901 --> 01:07:47.160
especially when it whips you on the air

01:07:47.160 --> 01:07:48.510
if you're using a proper rope.

01:07:48.510 --> 01:07:50.850
I'm just curious if you
could just give us a quick

01:07:50.850 --> 01:07:51.710
skipping rope 101.

01:07:51.710 --> 01:07:54.810
Do you like to see people
jumping with both feet and toes?

01:07:54.810 --> 01:07:57.720
We'll link to a video if
there was one and I missed it.

01:07:57.720 --> 01:07:59.160
Do you like to see
people doing high knees?

01:07:59.160 --> 01:08:01.980
Do you like people
basically like shuffling?

01:08:01.980 --> 01:08:03.240
You want to see people doing double Dutch?

01:08:03.240 --> 01:08:05.520
What do you want to see
people doing over time?

01:08:05.520 --> 01:08:07.260
- All of the above maybe
not the double Dutch,

01:08:07.260 --> 01:08:08.370
but all of the above.

01:08:08.370 --> 01:08:10.590
I mean, I think that's the
cool thing about it, right?

01:08:10.590 --> 01:08:13.380
Like once we sort of master
the skill 'cause for all of us,

01:08:13.380 --> 01:08:16.380
that first jump with the
two feet going together

01:08:16.380 --> 01:08:17.213
is a challenge.

01:08:17.213 --> 01:08:18.510
'Cause you just got to time that rope,

01:08:18.510 --> 01:08:19.680
you got to time your jump

01:08:19.680 --> 01:08:22.320
and then we get bored as
we often do as humans,

01:08:22.320 --> 01:08:23.307
we get bored with what we can do

01:08:23.307 --> 01:08:24.990
and we want to take on new challenges

01:08:24.990 --> 01:08:26.610
so then it becomes one leg at a time

01:08:26.610 --> 01:08:29.520
or then it becomes side
to side hops, right?

01:08:29.520 --> 01:08:32.730
All of those things are
beneficial I believe

01:08:32.730 --> 01:08:34.650
neurologically to enhancing the ability

01:08:34.650 --> 01:08:38.010
to do the skill as a whole
but also just because

01:08:38.010 --> 01:08:41.010
I'm such a believer in
training in all three planes.

01:08:41.010 --> 01:08:43.620
So like just doing straight up and down

01:08:43.620 --> 01:08:46.710
versus now I can do frontal
plane side to side motion

01:08:46.710 --> 01:08:48.780
and then I can even do small little twists

01:08:48.780 --> 01:08:50.247
or core screws you call them.

01:08:50.247 --> 01:08:51.840
It requires a different,

01:08:51.840 --> 01:08:53.103
you would know more better than I do

01:08:53.103 --> 01:08:56.610
that it requires different
neurological patterns

01:08:56.610 --> 01:08:59.250
to be able to coordinate
that because you're changing

01:08:59.250 --> 01:09:00.720
the orientation of your body in space.

01:09:00.720 --> 01:09:03.120
So it's not just them
changing the exercise

01:09:03.120 --> 01:09:06.000
but I'm changing how my body
interprets that exercise

01:09:06.000 --> 01:09:07.950
because what's happening
to my body in space.

01:09:07.950 --> 01:09:12.330
So I love whatever people wind up doing,

01:09:12.330 --> 01:09:15.570
but I am amazed there are people,

01:09:15.570 --> 01:09:17.790
I just started following
this young woman on Instagram

01:09:17.790 --> 01:09:21.294
who is like, I'll give her a plug out,

01:09:21.294 --> 01:09:22.350
I think it's like @anna.skips or something

01:09:22.350 --> 01:09:24.960
and she is ridiculous.

01:09:24.960 --> 01:09:27.000
Like I watch her and I'm like mesmerized

01:09:27.000 --> 01:09:28.620
at what she can do with the rope.

01:09:28.620 --> 01:09:31.710
It's like is an extremely
athletic endeavor

01:09:31.710 --> 01:09:33.270
believe when it gets to be at that level

01:09:33.270 --> 01:09:35.283
and the speed and the precision and the-

01:09:37.071 --> 01:09:39.949
I think one of the goals that
you want to be able to have is

01:09:39.949 --> 01:09:42.514
to where you're feeling
as if you're almost

01:09:42.514 --> 01:09:45.270
effortlessly dancing without a rope.

01:09:45.270 --> 01:09:47.220
Like where you're just
bouncing off of the ball

01:09:47.220 --> 01:09:48.359
of your foot.

01:09:48.359 --> 01:09:51.000
And it's an important skill to learn too

01:09:51.000 --> 01:09:55.500
whether you go back to run
or even even jog, right?

01:09:55.500 --> 01:09:57.720
Just like more casual running,

01:09:57.720 --> 01:10:00.330
learning how to land is so important.

01:10:00.330 --> 01:10:03.510
One of the drills that
people should try is like,

01:10:03.510 --> 01:10:05.280
try to jump on your heels.

01:10:05.280 --> 01:10:07.950
So just stand up, pull your
toes off the ground, right?

01:10:07.950 --> 01:10:10.590
And just jump from your
heels and land on your heels.

01:10:10.590 --> 01:10:12.150
You'll feel it in your jaw.

01:10:12.150 --> 01:10:13.740
You'll literally feel your jaw rattle

01:10:13.740 --> 01:10:14.880
when you land on your heels.

01:10:14.880 --> 01:10:16.980
There is no shock absorption capabilities

01:10:16.980 --> 01:10:17.940
through your heels.

01:10:17.940 --> 01:10:21.420
Meantime, a lot of people
land on their heels a lot

01:10:21.420 --> 01:10:23.460
when they run and you're just,

01:10:23.460 --> 01:10:25.590
your body's not built to absorb the forces

01:10:25.590 --> 01:10:27.000
like the ball of your foot could.

01:10:27.000 --> 01:10:29.010
It's really built as a spring.

01:10:29.010 --> 01:10:31.980
And the foot is a, to me
as a physical therapist,

01:10:31.980 --> 01:10:33.780
the foot has always been
one of the most amazing,

01:10:33.780 --> 01:10:36.420
you talk about having
bad feet I have flat feet

01:10:36.420 --> 01:10:39.030
it looks like I got flippers
if I took my shoes off.

01:10:39.030 --> 01:10:41.151
Like I'm wearing scuba fins.

01:10:41.151 --> 01:10:43.110
There is no-

01:10:43.110 --> 01:10:47.310
There is no adaptability of
that foot to the surface.

01:10:47.310 --> 01:10:50.220
When it's completely caved
and flattened like that,

01:10:50.220 --> 01:10:53.400
the job of the foot is to be a adaptable.

01:10:53.400 --> 01:10:55.260
Well, maybe there is some adaptability

01:10:55.260 --> 01:10:58.140
because it's so floppy, but
at the same time at some point

01:10:58.140 --> 01:11:00.690
that critical junction when
you're going to then step through

01:11:00.690 --> 01:11:02.550
and you need to be able to push off

01:11:02.550 --> 01:11:05.700
the foot has to actually
changes in the midfoot itself

01:11:05.700 --> 01:11:08.370
to become a rigid lever
is what they call it.

01:11:08.370 --> 01:11:11.640
You're going from a mobile
adapter to a rigid lever.

01:11:11.640 --> 01:11:14.790
That rigid lever literally
locks up the metatarsal joint

01:11:14.790 --> 01:11:16.620
to become solid

01:11:16.620 --> 01:11:18.990
so that you can push
off of it with leverage.

01:11:18.990 --> 01:11:21.030
If you lack that capability,

01:11:21.030 --> 01:11:23.700
all those stresses that are
supposed to be born by the foot,

01:11:23.700 --> 01:11:25.860
go up into the ankle, into
the knee, into the hip,

01:11:25.860 --> 01:11:27.060
into the low back.

01:11:27.060 --> 01:11:32.060
So learning how to land and
start to train your body

01:11:32.123 --> 01:11:36.444
to experience ground
reaction forces the right way

01:11:36.444 --> 01:11:40.197
is so critical to all other function

01:11:40.197 --> 01:11:43.170
and all other disability
of the kinetic chain

01:11:43.170 --> 01:11:45.390
and jumping rope is like
one of the best ways

01:11:45.390 --> 01:11:46.440
to learn how to do that.

01:11:46.440 --> 01:11:47.490
- Great.

01:11:47.490 --> 01:11:50.430
I own a jump rope I love
doing it in the morning

01:11:50.430 --> 01:11:51.870
while I get sunlight in my eyes.

01:11:51.870 --> 01:11:55.680
It's actually a protocol I
picked up from Tim Ferris

01:11:55.680 --> 01:11:58.410
who mentioned 'cause
listeners in my podcast know

01:11:58.410 --> 01:12:01.170
I'm like a broken record with
get sunlight in your eyes

01:12:01.170 --> 01:12:03.990
even through cloud cover it's
just sets your sleep rhythms

01:12:03.990 --> 01:12:06.657
and your waking rhythms
of yada yada on and on.

01:12:06.657 --> 01:12:09.300
But sometimes it'd be
kind of boring for people

01:12:09.300 --> 01:12:10.500
and I want to get them off their phone.

01:12:10.500 --> 01:12:12.750
So jumping rope is also
just a great way to wake up.

01:12:12.750 --> 01:12:17.727
So jumping rope can be the cardio workout,

01:12:17.727 --> 01:12:20.040
the 15, 30 minutes.
- Definitely.

01:12:20.040 --> 01:12:21.180
And there's sort of that hybrid

01:12:21.180 --> 01:12:23.436
that we were talking about before of like,

01:12:23.436 --> 01:12:25.650
you're not necessarily
dropping down to the ground

01:12:25.650 --> 01:12:26.483
and doing burpees,

01:12:26.483 --> 01:12:29.070
but I just look at it as
a more athletic endeavor

01:12:29.070 --> 01:12:30.900
because of the coordination involved

01:12:30.900 --> 01:12:33.150
than just simply walking or jogging.

01:12:33.150 --> 01:12:36.420
- Yeah, and it's not much
of equipment requirement.

01:12:36.420 --> 01:12:38.070
Very minimal cost.

01:12:38.070 --> 01:12:41.370
You could even use a rope
or something if you...

01:12:41.370 --> 01:12:43.770
- We even instruct people
they could use no rope

01:12:43.770 --> 01:12:46.770
and just pretend and just
move the arms, right?

01:12:46.770 --> 01:12:48.037
- Truly zero cost.

01:12:48.037 --> 01:12:49.140
- You're never going to
hit the rope which is good

01:12:49.140 --> 01:12:50.130
but at the same time,

01:12:50.130 --> 01:12:51.660
So you're never going to know
if you're doing it wrong,

01:12:51.660 --> 01:12:53.580
but at least you can move through that

01:12:53.580 --> 01:12:55.290
and get the same benefits
through the feet.

01:12:55.290 --> 01:12:56.861
- I love it, I love it.

01:12:56.861 --> 01:13:00.000
I told myself before
sitting down with you today

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:02.610
that I wasn't going to
focus on specific exercises

01:13:02.610 --> 01:13:05.370
because there's such a
wealth of incredible content

01:13:05.370 --> 01:13:06.203
that you put out there

01:13:06.203 --> 01:13:08.400
that people could just put
into YouTube or elsewhere

01:13:08.400 --> 01:13:12.240
and arrive at the proper
way to do a chin or a dip,

01:13:12.240 --> 01:13:13.440
for whatever purpose.

01:13:13.440 --> 01:13:16.920
But there's one exercise
and one particular motion

01:13:16.920 --> 01:13:19.380
that I'd like to discuss for a moment

01:13:19.380 --> 01:13:21.163
because I believe that

01:13:21.163 --> 01:13:24.674
learning about this
cautionary note from you

01:13:24.674 --> 01:13:26.820
is one of the reasons

01:13:26.820 --> 01:13:29.970
that I've maintained steady
training for 30 years

01:13:29.970 --> 01:13:33.060
with no major injury knock on wood

01:13:33.060 --> 01:13:34.653
and that's the upright row.

01:13:35.738 --> 01:13:38.930
One thing that whether
you weight train or not.

01:13:38.930 --> 01:13:40.862
- Do we censor this podcast?

01:13:40.862 --> 01:13:41.970
I mean censoring, do we
beep this out or not?

01:13:41.970 --> 01:13:43.130
- I do.

01:13:43.130 --> 01:13:44.830
Do you get beef about this?
- No.

01:13:46.448 --> 01:13:48.600
You know what, we always get beef

01:13:48.600 --> 01:13:50.760
in any social media platform
where we're put out.

01:13:50.760 --> 01:13:52.890
But like, no, I guess
some, I get some from it,

01:13:52.890 --> 01:13:56.012
but I'm fully prepared to defend myself.

01:13:56.012 --> 01:13:58.470
- But here's the reason
for asking about this.

01:13:58.470 --> 01:14:00.840
I never really cared much for upright rows

01:14:00.840 --> 01:14:02.340
it's not an exercise I tend to do.

01:14:02.340 --> 01:14:05.340
But one thing that's apparent
in all my colleagues,

01:14:05.340 --> 01:14:08.370
in every child I see, in
every adult I see is that

01:14:08.370 --> 01:14:11.298
almost everybody is in
inward rotation now.

01:14:11.298 --> 01:14:12.930
So folks think if you stay,

01:14:12.930 --> 01:14:14.190
I think I learned this from you also,

01:14:14.190 --> 01:14:15.120
if you stand up straight

01:14:15.120 --> 01:14:16.762
and then you just point your thumbs out,

01:14:16.762 --> 01:14:19.590
like a thumbs up but your hands are down

01:14:19.590 --> 01:14:21.330
you're pointing your thumbs straight out,

01:14:21.330 --> 01:14:23.100
ideally they would go straight out.

01:14:23.100 --> 01:14:24.060
Most people the thumbs

01:14:24.060 --> 01:14:25.140
are going to be pointing
toward one another

01:14:25.140 --> 01:14:28.040
because most people are starting
to look somewhere between

01:14:29.486 --> 01:14:33.860
a non human primate and a melted candle.

01:14:35.575 --> 01:14:38.700
Bent at the hips, et cetera,
from too much sitting,

01:14:38.700 --> 01:14:40.920
we're all sitting, we're
in an inward rotation,

01:14:40.920 --> 01:14:43.500
but I learned from you
that the upright row

01:14:43.500 --> 01:14:48.180
compromises some important
aspects of our shoulder mechanics

01:14:48.180 --> 01:14:50.310
and could be actually sort
of a dangerous movement

01:14:50.310 --> 01:14:51.143
in some ways.

01:14:51.143 --> 01:14:53.040
I'm sure there's a safe
way for people to do it.

01:14:53.040 --> 01:14:55.770
But so I've always made it a point now

01:14:55.770 --> 01:14:59.790
on the basis of this advice
to A, not do upright rows,

01:14:59.790 --> 01:15:00.720
but I wasn't doing them before

01:15:00.720 --> 01:15:04.320
but to really strive for external rotation

01:15:04.320 --> 01:15:07.350
on things like bench dips, on
a number of different things.

01:15:07.350 --> 01:15:09.870
Whenever I can I try and
go into external rotation

01:15:09.870 --> 01:15:12.030
provided that without looking
like an idiot walking around

01:15:12.030 --> 01:15:13.770
with my palms facing outward.

01:15:13.770 --> 01:15:16.293
Please tell us about
internal external rotation.

01:15:17.700 --> 01:15:20.100
The upright row is one aspect of that,

01:15:20.100 --> 01:15:23.730
but why this is so important
not just for weight training,

01:15:23.730 --> 01:15:26.400
but as in terms of posture and mechanics

01:15:26.400 --> 01:15:29.730
and not looking like a melted candle

01:15:29.730 --> 01:15:31.740
or partially melted candle.

01:15:31.740 --> 01:15:32.573
- I actually love it.

01:15:32.573 --> 01:15:36.750
I'm happy to talk about it
cause I love the shoulder

01:15:36.750 --> 01:15:37.583
as a joint.

01:15:37.583 --> 01:15:39.930
I think PTs tend to fall
in love with certain areas

01:15:39.930 --> 01:15:41.790
and the shoulder is one
of the cool areas for me,

01:15:41.790 --> 01:15:43.590
it's like the foot is but like,

01:15:43.590 --> 01:15:47.910
the shoulder has the most
mobility in the body of any joint,

01:15:47.910 --> 01:15:49.530
but it's also got the
least stability, right?

01:15:49.530 --> 01:15:52.140
There's always that trade off
of mobility and stability.

01:15:52.140 --> 01:15:57.140
So your stability comes
from certain muscle groups

01:15:57.720 --> 01:15:59.970
and 101 is that the only muscle group

01:15:59.970 --> 01:16:02.040
that actually externally
rotates the shoulder

01:16:02.040 --> 01:16:03.690
is going to be the rotator cuff, okay.

01:16:03.690 --> 01:16:06.210
And unless you are devoted to training

01:16:06.210 --> 01:16:08.970
through external rotation
and exercises that

01:16:08.970 --> 01:16:11.430
are going to externally
rotate the shoulder,

01:16:11.430 --> 01:16:13.680
you're not training that function.

01:16:13.680 --> 01:16:17.040
And it's so easy for us in everyday life

01:16:17.040 --> 01:16:18.630
especially those that aren't training

01:16:18.630 --> 01:16:21.480
to not ever really undergo
any of those stresses

01:16:21.480 --> 01:16:23.580
that could be beneficial to counteracting

01:16:23.580 --> 01:16:25.830
what happens freely and naturally,

01:16:25.830 --> 01:16:27.150
which is internal rotation.

01:16:27.150 --> 01:16:29.160
So when you think about
the imbalance created

01:16:29.160 --> 01:16:31.470
just by nature and how we live our lives,

01:16:31.470 --> 01:16:35.880
internal rotation far, far, far
outweighs external rotation.

01:16:35.880 --> 01:16:37.650
So you need to address it.

01:16:37.650 --> 01:16:39.480
And the reason why you need
to address it is because

01:16:39.480 --> 01:16:42.270
you need to normalize those
biomechanics to the shoulder

01:16:42.270 --> 01:16:43.770
if you want their long term health.

01:16:43.770 --> 01:16:45.480
And one of the functions of the shoulder

01:16:45.480 --> 01:16:47.400
is to raise our arm up over our head.

01:16:47.400 --> 01:16:50.324
And if we do that from an
internally rotated position,

01:16:50.324 --> 01:16:53.330
we're going to have a higher likelihood

01:16:53.330 --> 01:16:55.800
of creating stress inside that joint.

01:16:55.800 --> 01:16:58.590
Funny thing is I talked
about before my PT brethren

01:16:58.590 --> 01:17:01.710
who can be somewhat angry these days,

01:17:01.710 --> 01:17:03.960
I don't know what
happened, but fairly angry.

01:17:03.960 --> 01:17:07.890
They want to discredit the
existence of something like

01:17:07.890 --> 01:17:11.850
shoulder impingement,
which I don't know how,

01:17:11.850 --> 01:17:14.610
I mean, certain studies,
look, we both all read studies

01:17:14.610 --> 01:17:17.010
and studies will say one thing one day

01:17:17.010 --> 01:17:19.770
and potentially conflict entirely
in a different direction.

01:17:19.770 --> 01:17:21.586
Some studies will point
to the non-existence

01:17:21.586 --> 01:17:24.180
of a shoulder impingement.

01:17:24.180 --> 01:17:28.306
Meanwhile, we have thankfully
digital motion x-rays

01:17:28.306 --> 01:17:32.580
that will literally show the
impingement occur in real time,

01:17:32.580 --> 01:17:33.660
in real function.

01:17:33.660 --> 01:17:35.610
And that's one of the limitations
I'm off on a tangent here,

01:17:35.610 --> 01:17:37.170
but like those types of x-rays

01:17:37.170 --> 01:17:39.360
or that type of fluoroscopy
that we have nowadays,

01:17:39.360 --> 01:17:41.850
like gives us such insight
that we never had before

01:17:41.850 --> 01:17:43.320
'cause we're taking static x-rays

01:17:43.320 --> 01:17:45.120
of someone laying down on a table.

01:17:45.120 --> 01:17:46.170
Well, I want to see what happens

01:17:46.170 --> 01:17:48.870
when you actually raise my arm
up over my head in function

01:17:48.870 --> 01:17:51.330
and the tools now exist to do that.

01:17:51.330 --> 01:17:54.360
We see the problems occurring because

01:17:54.360 --> 01:17:56.850
in order to get normal mechanics

01:17:56.850 --> 01:17:59.250
and free up the joint maximally inside

01:17:59.250 --> 01:18:02.490
you need to externally rotate
as you raise the arm up.

01:18:02.490 --> 01:18:04.560
So if your muscles aren't firing

01:18:04.560 --> 01:18:07.830
and they're not necessarily as strong

01:18:07.830 --> 01:18:10.830
as the internal rotation
bias that pulls them in,

01:18:10.830 --> 01:18:12.750
you're asking for trouble
every time you do that.

01:18:12.750 --> 01:18:17.490
Well, this exercise is literally
putting you in elevation

01:18:17.490 --> 01:18:18.480
and internal rotation.

01:18:18.480 --> 01:18:21.277
And if you were to walk into
a PT office and someone said,

01:18:21.277 --> 01:18:23.550
"I think he's got impingement,
will you diagnose him?"

01:18:23.550 --> 01:18:25.470
There's a test called
a Hawkins Kennedy Test.

01:18:25.470 --> 01:18:27.300
And I would put you in the position

01:18:27.300 --> 01:18:29.730
I know we're not visible at
this point through the podcast,

01:18:29.730 --> 01:18:31.680
but I'll put you in this position here

01:18:31.680 --> 01:18:33.420
where I have your arm elevated

01:18:33.420 --> 01:18:35.940
and your hand pretty much under your chin

01:18:35.940 --> 01:18:37.200
pushing downward on that

01:18:37.200 --> 01:18:39.000
to create that internal shoulder rotation.

01:18:39.000 --> 01:18:40.800
Pretty much the exact
position that we're in

01:18:40.800 --> 01:18:43.650
when we're holding a
bar in an upright row.

01:18:43.650 --> 01:18:45.660
Some will say, well, just don't go so high

01:18:45.660 --> 01:18:46.980
going up to the level of the chest,

01:18:46.980 --> 01:18:49.890
but you're still in this
internally rotated position.

01:18:49.890 --> 01:18:51.930
The thing that I think
frustrates me the most

01:18:51.930 --> 01:18:55.320
about the exercise is
that I have an alternative

01:18:55.320 --> 01:18:56.850
and the alternative does the same thing

01:18:56.850 --> 01:18:58.950
in terms of helping the muscles grow

01:18:58.950 --> 01:19:01.160
by simply fixing the
biomechanics of the exercise

01:19:01.160 --> 01:19:03.780
by just allowing the hands
to go higher than the elbows.

01:19:03.780 --> 01:19:06.120
So instead of the elbows
being higher than the hand

01:19:06.120 --> 01:19:08.130
which drives you into internal rotation,

01:19:08.130 --> 01:19:10.230
if the elbow is lower than the hand,

01:19:10.230 --> 01:19:13.470
the hand being higher here,
I'm in external rotation.

01:19:13.470 --> 01:19:15.630
And I could do something
called a high pull

01:19:15.630 --> 01:19:18.330
and still get the same
abduction of the arm

01:19:18.330 --> 01:19:20.460
and still get the same
benefits of the shoulders,

01:19:20.460 --> 01:19:21.720
the delts and the traps

01:19:21.720 --> 01:19:23.730
without having to undergo
any of the stresses

01:19:23.730 --> 01:19:24.810
that would come from

01:19:24.810 --> 01:19:27.720
the somewhat awkward
movement of an upright row.

01:19:27.720 --> 01:19:29.070
- And for those listening,

01:19:29.070 --> 01:19:31.530
we'll put a link to a short
clip of what this looks like.

01:19:31.530 --> 01:19:33.060
But basically what Jeff is doing

01:19:33.060 --> 01:19:35.550
and tell me if I'm describing
this incorrectly or correctly.

01:19:35.550 --> 01:19:38.733
Jeff is taking your two thumbs
and pointing behind you,

01:19:39.780 --> 01:19:41.400
so elbows up kind of near the chin

01:19:41.400 --> 01:19:43.320
and pointing behind you
like, oh, headed that way.

01:19:43.320 --> 01:19:45.300
Like somebody directing the
airplane, like come back,

01:19:45.300 --> 01:19:46.519
come back and back.

01:19:46.519 --> 01:19:47.790
I forget what they call that.

01:19:47.790 --> 01:19:49.590
I think it's called semaphorin,

01:19:49.590 --> 01:19:52.020
is the action of like where
they direct the planes

01:19:52.020 --> 01:19:53.610
or something the flags or whatever.

01:19:53.610 --> 01:19:55.890
Someone will, of course tell
me I'm wrong about that too,

01:19:55.890 --> 01:19:57.390
which is why I say these things

01:19:57.390 --> 01:20:00.409
because I like being told
what the correct answer is.

01:20:00.409 --> 01:20:04.680
In any case, so this
replaces the upright row

01:20:04.680 --> 01:20:05.550
and probably does a number

01:20:05.550 --> 01:20:07.350
of other important things as well.

01:20:07.350 --> 01:20:11.100
- Well, again, listen, without
naming names or programs

01:20:11.100 --> 01:20:11.933
or anything like that.

01:20:11.933 --> 01:20:15.873
When I got involved in, when
I got involved in Athlean-X,

01:20:15.873 --> 01:20:19.602
when I first my online presence,

01:20:19.602 --> 01:20:23.340
there was a very, very,
very popular program

01:20:23.340 --> 01:20:26.820
that was out there that I just
for fun I wanted a as a PT,

01:20:26.820 --> 01:20:28.290
is the nerdy things we do,

01:20:28.290 --> 01:20:32.760
but I wanted to evaluate
the workout structure.

01:20:32.760 --> 01:20:34.920
And I went and I looked at every rep

01:20:34.920 --> 01:20:36.390
over the course of a week.

01:20:36.390 --> 01:20:39.720
And there was something
like 890 repetitions

01:20:39.720 --> 01:20:41.231
or something done

01:20:41.231 --> 01:20:44.120
and zero of them were
dedicated external rotation

01:20:44.120 --> 01:20:45.240
in the shoulder.

01:20:45.240 --> 01:20:46.619
So if you think about it, I mean,

01:20:46.619 --> 01:20:47.760
again, it was a very popular program

01:20:47.760 --> 01:20:49.139
that was done by a lot of people.

01:20:49.139 --> 01:20:51.240
There was no focus at all,

01:20:51.240 --> 01:20:54.540
no dedicated focus towards creating

01:20:54.540 --> 01:20:57.720
a balance to an action
that is so predominant.

01:20:57.720 --> 01:21:00.870
And remember, it's not just
because we sit with bad posture,

01:21:00.870 --> 01:21:05.010
but the fact that our chest
can internally rotate,

01:21:05.010 --> 01:21:06.810
our latch can internally rotate,

01:21:06.810 --> 01:21:09.390
there's like muscle, other
big muscles that participate

01:21:09.390 --> 01:21:10.980
in things that we do every day

01:21:10.980 --> 01:21:13.710
that will further internally
rotate the shoulder.

01:21:13.710 --> 01:21:16.770
The only weapons we have
for external rotation

01:21:16.770 --> 01:21:18.750
are those little rotator cuff muscles

01:21:18.750 --> 01:21:21.150
and three of them actually
three of the four.

01:21:21.150 --> 01:21:23.040
And the job is to sort of

01:21:23.040 --> 01:21:25.320
actively and consciously train them

01:21:25.320 --> 01:21:27.210
through really the
boring exercises, right?

01:21:27.210 --> 01:21:28.620
Like you've seen them with the band,

01:21:28.620 --> 01:21:30.990
you anchor a band to a pole,

01:21:30.990 --> 01:21:33.180
you stand with the band
in the opposite hand.

01:21:33.180 --> 01:21:35.160
So if it's anchored to
the pole on my left side

01:21:35.160 --> 01:21:36.660
I've got the band on my right side

01:21:36.660 --> 01:21:38.730
and you see people where they
kind of rotate their hand

01:21:38.730 --> 01:21:39.660
towards the back.

01:21:39.660 --> 01:21:40.920
Again, kind of what you were saying

01:21:40.920 --> 01:21:43.860
but at a lower elevation
taking the back of my hand

01:21:43.860 --> 01:21:46.830
and trying to point it
to somebody behind me.

01:21:46.830 --> 01:21:51.270
Well, that is one of the
ways to train the muscle.

01:21:51.270 --> 01:21:53.010
It's just a one function of the shoulder,

01:21:53.010 --> 01:21:56.460
external rotation of the
shoulder and you need to do it.

01:21:56.460 --> 01:21:58.980
And again, it's not that

01:21:58.980 --> 01:22:01.140
if somebody was doing more
external rotation work

01:22:01.140 --> 01:22:04.230
could they absorb the upright row better?

01:22:04.230 --> 01:22:06.990
Probably, because as
they elevated the arm,

01:22:06.990 --> 01:22:10.800
they probably have a little
bit more of a contribution

01:22:10.800 --> 01:22:13.890
from the rotator cuff to
what one of the functions is

01:22:13.890 --> 01:22:16.890
to centralize the head
of the humorous inside

01:22:16.890 --> 01:22:18.630
of the glenoid, the capsule.

01:22:18.630 --> 01:22:22.050
So as it rises up, it stays central

01:22:22.050 --> 01:22:23.943
as opposed to migrating up

01:22:23.943 --> 01:22:26.520
because the deltoid likes to pull up.

01:22:26.520 --> 01:22:30.240
So if the rotator cuff has
some ability to counteract

01:22:30.240 --> 01:22:32.190
the upward pull of a del

01:22:32.190 --> 01:22:34.650
then it can maintain a
more healthy relationship

01:22:34.650 --> 01:22:35.700
with overhead movement.

01:22:35.700 --> 01:22:37.920
So just realizing that that function

01:22:37.920 --> 01:22:39.808
is only gained through
doing these exercises,

01:22:39.808 --> 01:22:43.746
we would probably
dedicate more time there,

01:22:43.746 --> 01:22:46.800
the upright row might be
better absorbed by that person

01:22:46.800 --> 01:22:48.420
'cause they have a
little bit more strength.

01:22:48.420 --> 01:22:50.070
But again, why?

01:22:50.070 --> 01:22:52.800
Because if you have an exercise
that does the same thing

01:22:52.800 --> 01:22:54.660
for what you're trying to do muscularly,

01:22:54.660 --> 01:22:56.760
to build the muscles that it affects,

01:22:56.760 --> 01:22:58.925
why wouldn't you just do
it where you can still see

01:22:58.925 --> 01:23:02.520
actually pick up more
repetitions of external rotation?

01:23:02.520 --> 01:23:05.430
So you're getting none of
the harm all of the benefits.

01:23:05.430 --> 01:23:08.550
I see zero reason to
ever do the upright row

01:23:08.550 --> 01:23:11.100
and people will argue, this
is the way they argue that,

01:23:11.100 --> 01:23:13.980
I've done this for 30 years
and I've never hurt myself.

01:23:13.980 --> 01:23:16.950
And I always say yet, yet.

01:23:16.950 --> 01:23:21.000
Like, listen, the goal is
to not hurt yourself ever.

01:23:21.000 --> 01:23:25.560
So even if you, it's sort of
like the championship game.

01:23:25.560 --> 01:23:27.330
You might play the game of your life,

01:23:27.330 --> 01:23:28.680
but if you lose you lost.

01:23:28.680 --> 01:23:30.780
And when you get into the
end of the record books,

01:23:30.780 --> 01:23:31.740
you still lost.

01:23:31.740 --> 01:23:33.913
So even if you had the
game in your life you lost,

01:23:33.913 --> 01:23:35.687
I don't care if you do
it for 30 years, no pain,

01:23:35.687 --> 01:23:37.980
you're still doing it and there's no pain

01:23:37.980 --> 01:23:39.390
I'm giving you an option

01:23:39.390 --> 01:23:41.280
that's going to give you the same results

01:23:41.280 --> 01:23:42.750
in the exercise that you're seeking.

01:23:42.750 --> 01:23:44.460
That's why you're doing the exercise

01:23:44.460 --> 01:23:47.910
without the possibility of
having the the bad outcome

01:23:47.910 --> 01:23:48.780
come from it.

01:23:48.780 --> 01:23:52.500
So I get a little bit
defensive of the move,

01:23:52.500 --> 01:23:54.630
but I feel like it's like,
why would you do that?

01:23:54.630 --> 01:23:55.463
- No, it makes me say,

01:23:55.463 --> 01:23:59.250
being able to train for a long
period of time and feel good.

01:23:59.250 --> 01:24:02.010
I'm proud to say and I don't have

01:24:02.010 --> 01:24:04.020
the kind of genetics where
like we don't have a lot of

01:24:04.020 --> 01:24:06.600
impressive athletes in our
family tree or anything.

01:24:06.600 --> 01:24:09.000
It's some fit individuals
some less fit individuals,

01:24:09.000 --> 01:24:09.960
but I really believe

01:24:09.960 --> 01:24:12.030
it's about putting in the
work consistently over time.

01:24:12.030 --> 01:24:16.742
And the more often you can
wake up not in pain the better.

01:24:16.742 --> 01:24:21.060
And so I think that being
in external rotation

01:24:21.060 --> 01:24:22.170
as often as possible is good.

01:24:22.170 --> 01:24:25.103
This is actually a good friend
who's a yoga teacher told me

01:24:25.103 --> 01:24:27.248
this is also a problem with the yogis,

01:24:27.248 --> 01:24:28.890
all the downward dog stuff.

01:24:28.890 --> 01:24:29.768
For those listening,

01:24:29.768 --> 01:24:32.813
you can think of inward
rotation as like thumbs down.

01:24:32.813 --> 01:24:35.700
Just think thumbs down
and rotation isn't bad

01:24:35.700 --> 01:24:38.520
but less thumbs down more
thumbs up is external rotation.

01:24:38.520 --> 01:24:41.490
So for those just listening,
maybe that gives a visual.

01:24:41.490 --> 01:24:43.722
The more exercise you can
do in external rotation

01:24:43.722 --> 01:24:47.081
the better it seems on average.

01:24:47.081 --> 01:24:50.820
I'd love to chat with you
just a little bit more about

01:24:50.820 --> 01:24:55.820
biomechanics, and this is a
personal thing that again,

01:24:56.760 --> 01:24:59.550
your content really helped solve for me.

01:24:59.550 --> 01:25:01.593
One is I thought I had lower back pain,

01:25:01.593 --> 01:25:05.691
that I had sciatica so
badly that on a few trips

01:25:05.691 --> 01:25:07.650
Work trips years ago

01:25:07.650 --> 01:25:09.870
when I was doing a lot
more international travel.

01:25:09.870 --> 01:25:11.327
I mean, it was hard to stand up sometimes.

01:25:11.327 --> 01:25:13.080
I mean like excruciating pain.

01:25:13.080 --> 01:25:14.310
I didn't want to take medication,

01:25:14.310 --> 01:25:15.985
I didn't want to do back surgery.

01:25:15.985 --> 01:25:19.910
In the end, turns out it
wasn't a back injury at all.

01:25:19.910 --> 01:25:23.700
And one of the things that
helped fix it was this

01:25:23.700 --> 01:25:26.310
just learning about this
thing called the medial glute.

01:25:26.310 --> 01:25:29.520
And you had a video that
said fixed back pain

01:25:29.520 --> 01:25:31.800
and then you quite accurately say that

01:25:31.800 --> 01:25:34.154
some back pain isn't really
about the back at all.

01:25:34.154 --> 01:25:39.030
And had me do an exercise or
allowed me to try an exercise

01:25:39.030 --> 01:25:43.860
where I lay on my side and
essentially pointing my toe down

01:25:43.860 --> 01:25:46.410
the top toe down, almost
like pointing a toe down

01:25:46.410 --> 01:25:49.050
and then would slowly lift the leg up

01:25:49.050 --> 01:25:50.940
while pointing the toe down.

01:25:50.940 --> 01:25:52.257
Maybe I got it...
- No, you got it good.

01:25:52.257 --> 01:25:53.697
- And then holding that,

01:25:53.697 --> 01:25:55.200
and there's a muscle that sort of sits

01:25:55.200 --> 01:25:56.190
at the top of the glute

01:25:56.190 --> 01:25:57.960
it kind of peaks out
every once in a while.

01:25:57.960 --> 01:25:59.550
You can feel it there with your thumb,

01:25:59.550 --> 01:26:01.680
which is I think you had push back on it.

01:26:01.680 --> 01:26:04.980
A bit creating that mind muscle link again

01:26:04.980 --> 01:26:06.600
and there with proprioception,

01:26:06.600 --> 01:26:10.680
the actual feeling of a
muscle literally with a limb,

01:26:10.680 --> 01:26:14.700
we know based on the neural
circuits for movement,

01:26:14.700 --> 01:26:17.580
that that enhances the
contractibility of a muscle.

01:26:17.580 --> 01:26:18.883
So like if you touch your bicep,

01:26:18.883 --> 01:26:21.450
you literally can contract
it more more strongly.

01:26:21.450 --> 01:26:23.308
And this makes total sense based on

01:26:23.308 --> 01:26:26.730
neuromuscular physiology.

01:26:26.730 --> 01:26:28.230
So had me do that repeatedly.

01:26:28.230 --> 01:26:30.163
And I started doing that in my hotel room

01:26:30.163 --> 01:26:32.310
and the pain started to disappear.

01:26:32.310 --> 01:26:33.840
And then it came back again the afternoon

01:26:33.840 --> 01:26:35.370
so I did it again in the afternoon.

01:26:35.370 --> 01:26:37.590
So this is something I
did for three or four days

01:26:37.590 --> 01:26:39.838
and lo and behold a back pain's gone.

01:26:39.838 --> 01:26:42.330
I handed this off to my father

01:26:42.330 --> 01:26:44.970
because he like me has a
slightly lower right shoulder.

01:26:44.970 --> 01:26:47.340
I think that our gate is
probably thrown off by this.

01:26:47.340 --> 01:26:49.425
It's probably a genetic thing, who knows.

01:26:49.425 --> 01:26:51.688
He handed off to somebody.

01:26:51.688 --> 01:26:54.477
It turns out that we don't
suffer from back pain

01:26:54.477 --> 01:26:56.310
and in fact, now I don't
suffer from any pain

01:26:56.310 --> 01:26:58.080
because I was doing this exercise

01:26:58.080 --> 01:27:00.960
which I think is helping my medial glute.

01:27:00.960 --> 01:27:02.160
Two reasons why I raised this.

01:27:02.160 --> 01:27:05.640
One, I know a lot of guys who
have this right side sciatica

01:27:05.640 --> 01:27:08.340
'cause people keep the
wallet there is one idea

01:27:08.340 --> 01:27:09.566
or left side sciatica.

01:27:09.566 --> 01:27:12.210
There are a lot of people male and female

01:27:12.210 --> 01:27:13.170
who think they have back pain

01:27:13.170 --> 01:27:14.610
when they don't actually have back pain.

01:27:14.610 --> 01:27:15.807
And the other thing is that

01:27:15.807 --> 01:27:18.390
is a general question about biomechanics

01:27:18.390 --> 01:27:19.800
or statement about biomechanics.

01:27:19.800 --> 01:27:21.420
I had of a feeling

01:27:21.420 --> 01:27:24.150
that a lot of what people
think is back pain or knee pain

01:27:24.150 --> 01:27:26.700
or neck pain or headache or shoulder pain

01:27:26.700 --> 01:27:31.110
is actually the consequence
of something that's happening

01:27:31.110 --> 01:27:33.750
above or below that site of pain.

01:27:33.750 --> 01:27:38.010
And this is a whole landscape
of stuff related to PT

01:27:38.010 --> 01:27:40.440
and recovery and pain management.

01:27:40.440 --> 01:27:43.290
But maybe if you could just
educate us a bit on this

01:27:43.290 --> 01:27:46.020
and why this works, what
is the medial glute?

01:27:46.020 --> 01:27:48.720
Why did it make my so-called
back pain disappear?

01:27:48.720 --> 01:27:51.060
And how should people think about pain?

01:27:51.060 --> 01:27:52.230
And I'd like to use this as a segue

01:27:52.230 --> 01:27:54.060
to get into a little bit deeper discussion

01:27:54.060 --> 01:27:55.110
about pain and recovery.

01:27:55.110 --> 01:27:55.943
- Sure.

01:27:55.943 --> 01:28:00.600
So this is definitely like a
big cornucopia PT stuff here,

01:28:00.600 --> 01:28:02.100
but like and this is what I love.

01:28:02.100 --> 01:28:04.703
So first of all, that video,

01:28:04.703 --> 01:28:07.287
it's my proudest video that I have.

01:28:07.287 --> 01:28:10.650
And the reason being is that
it's helped so many people.

01:28:10.650 --> 01:28:14.400
Like we get comments on
that video every day.

01:28:14.400 --> 01:28:15.630
I don't even know how
many views it's got now,

01:28:15.630 --> 01:28:17.342
30 some what million.

01:28:17.342 --> 01:28:19.890
- We will link to it.
- There's a lot of views.

01:28:19.890 --> 01:28:22.830
And quite honestly,

01:28:22.830 --> 01:28:24.930
it was a little bit of
an afterthought video

01:28:24.930 --> 01:28:27.600
in terms of it's origin.

01:28:27.600 --> 01:28:31.020
I think that that day maybe
Jesse was having some problems

01:28:31.020 --> 01:28:32.640
or something like that, a
little bit of low back pain

01:28:32.640 --> 01:28:35.310
and I showed him and it helped
right away and he was like,

01:28:35.310 --> 01:28:36.900
well, we can make a video on it

01:28:36.900 --> 01:28:38.370
'cause this will help people.

01:28:38.370 --> 01:28:40.743
Not everybody, if you
have a real disc problem,

01:28:40.743 --> 01:28:42.000
it's not going to help

01:28:42.000 --> 01:28:44.820
because you're not changing
the structural problem

01:28:44.820 --> 01:28:45.960
that's there.

01:28:45.960 --> 01:28:48.300
But as you said, a lot of people don't

01:28:48.300 --> 01:28:51.690
and even disc issues a lot
of them are non-operative.

01:28:51.690 --> 01:28:54.273
So you'd want to try these things first.

01:28:55.620 --> 01:28:57.540
As far as what you're sort of experienced

01:28:57.540 --> 01:28:59.790
sometimes as that glute
medias really tightens down

01:28:59.790 --> 01:29:02.460
and that's again, from poor biomechanics

01:29:02.460 --> 01:29:03.960
up and down the kinetic chain,

01:29:03.960 --> 01:29:06.510
it can actually press on the sciatic nerve

01:29:06.510 --> 01:29:09.060
and give you what they
call a pseudo sciatica.

01:29:09.060 --> 01:29:12.690
Where it's not like you're making it up,

01:29:12.690 --> 01:29:14.250
it's not like you're not feeling that pain

01:29:14.250 --> 01:29:16.380
over that same sciatic distribution,

01:29:16.380 --> 01:29:18.210
but it's not caused from a disc,

01:29:18.210 --> 01:29:20.670
it's not caused from
something mechanical there,

01:29:20.670 --> 01:29:23.310
it's caused by the fact that this gluteus

01:29:23.310 --> 01:29:27.690
has posturely become a
problem for you or weak

01:29:27.690 --> 01:29:30.810
because you don't train it
and you need to address it.

01:29:30.810 --> 01:29:34.110
So, unlike any other muscle in the body,

01:29:34.110 --> 01:29:37.680
there are common trigger
points in common areas where

01:29:37.680 --> 01:29:41.790
the muscle will become
tightened or painful or spasmed

01:29:41.790 --> 01:29:46.477
and you can basically apply
pressure to these areas

01:29:46.477 --> 01:29:50.520
and then sort of thread that
muscle through the pressure

01:29:50.520 --> 01:29:52.320
by pushing down through there and then

01:29:52.320 --> 01:29:54.300
contracting the muscle which is why

01:29:54.300 --> 01:29:56.168
you go through that action of,

01:29:56.168 --> 01:29:58.050
I think we call it toast stabber,

01:29:58.050 --> 01:30:00.180
but like stabbing down and lifting up

01:30:00.180 --> 01:30:01.440
and stabbing down and lifting up.

01:30:01.440 --> 01:30:04.050
Taking that glute medias
through its function.

01:30:04.050 --> 01:30:06.360
So it's basically kind of working

01:30:06.360 --> 01:30:08.910
underneath the downward
pressure of the finger

01:30:08.910 --> 01:30:13.500
and that tends to help
you to almost need out

01:30:13.500 --> 01:30:15.720
what might be that trigger point.

01:30:15.720 --> 01:30:18.060
And that's why people can
see immediate relief there

01:30:18.060 --> 01:30:22.080
because once the trigger
point lets go, it feels like,

01:30:22.080 --> 01:30:23.850
and that's what the
comments are in that video.

01:30:23.850 --> 01:30:25.980
Like my God, I literally, I couldn't walk,

01:30:25.980 --> 01:30:29.280
I've been on my hotel floor,
I did this and I'm fixed.

01:30:29.280 --> 01:30:31.530
And meanwhile then it could come back

01:30:31.530 --> 01:30:32.380
because your body is like,

01:30:32.380 --> 01:30:34.140
well, I like being more like this.

01:30:34.140 --> 01:30:37.740
This is how I've been ingrained to be.

01:30:37.740 --> 01:30:38.573
So it might come back

01:30:38.573 --> 01:30:40.620
but then when you do another round of it

01:30:40.620 --> 01:30:42.117
and another round of it and
then finally it starts to say,

01:30:42.117 --> 01:30:43.470
all right, I'm not going
to do that anymore.

01:30:43.470 --> 01:30:45.660
It kind of eases up and
you can relieve yourself

01:30:45.660 --> 01:30:46.745
of those trigger points.

01:30:46.745 --> 01:30:49.560
You could do that up and down the back.

01:30:49.560 --> 01:30:50.393
There's other people that get that

01:30:50.393 --> 01:30:53.100
and that sort of inside
their shoulder blade,

01:30:53.100 --> 01:30:56.910
that same type of
cramping in another area.

01:30:56.910 --> 01:31:00.120
But once that takes place,

01:31:00.120 --> 01:31:02.130
well, then the job that
I think people have

01:31:02.130 --> 01:31:04.080
is like become educated that

01:31:04.080 --> 01:31:06.630
the glute medias is different
than the glute maximus.

01:31:06.630 --> 01:31:08.520
Like their functions are different.

01:31:08.520 --> 01:31:12.270
You have to work on not
just extending the hip

01:31:12.270 --> 01:31:14.010
but also abduction of the hip.

01:31:14.010 --> 01:31:17.045
External rotation of the hip,
same thing as in the shoulder.

01:31:17.045 --> 01:31:19.320
And this actually segues nicely into what,

01:31:19.320 --> 01:31:20.760
into the whole concept
you were talking about.

01:31:20.760 --> 01:31:22.890
Like the body is like a mirror image.

01:31:22.890 --> 01:31:24.450
The hip is like the shoulder, right?

01:31:24.450 --> 01:31:26.100
The ankle is the wrist.

01:31:26.100 --> 01:31:27.497
The foot is the hand.

01:31:27.497 --> 01:31:30.570
The knee is the elbow,
they're two hinge joints,

01:31:30.570 --> 01:31:31.530
they function that way.

01:31:31.530 --> 01:31:33.810
Well, with the shoulder, you've got that

01:31:33.810 --> 01:31:37.170
mobility that comes from having
all that freedom of motion

01:31:37.170 --> 01:31:38.730
but the stability is lacking.

01:31:38.730 --> 01:31:41.520
Well, the same thing with the
hip, like you've got mobility,

01:31:41.520 --> 01:31:43.500
but if you don't fully stabilize it

01:31:43.500 --> 01:31:45.660
by training all the muscles of the hip

01:31:45.660 --> 01:31:48.060
and if you don't strengthen
the external rotation

01:31:48.060 --> 01:31:52.143
of the hip, then you're
going to have issues.

01:31:52.143 --> 01:31:55.050
Like it's not biomechanically
going to work the same way.

01:31:55.050 --> 01:31:58.779
If you think of the body
as a series of bands

01:31:58.779 --> 01:32:00.300
pulling in different directions

01:32:00.300 --> 01:32:02.139
at different levels of tension,

01:32:02.139 --> 01:32:05.310
you're being pulled into
one direction or the other

01:32:05.310 --> 01:32:07.140
just by the balance of tension

01:32:07.140 --> 01:32:11.220
from one weak area to one
dominantly tight area.

01:32:11.220 --> 01:32:12.660
And you need to make sure that

01:32:12.660 --> 01:32:14.460
you can sort of balance this out

01:32:14.460 --> 01:32:16.980
in order to eliminate
some of the adaptations

01:32:16.980 --> 01:32:18.594
and compensations that happen.

01:32:18.594 --> 01:32:23.594
So what I say, when we look at
sort of the body as a whole,

01:32:25.060 --> 01:32:28.020
most often wherever
you're feeling the pain

01:32:28.020 --> 01:32:30.630
is absolutely not to blame.

01:32:30.630 --> 01:32:31.530
There's not to blame.

01:32:31.530 --> 01:32:34.350
It is somewhere above or
below as you hint to that.

01:32:34.350 --> 01:32:38.070
You're talking about, the knee
is my favorite example of it.

01:32:38.070 --> 01:32:41.160
Whenever you have knee
pain, patella tendonitis

01:32:41.160 --> 01:32:43.110
which I have forever.

01:32:43.110 --> 01:32:45.780
I've had bad, bad cases
of tendonitis where

01:32:45.780 --> 01:32:47.949
squatting is very difficult for me.

01:32:47.949 --> 01:32:52.077
It's not the knee, the knee is
literally a hinge joint that

01:32:52.077 --> 01:32:54.630
there's minor rotation
capabilities in the knee

01:32:54.630 --> 01:32:56.100
but it's a hinge joint.

01:32:56.100 --> 01:33:00.270
And it's being impacted
by the hip and the ankle

01:33:00.270 --> 01:33:03.690
and the foot, as I said before,
how critical the foot is.

01:33:03.690 --> 01:33:06.433
If you thought of the knee being

01:33:06.433 --> 01:33:08.520
like the middle of a train track,

01:33:08.520 --> 01:33:10.410
where the femur down your thigh

01:33:10.410 --> 01:33:13.830
and your shin down below your
knee, where the train track,

01:33:13.830 --> 01:33:16.770
well, what would happen if the
foot collapses at the bottom?

01:33:16.770 --> 01:33:19.260
All of a sudden that
train track on the bottom

01:33:19.260 --> 01:33:21.072
gets torqued just a little bit.

01:33:21.072 --> 01:33:23.670
Well, who's going to feel that the most,

01:33:23.670 --> 01:33:25.740
the area where it's torquing,
which is at the knee.

01:33:25.740 --> 01:33:27.210
So the stresses are going to be felt there

01:33:27.210 --> 01:33:28.800
meanwhile the problem is the foot

01:33:28.800 --> 01:33:30.270
or the problem is the ankle.

01:33:30.270 --> 01:33:32.384
People that are chronic ankle sprainers

01:33:32.384 --> 01:33:35.490
are almost always going to
wind up having back pain

01:33:35.490 --> 01:33:38.010
because the ankle sprain causes weakness

01:33:38.010 --> 01:33:39.780
in mal adaptations in the ankle

01:33:39.780 --> 01:33:41.880
that then gets connected
through the chain.

01:33:41.880 --> 01:33:44.970
Because now once I distort
the ankle and the shin,

01:33:44.970 --> 01:33:47.160
now the knee is trying to maintain

01:33:47.160 --> 01:33:49.470
its ability to hinge smoothly.

01:33:49.470 --> 01:33:52.170
So it toques on the femur to do that.

01:33:52.170 --> 01:33:53.970
Well, the femur is now
inside the hip joint

01:33:53.970 --> 01:33:56.880
pulling on the pelvis and
the pelvis is out of whack.

01:33:56.880 --> 01:33:58.560
It's really is fascinating.

01:33:58.560 --> 01:34:00.600
Like it's one of my favorite things about

01:34:00.600 --> 01:34:04.590
how the body works is like
how interconnected it is

01:34:04.590 --> 01:34:06.630
and how one little thing somewhere

01:34:06.630 --> 01:34:08.610
causes repercussion somewhere else.

01:34:08.610 --> 01:34:12.180
And the easiest way to find
out what your problem is,

01:34:12.180 --> 01:34:14.130
is to say, okay, I know
where my symptom is,

01:34:14.130 --> 01:34:16.110
but I got to find someone who can help me

01:34:16.110 --> 01:34:17.910
find the source somewhere else,

01:34:17.910 --> 01:34:20.490
because it is going to be
usually either above or below.

01:34:20.490 --> 01:34:21.660
Mostly, usually below

01:34:21.660 --> 01:34:23.970
'cause it usually translates
up the kinetic chain.

01:34:23.970 --> 01:34:26.880
But usually it's going to be
below where the real source is.

01:34:26.880 --> 01:34:30.000
So people with low back
pain usually have hip issues

01:34:30.000 --> 01:34:32.480
weaknesses, tightnesses,
flexibility issues,

01:34:32.480 --> 01:34:34.263
it's almost always below.

01:34:35.100 --> 01:34:38.429
When you get into really high
performance athletics though,

01:34:38.429 --> 01:34:40.200
it almost works the other way.

01:34:40.200 --> 01:34:43.470
Like where we have pitchers, who can't,

01:34:43.470 --> 01:34:47.820
I'm always fascinated by guys
that have Tommy John issues

01:34:47.820 --> 01:34:48.653
in their elbow, right?

01:34:48.653 --> 01:34:49.486
Pitchers.

01:34:49.486 --> 01:34:53.040
Like if you can't externally
rotate the shoulder

01:34:53.040 --> 01:34:54.090
that we talked about, again,

01:34:54.090 --> 01:34:55.950
the ability to get your shoulder back

01:34:55.950 --> 01:34:57.060
into external rotation.

01:34:57.060 --> 01:35:01.140
Well, your arm has to
get to a certain position

01:35:01.140 --> 01:35:02.970
for release of the baseball.

01:35:02.970 --> 01:35:04.310
And if it can't get there,

01:35:04.310 --> 01:35:06.930
if you can't externally rotate
the shoulder to get there

01:35:06.930 --> 01:35:09.870
then the elbow has to sort of torque more

01:35:09.870 --> 01:35:12.870
in order to allow the
arm to get back further.

01:35:12.870 --> 01:35:15.150
And it will try to take
some of that motion

01:35:15.150 --> 01:35:16.530
from a joint that's not really,

01:35:16.530 --> 01:35:19.920
again, it is the hinge joint,
really capable of doing that.

01:35:19.920 --> 01:35:22.260
So it starts to stress
that media level ligament

01:35:22.260 --> 01:35:23.730
to get a little bit further back

01:35:23.730 --> 01:35:25.350
because the shoulder's not working

01:35:25.350 --> 01:35:27.870
and that just ultimately
places strain on the elbow.

01:35:27.870 --> 01:35:31.170
So when you see a guy that has pain

01:35:31.170 --> 01:35:34.320
that floats around, a pitcher
that floats around their arm,

01:35:34.320 --> 01:35:36.870
all that is is sort of this
balance of compensation.

01:35:36.870 --> 01:35:38.820
Once his elbow starts hurting,

01:35:38.820 --> 01:35:41.910
then he can't get the
range from the elbow,

01:35:41.910 --> 01:35:44.130
so he tries to dig a
little bit further back

01:35:44.130 --> 01:35:45.240
into external rotation

01:35:45.240 --> 01:35:46.800
and then the rotator cuff gets inflamed

01:35:46.800 --> 01:35:48.570
and then he feels that's inflamed,

01:35:48.570 --> 01:35:49.590
and by the way, during that time period,

01:35:49.590 --> 01:35:51.330
it takes some of the strain off the elbow

01:35:51.330 --> 01:35:52.530
so the elbow feels better.

01:35:52.530 --> 01:35:55.110
Then he decides, okay, now
I got the extra rotation,

01:35:55.110 --> 01:35:56.400
but I'm getting too much of that

01:35:56.400 --> 01:35:58.290
so now he starts straining the elbow again

01:35:58.290 --> 01:35:59.910
and then keeps going through this cycle.

01:35:59.910 --> 01:36:03.300
So your body is very smart

01:36:03.300 --> 01:36:05.760
and it's going to compensate
every single time.

01:36:05.760 --> 01:36:07.020
It's going to find the compensation

01:36:07.020 --> 01:36:08.970
but there's no guarantee
that that compensation

01:36:08.970 --> 01:36:11.430
doesn't leave you with a
whole host of other issues.

01:36:11.430 --> 01:36:12.883
- Yeah, it's fascinating.

01:36:12.883 --> 01:36:15.750
In another lifetime, I
would've gone and been a PT

01:36:15.750 --> 01:36:18.480
although it sounds like the
community among PTs online...

01:36:18.480 --> 01:36:19.313
- I don't know what they,

01:36:19.313 --> 01:36:21.150
listen, we're good people but it's like...

01:36:21.150 --> 01:36:22.740
- Yeah, scientists and neuroscientists

01:36:22.740 --> 01:36:24.691
can get into pretty intense battles.

01:36:24.691 --> 01:36:26.940
Coming from the academic community,

01:36:26.940 --> 01:36:29.273
the etiquette is so different
online because I would say,

01:36:29.273 --> 01:36:30.450
I think in person

01:36:30.450 --> 01:36:31.978
people would probably
behave a bit differently.

01:36:31.978 --> 01:36:33.120
- They shake your hand and say, hello.

01:36:33.120 --> 01:36:34.688
- Yeah, they shake your
hand and say, hello.

01:36:34.688 --> 01:36:39.030
And there's also, look, I'll
just be very direct about this.

01:36:39.030 --> 01:36:41.280
There are a lot of people online for whom

01:36:41.280 --> 01:36:45.120
their only content is pointing
out the misunderstandings

01:36:45.120 --> 01:36:47.243
or alleged flaws of other people.

01:36:47.243 --> 01:36:49.590
It's like the bulk of their identity.

01:36:49.590 --> 01:36:51.570
Which to me is sort of a sad existence

01:36:51.570 --> 01:36:54.420
but there's always more
to gain by thinking about

01:36:54.420 --> 01:36:56.400
what's possible, and
what's new and what's good.

01:36:56.400 --> 01:37:00.390
But teach their own demise or win.

01:37:00.390 --> 01:37:03.491
- I mean questioning what's
out there is healthy,

01:37:03.491 --> 01:37:05.890
it's normal, is great, it
actually sparks conversation.

01:37:05.890 --> 01:37:08.070
But as you said, some people's existence

01:37:08.070 --> 01:37:11.820
is solely to find things to nag about

01:37:11.820 --> 01:37:14.580
and not actually with the goal
being to advance anything,

01:37:14.580 --> 01:37:16.230
but rather just to.

01:37:16.230 --> 01:37:18.390
- Yeah, in the world of science

01:37:18.390 --> 01:37:22.320
being skeptical but not
cynical is encouraged.

01:37:22.320 --> 01:37:23.910
But I always say that

01:37:23.910 --> 01:37:26.220
the longer that somebody's
in a career path

01:37:26.220 --> 01:37:27.840
that's certainly in science or medicine

01:37:27.840 --> 01:37:31.920
and they realize how hard
it is to do various studies.

01:37:31.920 --> 01:37:33.150
Once they publish a few studies,

01:37:33.150 --> 01:37:36.180
generally they sort of get
a better understanding of

01:37:36.180 --> 01:37:38.040
how the various things are done.

01:37:38.040 --> 01:37:43.040
In any case, along the lines
of pain and pain relief

01:37:44.220 --> 01:37:47.143
and misunderstandings about the
origins of pain in the body,

01:37:47.143 --> 01:37:50.940
one of the great tools that
I picked up from your content

01:37:50.940 --> 01:37:54.360
which is benefit I know a
huge number of people is

01:37:54.360 --> 01:37:55.980
I think I used to hold weights

01:37:55.980 --> 01:37:57.900
sometimes in the tips of my fingers

01:37:57.900 --> 01:38:00.390
as opposed to in the meat
or the palm of my hands.

01:38:00.390 --> 01:38:01.740
And I had elbow pain.

01:38:01.740 --> 01:38:02.730
And I always thought that,

01:38:02.730 --> 01:38:05.427
I felt it most on tricep
exercises and pushing exercise

01:38:05.427 --> 01:38:07.920
and I thought I was doing
those exercises wrong.

01:38:07.920 --> 01:38:11.250
Turns out toward the end of
my pull ups or my bicep work,

01:38:11.250 --> 01:38:12.660
I was letting the weight or the bar

01:38:12.660 --> 01:38:14.250
drift into my fingertips.

01:38:14.250 --> 01:38:16.710
And the mere shift

01:38:16.710 --> 01:38:19.350
to making sure that my
knuckles were well over the bar

01:38:19.350 --> 01:38:22.560
or that the weight was really
in the meat of my palms

01:38:22.560 --> 01:38:24.540
has completely ameliorated that

01:38:24.540 --> 01:38:26.190
for reasons that you point out

01:38:26.190 --> 01:38:27.930
and maybe you could just
share with us why that is

01:38:27.930 --> 01:38:30.720
you have this kind of
finger pull exercise.

01:38:30.720 --> 01:38:32.100
Usually when someone says, pull my finger,

01:38:32.100 --> 01:38:34.746
it's like a bad middle school
or elementary school joke...

01:38:34.746 --> 01:38:36.426
- Yeah, we would say push your finger.

01:38:36.426 --> 01:38:37.259
- [Andrew] Right, right.

01:38:37.259 --> 01:38:38.790
- You know this is fascinating.

01:38:38.790 --> 01:38:40.980
This is because it just shows again

01:38:40.980 --> 01:38:42.300
how intricate the body is

01:38:42.300 --> 01:38:44.760
and how responsive or
over responsive it can be

01:38:44.760 --> 01:38:45.990
to something so little.

01:38:45.990 --> 01:38:50.370
And what you're talking about
is that when you grip a bar

01:38:50.370 --> 01:38:52.467
whether it be through a curl
or whether it be through-

01:38:52.467 --> 01:38:55.230
And this is mostly
pulling exercises because

01:38:55.230 --> 01:38:57.180
the tendency for the bar is going to be to

01:38:57.180 --> 01:38:59.490
fall out of your hand not
like with a pushing exercise

01:38:59.490 --> 01:39:01.710
where it's kind of you're
pushing your hand into the bar.

01:39:01.710 --> 01:39:03.063
So on a bench press say.

01:39:04.200 --> 01:39:08.850
That bar can drift just
by gravity doing its thing

01:39:08.850 --> 01:39:12.140
or fatigue of the hand grip strength.

01:39:12.140 --> 01:39:16.650
Can start to drift further
away towards the distal digits.

01:39:16.650 --> 01:39:18.960
Through those last couple knuckles

01:39:18.960 --> 01:39:20.340
that we have on our hands.

01:39:20.340 --> 01:39:24.094
And though our hand can
still hold it there,

01:39:24.094 --> 01:39:28.500
the muscles are not equipped
to handle those types of loads.

01:39:28.500 --> 01:39:31.680
And that can start at a very,
I'm not going to say light,

01:39:31.680 --> 01:39:35.700
but like it could start
at dumbbell weight.

01:39:35.700 --> 01:39:38.970
40 pounds, 30 pounds, even
25 pounds or something

01:39:38.970 --> 01:39:40.950
depending upon their
overall strength levels.

01:39:40.950 --> 01:39:43.590
But then when you start to
apply it to something like

01:39:43.590 --> 01:39:46.290
your body weight with a chin up, right?

01:39:46.290 --> 01:39:49.050
'Cause that's natural for
the bar to somewhat kind of

01:39:49.050 --> 01:39:50.880
float down towards your fingertips.

01:39:50.880 --> 01:39:53.310
And it actually is a little bit easier

01:39:53.310 --> 01:39:56.790
to perform the exercise with
that sort of like false script

01:39:56.790 --> 01:39:58.140
little hook grip at the end

01:39:58.140 --> 01:40:00.840
because you're not going
to engage the forearms

01:40:00.840 --> 01:40:03.589
into the exercise, you're not
going to start pulling down.

01:40:03.589 --> 01:40:07.980
But at the same time while it
could help you to perform them

01:40:07.980 --> 01:40:10.290
better by getting the back more activated,

01:40:10.290 --> 01:40:12.180
if you have weakness in these muscles.

01:40:12.180 --> 01:40:13.770
'Cause it's not a thing
that happens to every,

01:40:13.770 --> 01:40:15.810
it's not one of those
upright road type things

01:40:15.810 --> 01:40:18.210
where I think this is
happening to everybody.

01:40:18.210 --> 01:40:19.170
This is happening to people

01:40:19.170 --> 01:40:23.580
that have these inherent
weaknesses in these muscles.

01:40:23.580 --> 01:40:25.829
You or haven't done enough of the gripping

01:40:25.829 --> 01:40:29.850
in the meat of the hand for long enough

01:40:29.850 --> 01:40:33.033
but it starts to put that
stress on these muscles

01:40:33.033 --> 01:40:35.727
that are ill-equipped to
do this and to handle this

01:40:35.727 --> 01:40:38.954
and it starts to it's particularly
on that fourth finger,

01:40:38.954 --> 01:40:41.790
which is part of the
muscle we call the FDS,

01:40:41.790 --> 01:40:45.420
a flexor digitorum that is
just too much for it to handle.

01:40:45.420 --> 01:40:46.830
And that comes all the way down

01:40:46.830 --> 01:40:48.360
and meets right at the media level.

01:40:48.360 --> 01:40:50.010
Right on that spot that you can say

01:40:50.010 --> 01:40:51.990
feels like someone's knifing
you right in the middle

01:40:51.990 --> 01:40:53.160
in that medial level.

01:40:53.160 --> 01:40:56.730
And medial epicondylitis or
they call it golfers elbow

01:40:56.730 --> 01:40:59.850
is something that a lot of
us deal with in the gym.

01:40:59.850 --> 01:41:02.430
It's one of the most common
inflammatory conditions

01:41:02.430 --> 01:41:03.540
people get from the gym

01:41:03.540 --> 01:41:07.440
and it all comes from this
positioning of the dumbbell

01:41:07.440 --> 01:41:10.530
or barbell or hand on a
pull-up bar over time.

01:41:10.530 --> 01:41:13.620
So the easiest thing to
do is just grip deeper

01:41:13.620 --> 01:41:15.190
so that what you're doing is you're using

01:41:15.190 --> 01:41:19.410
more leverage from the
palm to encapsulate the bar

01:41:19.410 --> 01:41:20.760
or the dumbbell or whatever

01:41:20.760 --> 01:41:23.640
and you're not putting that
pressure really distally

01:41:23.640 --> 01:41:25.530
right on that last digit

01:41:25.530 --> 01:41:29.490
because that's where that
FDS muscle is most strained.

01:41:29.490 --> 01:41:33.240
So you just almost eliminating
that from the equation.

01:41:33.240 --> 01:41:36.316
And it's one of those exercises that

01:41:36.316 --> 01:41:39.090
the load can exceed its
capacity pretty quickly.

01:41:39.090 --> 01:41:42.796
So that like, maybe it's only
capable of handling 30 pounds

01:41:42.796 --> 01:41:44.430
and then when you're doing a chin up

01:41:44.430 --> 01:41:46.007
and it goes and it drifts so far

01:41:46.007 --> 01:41:49.296
that it's now you say
you're a 200 pound guy

01:41:49.296 --> 01:41:51.927
you've got let's say 100
pounds through one arm

01:41:51.927 --> 01:41:54.270
and 100 pounds, this is simplified math

01:41:54.270 --> 01:41:55.950
that obviously is offset by other muscles,

01:41:55.950 --> 01:41:58.020
but 100 pounds to one arm
100 pounds to the arm,

01:41:58.020 --> 01:42:00.960
100 pounds off of a
muscle that can handle 30

01:42:00.960 --> 01:42:03.265
is not going to take many
repetitions to strain it

01:42:03.265 --> 01:42:05.100
and you're going to feel that maybe

01:42:05.100 --> 01:42:06.420
by the time that a set's over

01:42:06.420 --> 01:42:08.610
or certainly by the time that
workout's over or the next day

01:42:08.610 --> 01:42:11.031
you wake up and you've got
that notable stabbing pain.

01:42:11.031 --> 01:42:12.833
Whenever someone feels that

01:42:12.833 --> 01:42:15.180
the best thing would be to determine,

01:42:15.180 --> 01:42:18.090
okay, what exercises would
I do that were pulling

01:42:18.090 --> 01:42:19.980
and where the bar could
have drifted deeper

01:42:19.980 --> 01:42:24.360
or further from the meat
of my palm into my fingers

01:42:24.360 --> 01:42:26.400
and figure out a way to deepen that grip.

01:42:26.400 --> 01:42:28.410
When that happens though,
the best thing to do

01:42:28.410 --> 01:42:30.630
with most of these inflammatory conditions

01:42:30.630 --> 01:42:33.240
is not do any of that
stuff for a little while.

01:42:33.240 --> 01:42:35.400
Not ever, just for a little while.

01:42:35.400 --> 01:42:37.350
There's always things
that you can do around it.

01:42:37.350 --> 01:42:40.050
I'm not saying ever do I say,
like, don't go to the gym

01:42:40.050 --> 01:42:41.880
or don't find something you can do,

01:42:41.880 --> 01:42:44.280
but I'm saying that particular exercise

01:42:44.280 --> 01:42:47.280
that you feel the pain
on while you're doing it,

01:42:47.280 --> 01:42:51.390
never a smart idea to do that
exercise when it's inflamed.

01:42:51.390 --> 01:42:53.070
If you are doing exercise and it hurts,

01:42:53.070 --> 01:42:54.630
you probably shouldn't do the exercise

01:42:54.630 --> 01:42:57.055
because another reason

01:42:57.055 --> 01:43:00.420
for the variability of exercise is

01:43:00.420 --> 01:43:02.730
there's so many other
options that you can do

01:43:02.730 --> 01:43:05.520
that will train similar
muscles or even the same motion

01:43:05.520 --> 01:43:07.110
and not cause that stress.

01:43:07.110 --> 01:43:11.160
So, I mean, a cable curl would
be much easier to do that on

01:43:11.160 --> 01:43:12.660
than let's say a chin up

01:43:12.660 --> 01:43:14.580
where you don't have the
control over the weight

01:43:14.580 --> 01:43:16.980
like you do by moving a pin on a stack.

01:43:16.980 --> 01:43:21.510
So I think that is a common
thing that people find

01:43:21.510 --> 01:43:23.520
and the best thing to
do is just figure out

01:43:23.520 --> 01:43:25.410
how deep you gripping in that bar.

01:43:25.410 --> 01:43:27.210
You're going to find that, oh
my God, I didn't realize that

01:43:27.210 --> 01:43:28.043
because it was just.

01:43:28.043 --> 01:43:30.540
Even though you might start
a set in a good position

01:43:30.540 --> 01:43:32.460
and then it drifts away as you go.

01:43:32.460 --> 01:43:34.500
- I think that's what was happening to me

01:43:34.500 --> 01:43:35.910
and I'm very conscious of this now.

01:43:35.910 --> 01:43:40.184
Again, for me, I haven't
had this elbow pain at all.

01:43:40.184 --> 01:43:42.120
- [Jeff] That's great.
- Very fortunate.

01:43:42.120 --> 01:43:45.060
So again, a debt of
gratitude to your never-

01:43:45.060 --> 01:43:47.365
I thought there was some
wrong in my elbow, basically.

01:43:47.365 --> 01:43:49.680
And I thought maybe it was tennis elbow

01:43:49.680 --> 01:43:50.894
I don't even play tennis.

01:43:50.894 --> 01:43:52.633
There you go.

01:43:52.633 --> 01:43:57.359
Other aspects of recovery
and variables for recovery.

01:43:57.359 --> 01:43:59.730
I think you and I both
put out content about

01:43:59.730 --> 01:44:02.190
the use of cold and I think
we can summarize it by saying,

01:44:02.190 --> 01:44:04.500
yeah, it does seem like
cold water immersion

01:44:04.500 --> 01:44:07.410
immediately after hypertrophy
restrains workouts

01:44:07.410 --> 01:44:08.243
might be a problem,

01:44:08.243 --> 01:44:09.660
but a cold shower is
probably not a problem.

01:44:09.660 --> 01:44:10.745
What about heat?

01:44:10.745 --> 01:44:15.745
Do you personally use
heat and cold saunas,

01:44:15.900 --> 01:44:17.974
hot baths, hot compresses.

01:44:17.974 --> 01:44:22.244
And by you, I mean you
personally and athletes

01:44:22.244 --> 01:44:25.440
that you coach or people that you coach,

01:44:25.440 --> 01:44:28.290
what are your thoughts on
the use of heat and or cold?

01:44:28.290 --> 01:44:32.550
- Well, I think it might
just be an inherited practice

01:44:32.550 --> 01:44:36.990
from the days of trainers
of since babe Ruth.

01:44:36.990 --> 01:44:38.040
But we in baseball

01:44:38.040 --> 01:44:40.890
we used a lot of cold
following performance.

01:44:40.890 --> 01:44:43.470
Just because the idea would be

01:44:43.470 --> 01:44:45.480
there is some especially pitchers,

01:44:45.480 --> 01:44:48.360
there is some inflammation
that is abnormal.

01:44:48.360 --> 01:44:50.640
The arm is not really
designed to do what they do

01:44:50.640 --> 01:44:53.010
especially at the speed that they move it

01:44:53.010 --> 01:44:53.880
and everything else.

01:44:53.880 --> 01:44:56.970
So we would use ice as a
pretty standard practice

01:44:56.970 --> 01:44:58.079
after that.

01:44:58.079 --> 01:45:01.650
But not a lot of heat
and use a lot of heat

01:45:01.650 --> 01:45:04.612
and of course from the
recovery or the healing aspect

01:45:04.612 --> 01:45:08.970
that actually becomes
rather personal preference

01:45:08.970 --> 01:45:13.830
they've found now after let's
say the first 12 to 24 hours

01:45:13.830 --> 01:45:15.600
where you're really trying
to control inflammation

01:45:15.600 --> 01:45:17.370
of what might be an injury.

01:45:17.370 --> 01:45:19.530
But then it can kind of
shift the personal preference

01:45:19.530 --> 01:45:22.800
because the heat can bring
blood to the area also

01:45:22.800 --> 01:45:27.270
and then the cold has its sort
of antiinflammatory effects.

01:45:27.270 --> 01:45:29.340
So like there's a balance between

01:45:29.340 --> 01:45:30.990
which one's working better for you.

01:45:30.990 --> 01:45:32.580
So there's really no standard anymore

01:45:32.580 --> 01:45:34.320
for heat or cold in that way.

01:45:34.320 --> 01:45:39.320
But from a standpoint of like
post-workout healthy status,

01:45:39.480 --> 01:45:42.750
I haven't used much heat or
cold in terms of what we do.

01:45:42.750 --> 01:45:44.520
We cover the topic of the cold showers

01:45:44.520 --> 01:45:47.416
and to try to dispel the myth of the,

01:45:47.416 --> 01:45:50.940
even people saying that there's
giant testosterone releases

01:45:50.940 --> 01:45:52.536
and you know all kinds of stuff that.

01:45:52.536 --> 01:45:53.927
Listen, we hear all kinds of things

01:45:53.927 --> 01:45:55.380
'cause people want-

01:45:55.380 --> 01:45:58.110
I think the idea of just
turning the water cold

01:45:58.110 --> 01:45:59.640
and being in it for 30 seconds

01:45:59.640 --> 01:46:00.473
and then all of a sudden

01:46:00.473 --> 01:46:02.430
magically growing three times your size

01:46:02.430 --> 01:46:04.500
is intriguing for a lot of people

01:46:04.500 --> 01:46:05.860
and that's why they ask these
questions 'cause they're like,

01:46:05.860 --> 01:46:07.470
well, that would be a hell of a lot easier

01:46:07.470 --> 01:46:09.870
than going to the gym and training hard.

01:46:09.870 --> 01:46:12.210
But like, I'm always
fascinated by some of the stuff

01:46:12.210 --> 01:46:13.320
that you talked about.

01:46:13.320 --> 01:46:15.300
In fact, we started to talk
about some of the stuff

01:46:15.300 --> 01:46:18.150
in terms of cooling and what
it can do on performance

01:46:18.150 --> 01:46:22.320
and that was like there's
some untapped territory there

01:46:22.320 --> 01:46:24.780
that I think you're finding out about.

01:46:24.780 --> 01:46:27.120
- Yeah, what would be fun
would be to bring the CoolMitt

01:46:27.120 --> 01:46:28.320
technology from Stanford.

01:46:28.320 --> 01:46:30.870
This is Craig Heller, my
colleague Craig Heller's lab

01:46:30.870 --> 01:46:34.650
at Stanford's done really
important and amazing work

01:46:34.650 --> 01:46:36.720
in this area but then it
moved on to some other things.

01:46:36.720 --> 01:46:38.190
He's also working on down syndrome

01:46:38.190 --> 01:46:40.470
and he works on a number of
other really important topics

01:46:40.470 --> 01:46:42.060
that scientists often do.

01:46:42.060 --> 01:46:44.640
But I have access to
this CoolMitt technology,

01:46:44.640 --> 01:46:46.320
no relationship to the
company, by the way,

01:46:46.320 --> 01:46:47.820
would love to come out to your facility

01:46:47.820 --> 01:46:52.110
and we can do the blind type studies.

01:46:52.110 --> 01:46:53.040
- Like the blue blocker test.

01:46:53.040 --> 01:46:54.651
- Yeah, exactly, exactly.

01:46:54.651 --> 01:46:59.028
And see how that goes in with somebody

01:46:59.028 --> 01:47:00.948
as advanced trained as you

01:47:00.948 --> 01:47:02.520
that's probably the best thing to do.

01:47:02.520 --> 01:47:04.953
So content for the future.

01:47:04.953 --> 01:47:06.780
Yeah, I think heat and cold

01:47:06.780 --> 01:47:08.640
are kind of staples in the PT world

01:47:08.640 --> 01:47:11.520
and it does seem like people
use them slightly differently

01:47:11.520 --> 01:47:15.150
but they are kind of the macro
nutrients of recovery there

01:47:15.150 --> 01:47:17.062
along with sleep.

01:47:17.062 --> 01:47:22.062
I do have a question about
precision of record keeping.

01:47:23.040 --> 01:47:25.410
Do you keep a training journal?

01:47:25.410 --> 01:47:27.720
Do you recommend people
keep training journals?

01:47:27.720 --> 01:47:31.410
Are you neurotically fixed
to cadence of movement

01:47:31.410 --> 01:47:32.820
and are you looking at the,

01:47:32.820 --> 01:47:34.410
do you have a buzzer going off

01:47:34.410 --> 01:47:35.700
when it's 90 seconds rest.

01:47:35.700 --> 01:47:36.990
Is it 90 seconds rest?

01:47:36.990 --> 01:47:40.051
I confess I have my slow
workouts and my faster workouts.

01:47:40.051 --> 01:47:42.630
And they scale with whether
or not I'm training heavier

01:47:42.630 --> 01:47:43.710
with longer rest.

01:47:43.710 --> 01:47:46.110
Or whether or not maybe
midway through a workout,

01:47:46.110 --> 01:47:48.360
I'll shift over to doing
higher repetition, lower rest.

01:47:48.360 --> 01:47:53.360
This is kind of my crude
way of keeping time.

01:47:53.430 --> 01:47:54.630
But I'm not-

01:47:54.630 --> 01:47:56.550
Will be just to kind of watch the clock.

01:47:56.550 --> 01:47:59.850
But I'm not neurotically
fixed to the buzzer

01:47:59.850 --> 01:48:02.430
nor am I on social media
during my workouts.

01:48:02.430 --> 01:48:05.040
Which is actually a way
to really improve workouts

01:48:05.040 --> 01:48:08.250
is to just not be on social media.

01:48:08.250 --> 01:48:10.530
- I can't claim that
I'm not guilty of that.

01:48:10.530 --> 01:48:12.090
Sometimes I am on social media

01:48:12.090 --> 01:48:13.590
but sometimes I'm trying
to post something.

01:48:13.590 --> 01:48:15.570
- Well, that's different,
it's your profession.

01:48:15.570 --> 01:48:16.644
It's your profession.

01:48:16.644 --> 01:48:21.644
- But I mean, I'm not necessarily chained

01:48:24.420 --> 01:48:27.270
to some sort of protocol
in terms of how I do.

01:48:27.270 --> 01:48:29.790
I think by this point I've
been doing this a long time

01:48:29.790 --> 01:48:31.620
and not only is it something
that I've done for a long time,

01:48:31.620 --> 01:48:33.750
but it's a passion of mine,
it's something I really enjoy.

01:48:33.750 --> 01:48:36.240
So I probably inherently have

01:48:36.240 --> 01:48:39.570
the ability to stick to these guidelines

01:48:39.570 --> 01:48:44.148
in terms of rest time
to know what I lifted.

01:48:44.148 --> 01:48:48.240
Even six months ago on
a lift and how it felt

01:48:48.240 --> 01:48:49.710
without journaling it.

01:48:49.710 --> 01:48:52.890
But I recognize the value
it has to a lot of people,

01:48:52.890 --> 01:48:55.920
it goes back to that whole
my muscle connection idea

01:48:55.920 --> 01:48:57.060
that we talked about in the beginning.

01:48:57.060 --> 01:48:58.890
Like there's a lack of awareness

01:48:58.890 --> 01:49:00.600
for all aspects of training.

01:49:00.600 --> 01:49:03.000
And especially maybe it isn't
like your interest level.

01:49:03.000 --> 01:49:07.200
We're talking you and I
from a position of interest.

01:49:07.200 --> 01:49:08.040
Like this is what we do.

01:49:08.040 --> 01:49:10.950
We enjoy just how our bodies work

01:49:10.950 --> 01:49:12.120
and understanding how they work.

01:49:12.120 --> 01:49:14.490
Some people don't care, they
just want the end result.

01:49:14.490 --> 01:49:17.520
But journaling and keeping track of that

01:49:17.520 --> 01:49:18.780
raises awareness to where like,

01:49:18.780 --> 01:49:21.660
oh my God, I have been on Instagram

01:49:21.660 --> 01:49:22.980
for the last seven minutes

01:49:22.980 --> 01:49:26.100
and I was supposed to be back
at my next set in 90 seconds.

01:49:26.100 --> 01:49:28.320
Like there is a training effect of that.

01:49:28.320 --> 01:49:32.130
Like if you're training
for a metabolic overload,

01:49:32.130 --> 01:49:34.530
you've blown that opportunity
because you haven't,

01:49:34.530 --> 01:49:38.940
your rest time very
important to that protocol,

01:49:38.940 --> 01:49:40.388
working as it should.

01:49:40.388 --> 01:49:42.090
If you were training for strength,

01:49:42.090 --> 01:49:44.190
maybe the extra few minutes
doesn't matter so much

01:49:44.190 --> 01:49:46.830
when you get back under
the bar you might find,

01:49:46.830 --> 01:49:49.260
you might find that it's a
better response for your body

01:49:49.260 --> 01:49:51.330
to rest even longer than you've been told

01:49:51.330 --> 01:49:52.953
three, four minutes, five minutes.

01:49:52.953 --> 01:49:54.960
And so that way maybe it helps,

01:49:54.960 --> 01:49:57.990
but I think that anything you can do

01:49:57.990 --> 01:50:00.360
to increase your awareness
of your performance

01:50:00.360 --> 01:50:04.380
and also give yourself
some objective goal.

01:50:04.380 --> 01:50:05.583
Whenever we have an objective goal,

01:50:05.583 --> 01:50:07.470
it's a lot easier to actually obtain it.

01:50:07.470 --> 01:50:09.570
When you're just there to get a pump

01:50:09.570 --> 01:50:12.753
and you're just there to
lift how you feel that day,

01:50:13.710 --> 01:50:15.420
you have to be incredibly disciplined

01:50:15.420 --> 01:50:17.160
in all other aspects of your workout

01:50:17.160 --> 01:50:18.960
in order to make that effective.

01:50:18.960 --> 01:50:20.040
And I've done that too.

01:50:20.040 --> 01:50:22.560
I've actually been able
to do that too, but again,

01:50:22.560 --> 01:50:25.320
the level of repetitions I've accumulated

01:50:25.320 --> 01:50:27.956
over the course of my
life in the amount that I

01:50:27.956 --> 01:50:30.086
read about this stuff.

01:50:30.086 --> 01:50:31.977
I think I'm able to get away with that,

01:50:31.977 --> 01:50:35.820
but I think more often
than not what I'm doing is

01:50:35.820 --> 01:50:38.850
not journaling but journaling in my head

01:50:38.850 --> 01:50:40.950
exactly what I think
people should be doing

01:50:40.950 --> 01:50:42.687
and that is getting a specific effect

01:50:42.687 --> 01:50:44.310
from what you're trying to do.

01:50:44.310 --> 01:50:45.750
It's not so haphazard.

01:50:45.750 --> 01:50:47.400
You want to get a specific effect

01:50:47.400 --> 01:50:49.230
just like any other
experiment that you're doing.

01:50:49.230 --> 01:50:51.000
If you're doing an
experiment on your own body

01:50:51.000 --> 01:50:51.900
with your own weight,

01:50:51.900 --> 01:50:54.780
which to me is one of the
most empowering things

01:50:54.780 --> 01:50:55.613
someone can ever do.

01:50:55.613 --> 01:50:57.110
When they get bitten by the bug

01:50:57.110 --> 01:51:00.450
for exercising and in training

01:51:00.450 --> 01:51:02.751
and I like to use the word
training rather than exercise

01:51:02.751 --> 01:51:03.990
'cause there's a purpose behind it.

01:51:03.990 --> 01:51:06.150
But when they get bitten
by that training bug

01:51:06.150 --> 01:51:08.760
and they start to see
actual changes and results,

01:51:08.760 --> 01:51:10.710
you know how empowering that
is 'cause we can't change,

01:51:10.710 --> 01:51:12.930
we can't really control that
many things in our life,

01:51:12.930 --> 01:51:13.770
unfortunately.

01:51:13.770 --> 01:51:15.180
And if there's some
things that happen to us

01:51:15.180 --> 01:51:16.833
that we really wish never happened.

01:51:16.833 --> 01:51:19.980
And those are not something
that we can do anything about,

01:51:19.980 --> 01:51:22.470
but this is one thing that
we can do our best to.

01:51:22.470 --> 01:51:24.270
We can't avoid disease entirely,

01:51:24.270 --> 01:51:25.860
we can't predict when we're going to die,

01:51:25.860 --> 01:51:27.960
we can't do those things,

01:51:27.960 --> 01:51:31.020
but we can certainly decide to
show up into the gym that day

01:51:31.020 --> 01:51:33.750
and get a workout in or go
for a run or do something

01:51:33.750 --> 01:51:36.150
and in by doing that, you're
giving yourself I think,

01:51:36.150 --> 01:51:38.040
a better chance at a
higher quality of life.

01:51:38.040 --> 01:51:41.070
So anything you could do to
increase your awareness of it

01:51:41.070 --> 01:51:44.880
and keep you on track with that
is like I'm endorsing fully.

01:51:44.880 --> 01:51:48.003
- Couldn't agree more,
I could not agree more.

01:51:48.003 --> 01:51:50.820
There is a topic it's
sort of a dreaded topic,

01:51:50.820 --> 01:51:51.900
but I think it's an important one

