02:00:53.790 --> 02:00:55.770
I mean that you could get the plate right

02:00:55.770 --> 02:00:57.510
but if the portions are out of control.

02:00:57.510 --> 02:01:00.033
- Plate has a dimensionality of height?

02:01:00.930 --> 02:01:03.140
- Or multiple plates like
second and third plate.

02:01:03.140 --> 02:01:04.079
- Or fourth.

02:01:04.079 --> 02:01:07.140
- Like then all these
things can be challenged

02:01:07.140 --> 02:01:10.920
but what I say is when you have your plate

02:01:10.920 --> 02:01:15.720
then you just simply look
at it as like a clock.

02:01:15.720 --> 02:01:19.770
And if you just make a 9:20 on the clock,

02:01:19.770 --> 02:01:22.110
so one arm goes over to the 9

02:01:22.110 --> 02:01:24.240
and one of the arms goes over to 20.

02:01:24.240 --> 02:01:25.800
Well, then you're basically...

02:01:25.800 --> 02:01:28.877
You're going to take the
second largest portion of that

02:01:28.877 --> 02:01:31.800
'cause you're going to make a
line towards 12 o'clock too.

02:01:31.800 --> 02:01:33.646
And the largest portion is going to be

02:01:33.646 --> 02:01:35.130
your fibers carbohydrate.

02:01:35.130 --> 02:01:38.640
So that's the green vegetables, right?

02:01:38.640 --> 02:01:41.640
So whether it be broccoli or
Brussels sprouts, or asparagus,

02:01:41.640 --> 02:01:45.330
or pick your favorites.

02:01:45.330 --> 02:01:46.740
Like those are the ones

02:01:46.740 --> 02:01:48.960
that give us a lot of the
micronutrients we need,

02:01:48.960 --> 02:01:50.203
they're the ones that are generally

02:01:50.203 --> 02:01:52.273
accepted as more healthy

02:01:52.273 --> 02:01:54.840
and they're also going
to provide the fiber

02:01:54.840 --> 02:01:56.400
that's going to be both beneficial

02:01:56.400 --> 02:01:58.530
in terms of its impact on insulin

02:01:58.530 --> 02:02:01.260
and also just through
filling you up, right?

02:02:01.260 --> 02:02:04.620
And then I take the next
largest portion of that

02:02:04.620 --> 02:02:06.537
and I devote that towards protein.

02:02:06.537 --> 02:02:07.860
And I think it's really important

02:02:07.860 --> 02:02:09.503
especially for anybody active,

02:02:09.503 --> 02:02:11.190
the more active you are

02:02:11.190 --> 02:02:13.230
the more you embark on
trying to build muscle

02:02:13.230 --> 02:02:15.060
you're going to need to
have protein in every meal.

02:02:15.060 --> 02:02:15.960
So I have that.

02:02:15.960 --> 02:02:17.880
And again, you know, here we're talking

02:02:17.880 --> 02:02:19.200
cleaner sources of protein.

02:02:19.200 --> 02:02:22.710
But like, you'll never find
like boiled chicken on my plate.

02:02:22.710 --> 02:02:25.800
Like I ditched those days
when I was 16 or 15 or 16,

02:02:25.800 --> 02:02:28.530
like I realized after reading
those body building magazines

02:02:28.530 --> 02:02:31.290
that maybe the low fat
thing stuck for too long,

02:02:31.290 --> 02:02:33.780
but the no fat thing stuck for too long,

02:02:33.780 --> 02:02:38.460
but the boiled chicken
and a steam broccoli thing

02:02:38.460 --> 02:02:39.293
that ended quickly for me

02:02:39.293 --> 02:02:41.460
'cause I really I'm not
going to eat this forever.

02:02:41.460 --> 02:02:44.370
So I'll have some sort of fish or chicken

02:02:44.370 --> 02:02:48.150
but it'll be cooked in a way that's like

02:02:48.150 --> 02:02:49.940
it's got maybe some sauce on it

02:02:49.940 --> 02:02:52.260
or it's got some maybe it's tomato sauce.

02:02:52.260 --> 02:02:54.270
Anything to just make it a
little bit more palatable

02:02:54.270 --> 02:02:56.580
and interesting without
blowing the value of the meal.

02:02:56.580 --> 02:02:57.780
And then that last portion

02:02:57.780 --> 02:03:00.060
is where I put my starchy carbohydrates.

02:03:00.060 --> 02:03:01.710
And again, that's the part
that some people will say

02:03:01.710 --> 02:03:03.420
exclude them entirely
'cause they're not healthy

02:03:03.420 --> 02:03:04.440
or they don't work for you

02:03:04.440 --> 02:03:06.930
or they're not beneficial long term.

02:03:06.930 --> 02:03:08.970
For me it's been a God sent.

02:03:08.970 --> 02:03:11.580
And I do think I'm like most people

02:03:11.580 --> 02:03:13.830
my body craves those carbohydrates.

02:03:13.830 --> 02:03:17.400
I choose things like sweet
potatoes which is my favorite

02:03:17.400 --> 02:03:21.150
or I'll have rice or I'll have pasta.

02:03:21.150 --> 02:03:22.860
I'm Italian so I like pasta.

02:03:22.860 --> 02:03:25.560
And like I will have those
things, I'm not excluding them,

02:03:25.560 --> 02:03:26.910
but I don't put them in the portions

02:03:26.910 --> 02:03:28.220
that you would generally find.

02:03:28.220 --> 02:03:30.540
My wife and I will go out

02:03:30.540 --> 02:03:32.370
and we'll go to the restaurant sometimes

02:03:32.370 --> 02:03:34.320
because we travel quite a bit

02:03:34.320 --> 02:03:36.330
or used to at least with baseball too.

02:03:36.330 --> 02:03:38.130
There's a cheesecake
factory everywhere you went

02:03:38.130 --> 02:03:39.870
and I love cheesecake factory,

02:03:39.870 --> 02:03:42.810
but like the way they structure meals is

02:03:42.810 --> 02:03:45.030
it's all rice at the bottom,

02:03:45.030 --> 02:03:46.260
and a little bit of chicken on top.

02:03:46.260 --> 02:03:48.570
And I mean, it's a plateful of rice

02:03:48.570 --> 02:03:50.820
that you wouldn't find
me make a plate that way.

02:03:50.820 --> 02:03:53.220
I'm going to just devote
that portion of the plate

02:03:53.220 --> 02:03:54.815
to the starchy carbohydrate.

02:03:54.815 --> 02:03:57.420
And so it gives me a little
bit more responsibility

02:03:57.420 --> 02:03:59.885
in terms of portion control
'cause those are the foods,

02:03:59.885 --> 02:04:03.630
again, probably dopamine driven that are

02:04:03.630 --> 02:04:05.310
most easily over eaten.

02:04:05.310 --> 02:04:06.510
I always ask the question,

02:04:06.510 --> 02:04:09.570
what's the last time you ate
10 chicken breasts at a meal?

02:04:09.570 --> 02:04:12.000
Like you're getting sick of
it after maybe two or three,

02:04:12.000 --> 02:04:14.640
but you could eat a whole hell
of a lot of carbohydrates,

02:04:14.640 --> 02:04:15.570
starchy carbohydrates

02:04:15.570 --> 02:04:18.120
because the they're just so satisfying

02:04:18.120 --> 02:04:20.640
and I think those triggers,
as you said, to want more,

02:04:20.640 --> 02:04:21.900
like that's what happens, right?

02:04:21.900 --> 02:04:23.610
You just keep eating, even
when you're feeling full,

02:04:23.610 --> 02:04:25.018
you want more.

02:04:25.018 --> 02:04:27.450
And that's the biggest
danger to carbohydrate.

02:04:27.450 --> 02:04:31.173
So if you can develop some
sort of discipline around them,

02:04:32.040 --> 02:04:33.210
then you can still enjoy them.

02:04:33.210 --> 02:04:37.080
If you can't develop that
discipline for whatever reason,

02:04:37.080 --> 02:04:38.520
then maybe they do become something

02:04:38.520 --> 02:04:39.930
that you have to work yourself around

02:04:39.930 --> 02:04:41.250
or adopt a different eating style.

02:04:41.250 --> 02:04:43.530
And as I said, I'm
never to the point where

02:04:43.530 --> 02:04:45.900
I'm not trying to be
dogmatic in my approach.

02:04:45.900 --> 02:04:48.270
I'm always trying to
say, this is how I do it

02:04:48.270 --> 02:04:49.530
and I'm a believer in it

02:04:49.530 --> 02:04:51.810
just like everyone else's
believer in their method,

02:04:51.810 --> 02:04:55.050
but I'm open to the idea that
something that works for you

02:04:55.050 --> 02:04:58.752
and gets you to a healthier
weight and a sustainability,

02:04:58.752 --> 02:05:01.710
like that is good, that's good for me.

02:05:01.710 --> 02:05:05.010
Provided doesn't introduce other issues.

02:05:05.010 --> 02:05:06.690
- Something one can do consistently,

02:05:06.690 --> 02:05:10.200
that's something I picked
up from you over the years.

02:05:10.200 --> 02:05:11.280
What can you do consistently?

02:05:11.280 --> 02:05:14.910
And for me, that also meant,
when, and how can I eat?

02:05:14.910 --> 02:05:16.320
What can I eat consistently

02:05:16.320 --> 02:05:18.420
that will also allow me
to be alert after lunch

02:05:18.420 --> 02:05:19.952
so I can actually get some work done.

02:05:19.952 --> 02:05:23.250
Or eat, I like to train
fasted in the morning

02:05:23.250 --> 02:05:24.660
but I don't do any long term fasting.

02:05:24.660 --> 02:05:28.230
It just so happens that I'm
fine doing water and caffeine

02:05:28.230 --> 02:05:30.210
in the morning and training in the morning

02:05:30.210 --> 02:05:32.280
and then I eat my first meal afterwards.

02:05:32.280 --> 02:05:34.020
But I get carbohydrates at night

02:05:34.020 --> 02:05:35.790
so my glycogen is restored.

02:05:35.790 --> 02:05:37.740
I think carbohydrates are wonderful.

02:05:37.740 --> 02:05:39.330
I just don't eat them in excess.

02:05:39.330 --> 02:05:41.729
So to me, I feel like when

02:05:41.729 --> 02:05:43.620
what you describe as a very rational

02:05:43.620 --> 02:05:45.450
literally balanced approach.

02:05:45.450 --> 02:05:46.952
And obviously there will be variations

02:05:46.952 --> 02:05:49.983
for people who are dealing
with obesity or diabetes.

02:05:51.000 --> 02:05:53.610
I've got friends that are
on the pure carnivore thing,

02:05:53.610 --> 02:05:54.690
I have friends that are vegan

02:05:54.690 --> 02:05:57.270
and it's always impressive to me

02:05:57.270 --> 02:06:01.256
when somebody can stick
to anything consistently

02:06:01.256 --> 02:06:04.500
except when they're sticking
to just poor behavior.

02:06:04.500 --> 02:06:06.720
'Cause there's nothing
impressive about that.

02:06:06.720 --> 02:06:09.450
Well, I think that's very helpful

02:06:09.450 --> 02:06:10.530
because I think there's,-

02:06:10.530 --> 02:06:13.369
For the typical listener of this podcast,

02:06:13.369 --> 02:06:16.440
the online content that people see,

02:06:16.440 --> 02:06:18.230
the battles are very confusing.

02:06:18.230 --> 02:06:20.010
They're distracting.

02:06:20.010 --> 02:06:20.843
Because people really think,

02:06:20.843 --> 02:06:22.737
oh, that there's a right
way and a wrong way

02:06:22.737 --> 02:06:24.785
and it sounds like the way that one can

02:06:24.785 --> 02:06:27.723
eat consistently over time that's healthy.

02:06:28.710 --> 02:06:30.960
Certainly fewer processed
and sugary foods,

02:06:30.960 --> 02:06:32.173
I think almost everybody agrees there.

02:06:32.173 --> 02:06:34.410
- Yeah, almost everyone
agrees on that, right?

02:06:34.410 --> 02:06:37.830
So I think it's calorie manipulation

02:06:37.830 --> 02:06:39.150
through some other method.

02:06:39.150 --> 02:06:42.840
So even intermittent
fasting, like you said,

02:06:42.840 --> 02:06:46.830
like that could be, it's
for people that are grazers.

02:06:46.830 --> 02:06:48.210
Like if you are a grazer

02:06:48.210 --> 02:06:49.980
and your real problem is portion control

02:06:49.980 --> 02:06:51.720
over the course of the day,

02:06:51.720 --> 02:06:53.700
but you can respond to a rule that says,

02:06:53.700 --> 02:06:56.043
no, you're eating between here and here,

02:06:56.043 --> 02:06:58.080
that you can obey that rule.

02:06:58.080 --> 02:07:00.180
Well, you're not going to be able to graze

02:07:00.180 --> 02:07:02.430
during the times that you might
be doing additional damage.

02:07:02.430 --> 02:07:06.600
So sure there's other hormonal benefits

02:07:06.600 --> 02:07:08.490
that people will talk
about from that approach.

02:07:08.490 --> 02:07:10.830
But from a longevity standpoint

02:07:10.830 --> 02:07:12.450
and habit forming standpoint,

02:07:12.450 --> 02:07:16.230
if it's fixing the habit that
you're breaking too often

02:07:16.230 --> 02:07:17.070
by eating throughout

02:07:17.070 --> 02:07:21.240
whenever you feel like you
walk by food, it's good

02:07:21.240 --> 02:07:22.073
and it works.

02:07:22.073 --> 02:07:25.530
And again, if people can, will tell you

02:07:25.530 --> 02:07:27.210
you can probably eat
whatever you want to eat

02:07:27.210 --> 02:07:29.520
as long as you're eating
within that window.

02:07:29.520 --> 02:07:31.560
But I think the more responsible people

02:07:31.560 --> 02:07:33.510
who are practitioners of that will say,

02:07:33.510 --> 02:07:35.730
no, you still want to
avoid processed sugar

02:07:35.730 --> 02:07:37.020
and things like that.

02:07:37.020 --> 02:07:40.320
And that's just a mechanism
of eating not really a diet.

02:07:40.320 --> 02:07:43.275
But like it's I think that people-

02:07:43.275 --> 02:07:47.430
I hate to be as like as
basic as it sounds with that

02:07:47.430 --> 02:07:49.320
but it's for the exact reason that

02:07:49.320 --> 02:07:53.015
if it it's that 23 hour day
phenomenon that it's like,

02:07:53.015 --> 02:07:55.350
you said you're impressed,
it is impressive.

02:07:55.350 --> 02:07:58.110
It's so hard to control
all of our behaviors

02:07:58.110 --> 02:07:59.537
and food being one of the hardest thing.

02:07:59.537 --> 02:08:02.010
One of the biggest temptations for people.

02:08:02.010 --> 02:08:04.230
You got to learn how to
control that for so long

02:08:04.230 --> 02:08:06.541
and then do it day after day after day,

02:08:06.541 --> 02:08:11.541
whatever that mechanism is that
works for you is impressive

02:08:11.821 --> 02:08:15.180
and I'm a believer in it.

02:08:15.180 --> 02:08:17.940
I think that's how I feel.

02:08:17.940 --> 02:08:19.380
I just feel like the
people need to be able

02:08:19.380 --> 02:08:20.670
to be given some reigns

02:08:20.670 --> 02:08:23.460
to be able to find what works for them.

02:08:23.460 --> 02:08:24.450
- Well, I love to eat

02:08:24.450 --> 02:08:26.070
and one of the beauties of weight training

02:08:26.070 --> 02:08:29.190
is I feel like I can eat plenty for my age

02:08:29.190 --> 02:08:30.870
and I'm not as lean as you are,

02:08:30.870 --> 02:08:33.343
but I'm happy with where I'm at.

02:08:33.343 --> 02:08:34.690
I could always do better.

02:08:34.690 --> 02:08:37.680
With each year actually I'm getting better

02:08:37.680 --> 02:08:39.120
probably because I'm eating cleaner.

02:08:39.120 --> 02:08:41.542
Probably 'cause I also have
someone to cook for me now.

02:08:41.542 --> 02:08:44.040
And we like...
I have that too.

02:08:44.040 --> 02:08:46.620
- We like healthy food
and so I'm very fortunate.

02:08:46.620 --> 02:08:49.860
I don't think we have any
packaged food in our home.

02:08:49.860 --> 02:08:52.037
We even started making sauerkraut
at home, I don't make it.

02:08:52.037 --> 02:08:53.940
I mean she makes it.

02:08:53.940 --> 02:08:56.850
- My wife actually she turned me on

02:08:56.850 --> 02:08:59.250
to a tip that I actually
shared with the whole channel

02:08:59.250 --> 02:09:03.240
which was like, you can go to,

02:09:03.240 --> 02:09:06.630
we have a Stew Leonard's around
our big grocery store chain

02:09:06.630 --> 02:09:08.730
around us and they have
a catering department.

02:09:08.730 --> 02:09:11.310
And they're often used
for catering big parties

02:09:11.310 --> 02:09:13.740
and big tubs of grilled chicken,

02:09:13.740 --> 02:09:15.930
but like really good grilled chicken.

02:09:15.930 --> 02:09:17.277
Again, not the boiled chicken

02:09:17.277 --> 02:09:19.020
and big tubs of sweet potatoes.

02:09:19.020 --> 02:09:22.170
And we'll get a bunch of those

02:09:22.170 --> 02:09:23.003
and she'll go over and she'll get them

02:09:23.003 --> 02:09:26.001
and then she'll sort of
arrange them on plates

02:09:26.001 --> 02:09:28.020
and put the plates in and like,

02:09:28.020 --> 02:09:29.670
I'm okay with repetitive eating.

02:09:29.670 --> 02:09:32.220
I think more people are probably
okay with repetitive eating

02:09:32.220 --> 02:09:33.053
than they think.

02:09:33.053 --> 02:09:35.721
I think that when you actually break down

02:09:35.721 --> 02:09:38.970
how many different breakfast
variations, do you have?

02:09:38.970 --> 02:09:40.260
Three, two.

02:09:40.260 --> 02:09:41.400
- Two or three maximum.

02:09:41.400 --> 02:09:43.500
- So like, I think when the
people do there's more variety

02:09:43.500 --> 02:09:45.210
for dinner probably but like

02:09:45.210 --> 02:09:48.120
even there you probably eat
five different types of dinners

02:09:48.120 --> 02:09:50.445
over the course of a week or a month.

02:09:50.445 --> 02:09:54.360
Well, if you have that ability to identify

02:09:54.360 --> 02:09:56.970
the things that you like, and
again, no plan is going to work

02:09:56.970 --> 02:09:58.110
if you're eating stuff you don't like.

02:09:58.110 --> 02:10:00.270
It's not going to work
forever, nothing will,

02:10:00.270 --> 02:10:01.920
you have to really enjoy
what you're eating.

02:10:01.920 --> 02:10:05.940
As long as these variations of this meal

02:10:05.940 --> 02:10:07.411
are something that you really enjoy

02:10:07.411 --> 02:10:10.380
and there are limited versions of them,

02:10:10.380 --> 02:10:13.365
the reproducibility of that is simple.

02:10:13.365 --> 02:10:16.020
It will take some time but
if you're fortunate enough

02:10:16.020 --> 02:10:18.420
in our case to have somebody
who can prepare it for you,

02:10:18.420 --> 02:10:20.640
now that's even part out of the equation.

02:10:20.640 --> 02:10:23.550
And it just makes it very simple.

02:10:23.550 --> 02:10:26.010
But I do think when you tally up

02:10:26.010 --> 02:10:28.095
all the costs of medical care

02:10:28.095 --> 02:10:31.726
that are spiked by having poor nutrition

02:10:31.726 --> 02:10:36.060
and you then offset that
by what it might cost you

02:10:36.060 --> 02:10:39.540
to invest in a faster strategy
like this catering trick

02:10:39.540 --> 02:10:40.830
or whatever it might be,

02:10:40.830 --> 02:10:42.870
you'd be best off figuring out a way

02:10:42.870 --> 02:10:44.670
to maybe reallocate some of your money

02:10:44.670 --> 02:10:47.220
to preparing this because
how important it is

02:10:47.220 --> 02:10:49.290
to your long term health and longevity.

02:10:49.290 --> 02:10:52.380
If you can figure out
your nutrition issues,

02:10:52.380 --> 02:10:53.850
if everyone listening to this podcast

02:10:53.850 --> 02:10:55.605
can figure out their nutrition issues,

02:10:55.605 --> 02:10:57.510
this whole world will be different.

02:10:57.510 --> 02:11:00.419
That is like one of the
largest sources of disease,

02:11:00.419 --> 02:11:02.190
and pain and discomfort

02:11:02.190 --> 02:11:04.200
because people really
struggle with nutrition.

02:11:04.200 --> 02:11:05.503
- Yeah, and it's a huge problem.

02:11:05.503 --> 02:11:09.630
I mean the obesity, it is
an epidemic in this country.

02:11:09.630 --> 02:11:11.190
It's a very, very serious.

02:11:11.190 --> 02:11:14.370
Also a lot of highly processed
foods are more expensive

02:11:14.370 --> 02:11:17.306
than healthier foods when
you really break it down.

02:11:17.306 --> 02:11:21.150
Even the better sourced high quality foods

02:11:21.150 --> 02:11:24.849
are right there on par less
than the processed foods

02:11:24.849 --> 02:11:27.231
for sure.

02:11:27.231 --> 02:11:31.560
But couple other questions
as it relates to training.

02:11:31.560 --> 02:11:35.508
Because I think that one
thing that a lot of people

02:11:35.508 --> 02:11:37.650
wonder about and maybe we could do this

02:11:37.650 --> 02:11:40.173
in kind of a true false method

02:11:40.173 --> 02:11:43.008
just to get through some of these...

02:11:43.008 --> 02:11:44.550
- 50/50 I'll get it right at least.

02:11:44.550 --> 02:11:46.170
- Exactly.

02:11:46.170 --> 02:11:48.300
Men and women should train differently.

02:11:48.300 --> 02:11:50.050
- The science of it will say false.

02:11:53.010 --> 02:11:54.720
And again, not to generalize,

02:11:54.720 --> 02:11:57.480
but kind of the point you
touched on earlier today.

02:11:57.480 --> 02:12:02.480
I do find that casually
interested women in training

02:12:02.700 --> 02:12:06.000
will migrate more towards
certain types of fitness,

02:12:06.000 --> 02:12:09.330
like kickboxing, like dancing, like,

02:12:09.330 --> 02:12:10.740
- Low rest circuit type.

02:12:10.740 --> 02:12:11.573
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

02:12:11.573 --> 02:12:12.690
And I think, again,

02:12:12.690 --> 02:12:15.712
whatever is that you are
going to engage in regularly

02:12:15.712 --> 02:12:17.370
is what you should do.

02:12:17.370 --> 02:12:18.510
Physiologically, no.

02:12:18.510 --> 02:12:20.970
And I think if we can get more women

02:12:20.970 --> 02:12:23.182
to feel more comfortable in the gym

02:12:23.182 --> 02:12:25.953
performing the same
exercises and the same,

02:12:26.790 --> 02:12:29.070
in receiving the same
strength training benefits

02:12:29.070 --> 02:12:30.900
and working on progressive
overload and like,

02:12:30.900 --> 02:12:32.340
we've hit the holy grail.

02:12:32.340 --> 02:12:35.880
But I think that it's a big bridge

02:12:35.880 --> 02:12:37.350
that has to be gapped still

02:12:37.350 --> 02:12:41.040
because there's just some reality to-

02:12:41.040 --> 02:12:46.020
Listen, there are very, my wife
is a perfect example of this

02:12:46.020 --> 02:12:48.708
living a very complicated busy life.

02:12:48.708 --> 02:12:51.317
We have two young boys they're twins

02:12:51.317 --> 02:12:55.440
and her attention and focus is there.

02:12:55.440 --> 02:12:58.710
And it's like, she doesn't do
this for a living like I do.

02:12:58.710 --> 02:13:02.070
And if she can get a decent
workout and she's happy,

02:13:02.070 --> 02:13:05.177
but she's not necessarily
working on her deadlift PR.

02:13:05.177 --> 02:13:09.120
And so I think that that
would help her and serve her

02:13:09.120 --> 02:13:13.047
in the long term to work
on increasing her PRs,

02:13:13.047 --> 02:13:15.510
and different lifts and building
her strength progressively.

02:13:15.510 --> 02:13:19.710
But in her life right now is
not necessarily in the cards

02:13:19.710 --> 02:13:21.510
to have the time to focus on that.

02:13:21.510 --> 02:13:23.314
So would you then discourage

02:13:23.314 --> 02:13:25.950
this other thing that she
might find interesting,

02:13:25.950 --> 02:13:27.961
like some boxing.

02:13:27.961 --> 02:13:31.230
There was a little, I
don't remember the brand,

02:13:31.230 --> 02:13:34.230
but like one of those
punchable boxing standup things

02:13:34.230 --> 02:13:35.542
and she enjoys it.

02:13:35.542 --> 02:13:40.542
Like anything to get you
moving is going be preferable

02:13:40.980 --> 02:13:43.045
but I don't think that
necessarily physiologically

02:13:43.045 --> 02:13:44.730
there's a difference.

02:13:44.730 --> 02:13:46.320
- You started weight
training pretty young.

02:13:46.320 --> 02:13:47.490
- Yeah.

02:13:47.490 --> 02:13:50.100
I messed around with my brother

02:13:50.100 --> 02:13:51.570
'cause he was older, he
was four years older.

02:13:51.570 --> 02:13:53.490
So I was kind of messing
around with weights probably

02:13:53.490 --> 02:13:55.415
12 or 13 with a 5 pound dumbbell.

02:13:55.415 --> 02:13:58.200
- Yeah, you hear that young
kids shouldn't work out

02:13:58.200 --> 02:13:59.033
with the weights.

02:13:59.033 --> 02:14:00.480
I don't know what the going standard is.

02:14:00.480 --> 02:14:03.907
Now they say, shuts down long
bone growth or growth plates

02:14:03.907 --> 02:14:05.910
this sort of thing.

02:14:05.910 --> 02:14:08.070
You've got two young boys
adorable kids by the way.

02:14:08.070 --> 02:14:09.120
- [Jeff] Thanks.

02:14:09.120 --> 02:14:12.085
- One of the things that is
very heartwarming is to see

02:14:12.085 --> 02:14:14.700
you're in great shape, you're
clearly extremely bright,

02:14:14.700 --> 02:14:16.466
you know your craft, you loved your craft.

02:14:16.466 --> 02:14:20.160
You work with Jesse, who
we'll talk about as well.

02:14:20.160 --> 02:14:22.170
- [Jeff] That's great.
- Which is great.

02:14:22.170 --> 02:14:23.910
There's a camaraderie there,

02:14:23.910 --> 02:14:25.920
having great teammates
as part of a business

02:14:25.920 --> 02:14:28.440
or to work out with is
just makes life better.

02:14:28.440 --> 02:14:29.580
Let's just be honest.

02:14:29.580 --> 02:14:31.650
I'm grateful to have great
teammates for the podcast

02:14:31.650 --> 02:14:33.243
and my lab, of course, as well.

02:14:34.410 --> 02:14:38.673
But to see your boys and your
dogs and the whole picture,

02:14:40.814 --> 02:14:42.660
I'm sure it has a lot of
contours and complexity

02:14:42.660 --> 02:14:44.460
that we don't know about
and shouldn't know about,

02:14:44.460 --> 02:14:46.659
but it's a beautiful picture.

02:14:46.659 --> 02:14:48.870
And will they weight train?

02:14:48.870 --> 02:14:51.180
I've seen the videos
of one or both of them

02:14:51.180 --> 02:14:52.013
hanging from the bar.

02:14:52.013 --> 02:14:54.300
- These kids are naturals,
I'm telling you that.

02:14:54.300 --> 02:14:55.896
- I wonder where they get it from.

02:14:55.896 --> 02:14:58.500
- I don't even encourage it.

02:14:58.500 --> 02:15:00.240
I'm not going to be the dad
who's sitting there saying,

02:15:00.240 --> 02:15:01.375
let's go somewhere.

02:15:01.375 --> 02:15:02.970
We got our two days.

02:15:02.970 --> 02:15:07.320
I'm not going to do that but
they have a natural interest

02:15:07.320 --> 02:15:08.153
in the gym.

02:15:08.153 --> 02:15:09.780
They just sometimes like
to be out with daddy.

02:15:09.780 --> 02:15:10.983
So they'll come out there.

02:15:10.983 --> 02:15:15.660
I of the two of us, my wife
and I will be the one who

02:15:15.660 --> 02:15:17.700
has a little bit more of a longer leash

02:15:17.700 --> 02:15:19.110
to let them explore things

02:15:19.110 --> 02:15:20.970
'cause I was a dummy at times too

02:15:20.970 --> 02:15:23.255
and figured out best
through the mistakes I made.

02:15:23.255 --> 02:15:24.480
- Through injury, right.

02:15:24.480 --> 02:15:26.550
In neuroscience we call
that one trial learning.

02:15:26.550 --> 02:15:27.450
- There you go.

02:15:27.450 --> 02:15:29.190
These guys are going to be
masters in one trial learning

02:15:29.190 --> 02:15:33.030
because you know, they'll
go grab the bars of my,

02:15:33.030 --> 02:15:34.800
the handles of my jamer that's there

02:15:34.800 --> 02:15:36.240
'cause it's at a lower level to them

02:15:36.240 --> 02:15:37.320
and they're swinging around

02:15:37.320 --> 02:15:39.360
they're doing pull-ups
on it naturally uncoached

02:15:39.360 --> 02:15:40.950
nothing from me.

02:15:40.950 --> 02:15:43.710
One will walk up to a deadlift
bar stand over it naturally

02:15:43.710 --> 02:15:44.910
never saw me do it,

02:15:44.910 --> 02:15:47.820
stands over there and just
goes, he tries to pull it.

02:15:47.820 --> 02:15:51.180
So there's a definitely an
inclination to liking the gym

02:15:51.180 --> 02:15:52.950
and I will fully support that.

02:15:52.950 --> 02:15:55.690
But of course body weight will
be good for quite a while.

02:15:55.690 --> 02:15:57.930
- Yeah, so what age do
you think is reasonable

02:15:57.930 --> 02:16:00.873
for kids to start exploring
a non body weight training?

02:16:01.740 --> 02:16:03.150
- I think around 13.

02:16:03.150 --> 02:16:03.983
I think around 13.

02:16:03.983 --> 02:16:06.333
Once puberty, I think
it's okay to start to.

02:16:08.125 --> 02:16:10.200
'Cause there's so much,

02:16:10.200 --> 02:16:12.810
I even say for people
that are like later in age

02:16:12.810 --> 02:16:13.680
who are just starting out,

02:16:13.680 --> 02:16:14.880
learn with your own body weight first,

02:16:14.880 --> 02:16:16.590
there's plenty of resistance to be had

02:16:16.590 --> 02:16:18.690
by learning how to command
your body in space.

02:16:18.690 --> 02:16:21.630
So if you have never trained before,

02:16:21.630 --> 02:16:23.490
you're going to get very stimulated

02:16:23.490 --> 02:16:25.440
by doing lunging and reverse lunging,

02:16:25.440 --> 02:16:27.240
even learning some of the
appropriate reception around

02:16:27.240 --> 02:16:28.500
movement through space.

02:16:28.500 --> 02:16:29.400
Pull-ups, chin ups.

02:16:29.400 --> 02:16:30.480
Pull-ups and chin ups are challenging

02:16:30.480 --> 02:16:33.030
for even people that have had
20, 30 years of experience

02:16:33.030 --> 02:16:33.863
in the gym.

02:16:33.863 --> 02:16:37.230
So there's a lot of stimulus
to be had by body weight

02:16:37.230 --> 02:16:40.770
and jumping straight to
dumbbells or barbells

02:16:40.770 --> 02:16:42.210
is actually doing yourself a disservice.

02:16:42.210 --> 02:16:44.970
You can learn better command
of your body in space

02:16:44.970 --> 02:16:47.330
so that when you go back
to the bigger lifts,

02:16:47.330 --> 02:16:49.650
you're going to have an easier time

02:16:49.650 --> 02:16:51.180
sort of progressively loading them

02:16:51.180 --> 02:16:52.950
and building up that
foundation of strength.

02:16:52.950 --> 02:16:54.330
I'm not saying that you have to become

02:16:54.330 --> 02:16:56.040
a master calisthenics athlete

02:16:56.040 --> 02:16:58.170
before you can touch a
barbell, that's not even true.

02:16:58.170 --> 02:17:00.240
I'm just saying there's so much capacity.

02:17:00.240 --> 02:17:01.530
Kids are going to be doing this anyway.

02:17:01.530 --> 02:17:03.720
And really just, if you
look at general play,

02:17:03.720 --> 02:17:06.360
they are jumping, they are
lunging, they are climbing,

02:17:06.360 --> 02:17:08.583
they are pulling like,
that's what they do.

02:17:09.614 --> 02:17:14.190
I don't know where the avoidance
of like structured training

02:17:14.190 --> 02:17:15.630
is for younger kids.

02:17:15.630 --> 02:17:17.220
Again, provided they're using body weight

02:17:17.220 --> 02:17:21.540
and maybe less ballistic
movements or something like that.

02:17:21.540 --> 02:17:24.602
Things that are, or certainly
overloaded movements.

02:17:24.602 --> 02:17:26.880
I think we should
encourage kids to do more.

02:17:26.880 --> 02:17:31.170
There's a lot of obesity
in kids on the rise also

02:17:31.170 --> 02:17:34.200
and that is incredibly
disconcerting to me.

02:17:34.200 --> 02:17:36.810
So I think and I hope it doesn't come from

02:17:36.810 --> 02:17:38.130
the advice of some that say,

02:17:38.130 --> 02:17:40.770
well, wait until you're older
to start doing something like.

02:17:40.770 --> 02:17:42.630
That's a way worse trade off

02:17:42.630 --> 02:17:45.360
than engaging in something smart now.

02:17:45.360 --> 02:17:47.250
- We used to get kicked out of
the house when we were kids.

02:17:47.250 --> 02:17:48.750
- [Jeff] Totally.
- My mom would kick us out.

02:17:48.750 --> 02:17:52.290
I had a huge pack of boys
that lived on my street

02:17:52.290 --> 02:17:53.745
but we'd get kicked outside.

02:17:53.745 --> 02:17:57.630
Like literal, you're not
allowed in, no television.

02:17:57.630 --> 02:17:59.190
But there were video games, of course

02:17:59.190 --> 02:18:00.570
but we were kicked out the house.

02:18:00.570 --> 02:18:01.500
We had to go play.

02:18:01.500 --> 02:18:02.790
For us it was skateboarding, soccer

02:18:02.790 --> 02:18:04.710
and then we'd find our trouble.

02:18:04.710 --> 02:18:09.313
But so post training nutrition.

02:18:09.313 --> 02:18:13.590
We're the same age, years
ago I was sort of neurotic

02:18:13.590 --> 02:18:15.000
about the idea that I had to ingest

02:18:15.000 --> 02:18:17.220
a certain amount of
carbohydrates and proteins

02:18:17.220 --> 02:18:20.580
within two hours then it
was 90 minutes of training.

02:18:20.580 --> 02:18:22.650
I confess I get, if I train hard,

02:18:22.650 --> 02:18:23.970
so I'm talking about
the resistance training

02:18:23.970 --> 02:18:24.900
not the running.

02:18:24.900 --> 02:18:28.920
But the resistance training,
60 to 90 minutes later,

02:18:28.920 --> 02:18:30.510
I'm really hungry.

02:18:30.510 --> 02:18:32.520
But there have been days when I just skip

02:18:32.520 --> 02:18:35.220
and then the hunger passes
and then later I eat more.

02:18:35.220 --> 02:18:37.020
I might eat twice as much later

02:18:37.020 --> 02:18:39.120
that's just the way
sometimes schedules go.

02:18:39.120 --> 02:18:41.460
But what are your thoughts in terms of

02:18:41.460 --> 02:18:45.360
the nutrition science, the
training related effects

02:18:45.360 --> 02:18:47.430
of the post-training meal?

02:18:47.430 --> 02:18:50.130
Is it something that you try to get?

02:18:50.130 --> 02:18:52.950
Is it something you think
people should pay attention to?

02:18:52.950 --> 02:18:55.650
- So that science has
actually probably been

02:18:55.650 --> 02:18:58.410
the one that's changed the
most in my lifetime, honestly,

02:18:58.410 --> 02:19:00.720
because, again, we're at the same age

02:19:00.720 --> 02:19:04.272
and I was falling for the same trap

02:19:04.272 --> 02:19:08.472
where I would really be focused on like,

02:19:08.472 --> 02:19:11.580
I'm risking speeding tickets
driving home from the gym

02:19:11.580 --> 02:19:14.100
to make you sure I got an anabolic window.

02:19:14.100 --> 02:19:16.680
Like I did all that, I really did.

02:19:16.680 --> 02:19:20.400
But thankfully that's been
sort of debunked in your body

02:19:20.400 --> 02:19:23.580
isn't just rushing through
these certain periods of time

02:19:23.580 --> 02:19:25.080
to utilize the nutrients in our body,

02:19:25.080 --> 02:19:27.690
but are able to partition
them and use them over a long,

02:19:27.690 --> 02:19:29.070
much greater duration.

02:19:29.070 --> 02:19:30.450
Up to now, they're saying,

02:19:30.450 --> 02:19:32.490
three to four hours after training,

02:19:32.490 --> 02:19:33.660
five hours after training,

02:19:33.660 --> 02:19:36.785
you could still see the
benefits of replenishment.

02:19:36.785 --> 02:19:38.316
A lot of that is just

02:19:38.316 --> 02:19:40.900
I think there's a
consistency element to it

02:19:42.150 --> 02:19:46.800
that just utilizing a post workout window

02:19:46.800 --> 02:19:48.510
or a post workout meal,

02:19:48.510 --> 02:19:51.090
even if it's within two hours or one hour

02:19:51.090 --> 02:19:53.280
is just integrating the habit of saying,

02:19:53.280 --> 02:19:55.680
listen, I just did this activity

02:19:55.680 --> 02:19:58.440
and now want to replenish
some of what I lost the energy

02:19:58.440 --> 02:20:02.040
that I used to perform
the exercises that I did.

02:20:02.040 --> 02:20:05.489
And just getting into
the routine knowing that

02:20:05.489 --> 02:20:09.600
the engine is ultimately
fed by what we put in it.

02:20:09.600 --> 02:20:14.040
And the concept of
replenishing the fuel lost

02:20:14.040 --> 02:20:16.020
is still a concept that I think again,

02:20:16.020 --> 02:20:18.390
different in mechanism but still important

02:20:18.390 --> 02:20:20.610
in terms of fueling the
overall performance.

02:20:20.610 --> 02:20:24.166
So the pre-workout period of time

02:20:24.166 --> 02:20:28.080
gives us a chance to
actually have a longer window

02:20:28.080 --> 02:20:31.530
because if those nutrients
are obtained pre-workout,

02:20:31.530 --> 02:20:34.680
it's not like they're gone in
that hour that you've trained,

02:20:34.680 --> 02:20:36.600
they're still there and
available for your body to use.

02:20:36.600 --> 02:20:40.470
So I think it's important
to get one of the two right

02:20:40.470 --> 02:20:42.569
or at least make sure you're consistently

02:20:42.569 --> 02:20:45.360
having one or the two

02:20:45.360 --> 02:20:48.330
or you might risk going
through all these periods

02:20:48.330 --> 02:20:51.660
of having no nutrition
to support your efforts.

02:20:51.660 --> 02:20:53.430
Not only will your
workouts potentially suffer

02:20:53.430 --> 02:20:54.570
in terms of the output,

02:20:54.570 --> 02:20:56.670
but then you're also
not providing your body

02:20:56.670 --> 02:21:01.320
any ability to capitalize
on an opportunity to feed it

02:21:01.320 --> 02:21:02.850
and refuel and recover.

02:21:02.850 --> 02:21:07.850
So I'm not very dogmatic
about what specifically to eat

02:21:09.540 --> 02:21:11.460
pre or post workout.

02:21:11.460 --> 02:21:12.990
But I do think you should have protein

02:21:12.990 --> 02:21:15.690
surrounding your training,

02:21:15.690 --> 02:21:18.000
whether that be ahead of time or after.

02:21:18.000 --> 02:21:20.671
Protein could be a
little bit hard to digest

02:21:20.671 --> 02:21:21.750
for some people.

02:21:21.750 --> 02:21:23.490
So if you do that pre-workout

02:21:23.490 --> 02:21:25.530
and then you're finding
your workout is slogging

02:21:25.530 --> 02:21:27.000
because you don't feel good

02:21:27.000 --> 02:21:29.160
then suddenly you put
that after your meal.

02:21:29.160 --> 02:21:32.070
But this whole concept
of the urgency of time

02:21:32.070 --> 02:21:34.710
has thankfully been removed

02:21:34.710 --> 02:21:38.540
and we can just learn to eat
a little bit more responsibly

02:21:39.750 --> 02:21:41.806
and drive more responsibly so we're not

02:21:41.806 --> 02:21:43.290
trying to rush home from the gym

02:21:43.290 --> 02:21:45.093
and risk killing people on the way.

02:21:47.380 --> 02:21:49.230
But I think it's great
because I think that

02:21:49.230 --> 02:21:50.910
that was something that it just showcases

02:21:50.910 --> 02:21:53.309
a belief that people had for so long

02:21:53.309 --> 02:21:56.850
that has since been proven
to be not that important.

02:21:56.850 --> 02:22:00.360
And there's a tip of the cap
towards research in a good way

02:22:00.360 --> 02:22:01.193
where it's like, all right,

02:22:01.193 --> 02:22:03.218
I think we could all agree
that this isn't necessarily

02:22:03.218 --> 02:22:05.048
true anymore.

02:22:05.048 --> 02:22:07.421
And look at yourself and say,

02:22:07.421 --> 02:22:10.560
oh my God, I did that so often.

02:22:10.560 --> 02:22:14.310
I bit that one hook line
and sinker but then realize,

02:22:14.310 --> 02:22:15.750
okay, we could always make a change.

02:22:15.750 --> 02:22:17.190
And the good thing about nutrition is

02:22:17.190 --> 02:22:19.740
those changes can happen the
very next time you go to eat

02:22:19.740 --> 02:22:21.600
and you'll start to see
the benefits of that.

02:22:21.600 --> 02:22:26.600
So I'm not a big believer
in that strict approach

02:22:26.670 --> 02:22:28.991
to pre or post-workout.

02:22:28.991 --> 02:22:32.133
I mean, even as far as
pre-workout supplements,

02:22:32.991 --> 02:22:34.230
a lot of people don't take them.

02:22:34.230 --> 02:22:36.060
A lot of people don't like
them, they don't take them,

02:22:36.060 --> 02:22:39.000
they don't like, they're not
necessarily even being used

02:22:39.000 --> 02:22:42.180
as the new nutritive
side of the pre-workout.

02:22:42.180 --> 02:22:45.214
They're just more used
to fuel the workout.

02:22:45.214 --> 02:22:49.170
- For me it's water and
some form of caffeine.

02:22:49.170 --> 02:22:50.003
- Yeah.

02:22:50.003 --> 02:22:51.180
I mean, it is whatever,

02:22:51.180 --> 02:22:52.410
again, I think it's important.

02:22:52.410 --> 02:22:53.820
I do think it's important to maintain

02:22:53.820 --> 02:22:55.080
a high level of output.

02:22:55.080 --> 02:22:58.320
So if your pre-work attrition requires

02:22:58.320 --> 02:23:00.270
a stimulant in order to help you do that

02:23:00.270 --> 02:23:01.759
or if your pre-work nutrition

02:23:01.759 --> 02:23:05.010
is causing you to have
a harder time to train

02:23:05.010 --> 02:23:07.800
because you're feeling full or stomachache

02:23:07.800 --> 02:23:09.720
or something else, then
that that's not achieving

02:23:09.720 --> 02:23:10.553
what you're trying to do.

02:23:10.553 --> 02:23:12.600
The ultimate goal is to
still be able to perform

02:23:12.600 --> 02:23:13.620
at the highest level.

02:23:13.620 --> 02:23:15.169
So whatever your nutrition is required

02:23:15.169 --> 02:23:17.220
to allow you to still do that,

02:23:17.220 --> 02:23:19.800
that is probably the most
important factor of all of it.

02:23:19.800 --> 02:23:22.530
- Great, I love the very
clear and rational approach.

02:23:22.530 --> 02:23:24.870
Don't ingest anything
right before your workout

02:23:24.870 --> 02:23:26.580
or near your workout

02:23:26.580 --> 02:23:27.960
that's going to make your workout worse.

02:23:27.960 --> 02:23:29.670
Yeah, and it's so simple

02:23:29.670 --> 02:23:30.810
and yet you don't hear this

02:23:30.810 --> 02:23:31.740
because I think people will think,

02:23:31.740 --> 02:23:33.330
oh, they must have a pre-workout

02:23:33.330 --> 02:23:34.620
they must have a post-workout.

02:23:34.620 --> 02:23:37.514
- No, again, even if the
benefits that are to be had

02:23:37.514 --> 02:23:40.110
from whatever's being suggested

02:23:40.110 --> 02:23:41.970
is going to be easily
offset by the fact that

02:23:41.970 --> 02:23:43.890
you can't perform at an output capable

02:23:43.890 --> 02:23:46.200
of driving any change.

02:23:46.200 --> 02:23:49.034
So that would pretty
much negate the fact that

02:23:49.034 --> 02:23:51.330
you're not outweighing those benefits

02:23:51.330 --> 02:23:53.489
of whatever nutritive approach you took

02:23:53.489 --> 02:23:55.590
and is struggling through your workout.

02:23:55.590 --> 02:23:58.260
- For me, the best pre-workout
is a good night's sleep,

02:23:58.260 --> 02:24:00.360
hydration, caffeine, and music.

02:24:00.360 --> 02:24:01.959
- Yeah, there you go.

02:24:01.959 --> 02:24:03.360
I mean, that's a simple formula.

02:24:03.360 --> 02:24:04.193
- It works.

02:24:04.193 --> 02:24:06.330
And then post what I do I
do find I get quite hungry

02:24:06.330 --> 02:24:07.840
and want to eat quite a bit more and...

02:24:07.840 --> 02:24:10.530
- Well, that's a natural
response the body's going to

02:24:10.530 --> 02:24:13.140
and most people want to do that
and I think it should be fed.

02:24:13.140 --> 02:24:15.060
I work out as you know,

02:24:15.060 --> 02:24:17.827
again, a lot of my postings
on Instagram will happen

02:24:17.827 --> 02:24:20.550
at 10 o'clock at night,
10:30 at night, 11 at night,

02:24:20.550 --> 02:24:21.780
'cause I am actually training there

02:24:21.780 --> 02:24:23.070
and that's where I'm
taking those little breaks

02:24:23.070 --> 02:24:25.410
in between sets to actually
film or post something.

02:24:25.410 --> 02:24:27.450
But like, I then go inside and eat dinner.

02:24:27.450 --> 02:24:29.521
So I'm eating at 11 o'clock at night.

02:24:29.521 --> 02:24:31.800
It's not necessarily ideal,

02:24:31.800 --> 02:24:33.870
I'm not recommending that
as a tool for anybody.

02:24:33.870 --> 02:24:35.310
I think it dispels one thing,

02:24:35.310 --> 02:24:38.596
I've never been a believer
in Kenny carbs after six.

02:24:38.596 --> 02:24:40.211
- That makes no sense to me.

02:24:40.211 --> 02:24:41.640
- [Jeff] Zero sense.
- Based on all the new,

02:24:41.640 --> 02:24:45.091
all the signs of metabolism
that I've seen makes no sense.

02:24:45.091 --> 02:24:47.070
I think as long as you
can, sort of like napping.

02:24:47.070 --> 02:24:49.650
I talked to Matt Walker, one
of the great sleep researchers

02:24:49.650 --> 02:24:51.480
wrote why we sleep, et cetera,

02:24:51.480 --> 02:24:54.360
and has his own podcast about
sleep, tremendous researcher,

02:24:54.360 --> 02:24:56.400
public communicator
about sleep and he said,

02:24:56.400 --> 02:24:58.380
naps are fine provided
they don't interrupt

02:24:58.380 --> 02:25:00.600
your ability to sleep well at night.

02:25:00.600 --> 02:25:01.433
Simple.

02:25:01.433 --> 02:25:03.120
Some people can sleep
from eight to 9:00 PM

02:25:03.120 --> 02:25:04.860
and then go to bed at
midnight and not a problem.

02:25:04.860 --> 02:25:06.840
Other people they take a
30 minute nap after lunch

02:25:06.840 --> 02:25:08.160
and they can't sleep at night.

02:25:08.160 --> 02:25:08.993
Same thing with-

02:25:08.993 --> 02:25:09.870
Caffeine's a little different

02:25:09.870 --> 02:25:11.910
because Matt would argue
the architecture of sleep

02:25:11.910 --> 02:25:12.930
can be disrupted, et cetera.

02:25:12.930 --> 02:25:16.470
But if you can eat dinner late
and eat carbohydrates late,

02:25:16.470 --> 02:25:17.820
I actually need carbohydrates at night

02:25:17.820 --> 02:25:19.380
in order to be able to sleep.

02:25:19.380 --> 02:25:22.800
Whenever I've done a low
carbohydrate type regimen

02:25:22.800 --> 02:25:24.780
in the evening, I have a
hard time falling asleep.

02:25:24.780 --> 02:25:26.010
I'm just too alert.

02:25:26.010 --> 02:25:28.500
And so I eat carbohydrates in the evening

02:25:28.500 --> 02:25:29.700
to restore glycogen

02:25:29.700 --> 02:25:33.277
but also in order to make
sure that I can fall asleep.

02:25:33.277 --> 02:25:35.220
- I actually can,

02:25:35.220 --> 02:25:36.990
again obviously it's
already late at night,

02:25:36.990 --> 02:25:38.070
by the time I'm done eating,

02:25:38.070 --> 02:25:40.830
but like I can fall asleep
within 5, 10 minutes

02:25:40.830 --> 02:25:41.970
of finishing my meal.

02:25:41.970 --> 02:25:44.885
Because I do think that they
have that same effect on me,

02:25:44.885 --> 02:25:46.324
but I'm never,

02:25:46.324 --> 02:25:49.200
I'm not bothered by the
feeling of fullness.

02:25:49.200 --> 02:25:52.053
I'm not unable to sleep because
of a feeling of fullness.

02:25:52.920 --> 02:25:56.010
But I do like the fact that

02:25:56.010 --> 02:25:58.320
I feel as if I'm at least replenishing

02:25:58.320 --> 02:26:00.150
what was lost through my hard training.

02:26:00.150 --> 02:26:02.640
And I do like to back it up with a dinner.

02:26:02.640 --> 02:26:04.500
I don't need to eat smaller amounts.

02:26:04.500 --> 02:26:05.940
Some people can't have that much.

02:26:05.940 --> 02:26:07.740
I will say after a hard leg workout,

02:26:07.740 --> 02:26:10.149
I don't have the same appetite
that I do after let's say,

02:26:10.149 --> 02:26:12.030
an upper body workout.

02:26:12.030 --> 02:26:17.030
It can really disrupt my
whole feeling of wellbeing.

02:26:17.400 --> 02:26:19.813
- You want to eat less
after you train your legs?

02:26:19.813 --> 02:26:22.380
- [Jeff] I do, yeah.
- Oh, wow, I'm the opposite.

02:26:22.380 --> 02:26:24.434
- No, 'cause I could
feel sick to my stomach.

02:26:24.434 --> 02:26:26.280
- You're clearly training harder.

02:26:26.280 --> 02:26:28.440
I've seen the way you train,
you do train very intensely.

02:26:28.440 --> 02:26:29.602
- Yeah.

02:26:29.602 --> 02:26:30.480
I think it's important.

02:26:30.480 --> 02:26:31.740
I mean, I think that...

02:26:31.740 --> 02:26:33.810
Again, it's that trade off between,

02:26:33.810 --> 02:26:36.686
if you're not going to train
for a long period of time

02:26:36.686 --> 02:26:38.940
then you're going to want to train harder.

02:26:38.940 --> 02:26:41.910
And again, I actually
feel like contrary to

02:26:41.910 --> 02:26:44.220
what people might think as you age,

02:26:44.220 --> 02:26:45.990
you're better off training harder

02:26:45.990 --> 02:26:47.636
for a shorter period of time.

02:26:47.636 --> 02:26:52.110
It's always within the
realm of safe training.

02:26:52.110 --> 02:26:53.917
I mean, I think that's
what I like to think

02:26:53.917 --> 02:26:54.840
that's what I bring to the table.

02:26:54.840 --> 02:26:58.170
Like an approach that's
smarter so I can train harder.

02:26:58.170 --> 02:27:00.893
Like not doing the dumb
things I did when I was a kid.

02:27:00.893 --> 02:27:05.893
And with that trade off
being a harder trainer,

02:27:05.988 --> 02:27:08.790
I think I get the results that I want

02:27:08.790 --> 02:27:11.520
because I'm able to really
push it and then back off

02:27:11.520 --> 02:27:16.520
and again, the meal feels like
almost a physiological reward

02:27:16.527 --> 02:27:18.330
for the hard effort I put in the gym

02:27:18.330 --> 02:27:22.230
knowing that I'm also replenishing
and setting the stage for

02:27:22.230 --> 02:27:25.260
the next day to be another
successful day of training

02:27:25.260 --> 02:27:27.660
or maybe not depending upon
how many times a day a week

02:27:27.660 --> 02:27:28.812
I train.

02:27:28.812 --> 02:27:31.170
I think that it's,

02:27:32.197 --> 02:27:33.766
It's a lot less,

02:27:33.766 --> 02:27:36.390
I hate to say but it's
a lot less scientific

02:27:36.390 --> 02:27:38.880
than we want to make it.

02:27:38.880 --> 02:27:41.820
And as it seems to be
coming back oftentimes

02:27:41.820 --> 02:27:43.170
like the thing that works for you

02:27:43.170 --> 02:27:44.610
is really the most important thing

02:27:44.610 --> 02:27:46.530
because ultimately
getting your ass in there

02:27:46.530 --> 02:27:47.370
and doing what you do

02:27:47.370 --> 02:27:50.310
is really the thing that
provides the best benefit.

02:27:50.310 --> 02:27:53.760
- Absolutely, and there are
many things that I would say

02:27:53.760 --> 02:27:55.320
are hallmarks of Jeff Cavaliere,

02:27:55.320 --> 02:27:58.200
but one of them is certainly consistency.

02:27:58.200 --> 02:28:00.600
You make it happen one way or another.

02:28:00.600 --> 02:28:04.350
- Huge. I mean, consistency
really is the determinant.

02:28:04.350 --> 02:28:06.847
And I know that that is
the hardest part for people

02:28:06.847 --> 02:28:10.320
that are, and why people tend
to look for the shortcut,

02:28:10.320 --> 02:28:13.530
'cause consistency is
the part that becomes

02:28:13.530 --> 02:28:14.400
the biggest challenge.

02:28:14.400 --> 02:28:18.510
But if you could find,
listen, if you could find the,

02:28:18.510 --> 02:28:20.640
you know through what I've
been trying to encourage here

02:28:20.640 --> 02:28:22.160
is like if you could find
the nutrition approach,

02:28:22.160 --> 02:28:23.700
if you could find the training approach,

02:28:23.700 --> 02:28:25.170
if you could try find the training split,

02:28:25.170 --> 02:28:27.720
if you could try all those
things that encourage you

02:28:27.720 --> 02:28:28.620
to want to go to the gym.

02:28:28.620 --> 02:28:30.270
Like you're locked in at
the point where you said

02:28:30.270 --> 02:28:33.390
you actually look forward to
going and doing your workout.

02:28:33.390 --> 02:28:34.243
- I love it.

02:28:34.243 --> 02:28:35.815
I look forward to, I mean, it's-

02:28:35.815 --> 02:28:37.436
Actually this morning,

02:28:37.436 --> 02:28:40.680
one of our teammates for the
podcast and I got to workout

02:28:40.680 --> 02:28:42.750
and halfway through I just
turned him and I said,

02:28:42.750 --> 02:28:44.670
I'll never figure out
why that feels so good,

02:28:44.670 --> 02:28:45.690
but it feels so good.

02:28:45.690 --> 02:28:47.700
I just, I really enjoy it and it lets,

02:28:47.700 --> 02:28:48.987
and I love to eat and it lets me eat right

02:28:48.987 --> 02:28:51.300
and I love the way it
makes me feel afterward.

02:28:51.300 --> 02:28:53.700
I don't understand this concept
of not enjoying the gym.

02:28:53.700 --> 02:28:54.990
Cardio's a little different,

02:28:54.990 --> 02:28:58.586
I always loathe the first
10 or 20 minutes of a jog.

02:28:58.586 --> 02:29:02.640
I mildly loathe the middle
third and by the end,

02:29:02.640 --> 02:29:04.110
I think this is the greatest thing ever,

02:29:04.110 --> 02:29:05.310
why don't want to do it all the time.

02:29:05.310 --> 02:29:06.510
And then that feeling evaporates

02:29:06.510 --> 02:29:07.680
before the next time I do it.

02:29:07.680 --> 02:29:09.270
- Yeah, of course I don't
even remember it either,

02:29:09.270 --> 02:29:10.920
after we get on, then do it again.

02:29:10.920 --> 02:29:11.753
- [Andrew] Exactly.

02:29:11.753 --> 02:29:14.580
- I think if people could,

02:29:14.580 --> 02:29:17.070
if we had one gift we
could give to everybody

02:29:17.070 --> 02:29:18.840
it would be the love of fitness.

02:29:18.840 --> 02:29:20.940
If they could be bestowed
the love of fitness

02:29:20.940 --> 02:29:22.860
it would change the entire world.

02:29:22.860 --> 02:29:27.060
But I think when you hear
things like this, that like,

02:29:27.060 --> 02:29:28.830
hey, that will work and that will work too

02:29:28.830 --> 02:29:30.300
and that this will work too.

02:29:30.300 --> 02:29:34.920
Rather than the dogmatic
one way only approach,

02:29:34.920 --> 02:29:37.590
which could become
discouraging for people.

02:29:37.590 --> 02:29:39.630
Then I think it becomes a
little bit uplifting like,

02:29:39.630 --> 02:29:40.740
well, I've never tried that.

02:29:40.740 --> 02:29:42.720
I've actually never
tried a total body split

02:29:42.720 --> 02:29:44.940
or I've never tried that style of eating.

02:29:44.940 --> 02:29:48.390
Like it becomes encouraging
that you might want to explore

02:29:48.390 --> 02:29:50.070
and then you might finally
get locked in and say,

02:29:50.070 --> 02:29:52.461
I really like this and then
you're off and running.

02:29:52.461 --> 02:29:55.791
- Some of what I so
enjoy about your content.

02:29:55.791 --> 02:30:00.561
We would be remiss if we
didn't briefly discuss Jesse.

02:30:00.561 --> 02:30:04.110
One of the great pleasures
for me in watching her content

02:30:04.110 --> 02:30:06.990
and learning from it over the
years is that you took on a,

02:30:06.990 --> 02:30:09.540
you decided to mentor somebody, Jesse.

02:30:09.540 --> 02:30:11.610
And there's some poking fun

02:30:11.610 --> 02:30:13.890
back and forth between the two
of you which is very amusing.

02:30:13.890 --> 02:30:16.860
But I have to say it
inspired me to do something

02:30:16.860 --> 02:30:18.480
early on in developing this podcast

02:30:18.480 --> 02:30:22.140
as I have a young intern who has helped me

02:30:22.140 --> 02:30:24.000
with some of the research
and he's a buddy,

02:30:24.000 --> 02:30:26.100
he's interested in science,
he's about to go off to college,

02:30:26.100 --> 02:30:26.933
but he also got really into fitness.

02:30:26.933 --> 02:30:28.750
So we would watch the videos of you guys.

02:30:28.750 --> 02:30:32.280
He was helping me get the
Instagram content out early on.

02:30:32.280 --> 02:30:33.270
And one thing that was just,

02:30:33.270 --> 02:30:36.000
it's such a pleasure to be
able to pass along knowledge

02:30:36.000 --> 02:30:38.190
and of course I'm learning from him.

02:30:38.190 --> 02:30:39.270
This is always the way it works.

02:30:39.270 --> 02:30:41.440
We learn from teaching and
we learn from students.

02:30:41.440 --> 02:30:44.940
But it's been great to see
Jesse's progress, it's amazing.

02:30:44.940 --> 02:30:48.000
I've gotten to meet him in person just now

02:30:48.000 --> 02:30:51.480
and he has grown, he's changed physically.

02:30:51.480 --> 02:30:53.670
And I think that you
mentioned a love of fitness.

02:30:53.670 --> 02:30:56.484
I think that one of the
best ways to be consistent

02:30:56.484 --> 02:30:59.400
is to take on the responsibility
of teaching others.

02:30:59.400 --> 02:31:01.290
Once one has proficiency in something.

02:31:01.290 --> 02:31:04.200
So maybe you just tell
us a little bit about

02:31:04.200 --> 02:31:05.033
how that's going.

02:31:05.033 --> 02:31:06.685
How is Jesse doing and

02:31:06.685 --> 02:31:10.530
where does he need a little
more work, where is he thriving?

02:31:10.530 --> 02:31:12.120
I'm impressed by the progress.

02:31:12.120 --> 02:31:12.990
- Well we have a, I mean,

02:31:12.990 --> 02:31:16.050
physically, we can
obviously see the changes,

02:31:16.050 --> 02:31:18.390
the list of things to
work on or is immense

02:31:18.390 --> 02:31:20.910
it's so long for him
to continue to improve.

02:31:20.910 --> 02:31:25.140
But no actually, in reality, Jesse,

02:31:25.140 --> 02:31:26.280
the story of Jesse was that

02:31:26.280 --> 02:31:28.980
I knew Jesse prior to
starting even Athlean-X.

02:31:28.980 --> 02:31:30.450
And a matter of fact,
I think the funny thing

02:31:30.450 --> 02:31:33.540
is the very first video that
was ever posted on my channel

02:31:33.540 --> 02:31:36.673
was a video that he shot as I don't know,

02:31:36.673 --> 02:31:38.310
a 13 year old or something.

02:31:38.310 --> 02:31:40.110
And I said, can you just
film this for a second.

02:31:40.110 --> 02:31:42.930
I was over there training
members of the family.

02:31:42.930 --> 02:31:47.285
So he then off went off to
college, went into film,

02:31:47.285 --> 02:31:50.850
realized he had much greener
pastures at Athlean-X

02:31:50.850 --> 02:31:53.370
instead of becoming the next
[indistinct] or something

02:31:53.370 --> 02:31:55.980
and he decided to come work with me.

02:31:55.980 --> 02:31:58.230
And the expectations in the beginning

02:31:58.230 --> 02:31:59.910
were just to edit videos

02:31:59.910 --> 02:32:04.200
or just to help with various aspects of

02:32:04.200 --> 02:32:06.870
like my day to day that
I don't think I was

02:32:06.870 --> 02:32:08.910
equipped to really handle and
grow the business anymore.

02:32:08.910 --> 02:32:13.910
So then look at by virtue of
being in that environment,

02:32:14.250 --> 02:32:15.083
there's an interest.

02:32:15.083 --> 02:32:17.070
I think if I worked in a gym,

02:32:17.070 --> 02:32:18.870
I might become interested in working out.

02:32:18.870 --> 02:32:20.670
And though that mine
was not a commercial gym

02:32:20.670 --> 02:32:24.810
it's sitting right
behind my office window,

02:32:24.810 --> 02:32:25.800
there became an interest

02:32:25.800 --> 02:32:27.510
in wanting to work out a little bit.

02:32:27.510 --> 02:32:31.143
And it wasn't even an
intentional experiment

02:32:31.143 --> 02:32:33.480
to put Jesse there.

02:32:33.480 --> 02:32:36.360
I just thought that he's
a very likable person.

02:32:36.360 --> 02:32:38.310
He has a very funny personality

02:32:38.310 --> 02:32:39.948
and he's also the, every man.

02:32:39.948 --> 02:32:44.190
In some ways as I'm sure
maybe you experience sometimes

02:32:44.190 --> 02:32:47.610
like I'm the guy that this
comes naturally for me

02:32:47.610 --> 02:32:48.443
is what people will say.

02:32:48.443 --> 02:32:50.194
Like, this is what you do for a living.

02:32:50.194 --> 02:32:51.027
Like this is what you-

02:32:51.027 --> 02:32:52.800
Like there's an element of disconnect

02:32:52.800 --> 02:32:54.870
in terms of the relatability

02:32:54.870 --> 02:32:56.850
because I do do this for a living.

02:32:56.850 --> 02:32:59.400
I can't deny that I do work
with professional athletes.

02:32:59.400 --> 02:33:01.170
So like there's a level
of interest in this

02:33:01.170 --> 02:33:02.310
above and beyond.

02:33:02.310 --> 02:33:04.620
But for him, he's just the
kid who wants to train.

02:33:04.620 --> 02:33:07.763
Maybe if he rolls out
of bed before 11:00 AM

02:33:07.763 --> 02:33:11.130
and doesn't have a date on Friday night,

02:33:11.130 --> 02:33:13.260
but that's the guy
everybody could relate to.

02:33:13.260 --> 02:33:15.578
And watching him transform

02:33:15.578 --> 02:33:19.397
and I love the fact that
even the interest level

02:33:19.397 --> 02:33:20.880
was up and down.

02:33:20.880 --> 02:33:24.240
Like it wasn't consistent
for him because he was like,

02:33:24.240 --> 02:33:25.262
part interested

02:33:25.262 --> 02:33:26.400
and then maybe not
interested for three months

02:33:26.400 --> 02:33:27.233
and then interested or not.

02:33:27.233 --> 02:33:28.800
And I never pushed it on him.

02:33:28.800 --> 02:33:31.500
This is again, this was no
orchestrated experiment for me.

02:33:31.500 --> 02:33:33.810
It was just like, if you want
to do this, then do this.

02:33:33.810 --> 02:33:38.220
And also from a standpoint
of like lending my help

02:33:38.220 --> 02:33:40.770
or expertise to him,
